      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. J</head>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. J</p>
        <p>WHOSE HAND?</p>
        <p>Dv ANNIE 0. TIDBIT. 3.</p>
        <p>Author of "The Love that Won," "Hunted Down," "Hidden Hands," "TLe 'Finger of Fate," "Robes of Shame," ez,)</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: As a vessel is going down Olga Murray tells Dr. Dacre that, six years since, she murdered Stephen Hendexson, of Greatbridge Hall, for which crime suspicion fell on Richard Sterry. if possible, the statement which she signs is to be kept from her loVer, Sir Geoffrey Verriker. A bottle containing the document is flung into the sea. Sir Geoffrey, who rescues Olga from the wreck, insists on taking her to his mother at Xemscote Hall, of which he has just come into possession by the death ef his uncle. Mrs. Dunston was to have married the uncle, and she plots with her daughter Julia to make the latter Lady Verriker by a marriage with Geoffrey or Geoffrey's coUsin Edward. Dick Sterry owns the neighbouring estate to the Verrikers, and is in love with Geoffrey's sister. As Geoffrey and Olga are nearing home Dick gets into the railway carriage, lie lives near Greatbridge. _</p>
        <p>Ile does not recogntse Olga, but when Ethel Nrerriker - introduces him next day Olga .faints.</p>
        <p>The Verrikers believe Olga's nerves were upset by the experiencos of the wreck.. Sterry cices not seem to know her. 1k explains to 'Ethel 'Verriker, in order afterwards to de- clare his love, the fact that he was tried for the Greatbridge murder. Hendemon, whose wild character he had learnt, .was to hate mar- ried Sterry's sister. . The latter threatened him, and placed his own revolver on the table. He left it there. He repaired again to the Hall, at his sister's request, and only saw her again through prison bars. Entering the billiard-rcom by the French win- dow—from which he saw a figure emerge as no approached—he found Henderson, who had been playing with a .j.3key, shot. lie would have been convicted, but that the fatal wound was found to have been a knife wound. The shock of the trial killed is sister. Ethel pro- mises to help him to prove his innocence. Geoffrey takes Olga to put up the banns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>CHAPTER XI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XI.</p>
        <p>"It's no use—it's no u'.3.2,". cried Julia Dunston. "I might try for ever and he would never look at me. He is blind to every woman except one. Did you see him driving her yesterday? Did you see his eyes when he looked at her ?" She got up and paced about, and Mrs. Dunston watched her almost nervously. _</p>
        <p>"Did you see him look at her ? " Julia re- peated. "If you had, you would know that it was no use. He will never look at any other woman ; and even if there is some mystery about her, as you think-,--evon if there is something she is afraid (;f---you will do no good. by raking it up. He will only hate us for it. He will hate anycne who takes Olga Murray - from him, whatever she may be— whatever she may have done. He -loves her. Oh! even I can see that, and it is no use try- ing to alter it." She dropped into a seat. Mrs. Dunston sat up. Her thin far',e, the faded replica of her (laughter's, was paler than usual. There was a queer, hard light in. her eyes. " Well, suppose you are right ? " she said, suddenly. ' Julia lifted her head.,</p>
        <p>"Suppose you are right," Mn. Dunstan re- peated, there is still E:lward." She remained looking at her daughter in a queer, cold 'way, and suddenly Julia shivered. "Edward !" she said.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Dunston got up and crossed the rcom. She looked strangely beautiful, strangely frafTile almost unreal, as she stooped over Julia's head. There was an ethereal look about her, and yet her words made Julia start upright. They s,ounded harsh and loud, even though they were whispered close to her ear. "There is Edward, and he would do mur- der for. you,!' she whispered. Julia -stared with wild eyes at her mother's face.</p>
        <p>"We shall see at dinner to-morrow night how things • are there," Mrs. Dunston added. "Edward will be there too, and then—we shall see. But. Julia, are you going to let the estate and the money and the title slp through your hands? Are you going to stain by and see it go?-" What can I do?" Julia hoarsely</p>
        <p>whispe-ecl,</p>
        <p>Mrs.' looked; round, and then, bond- ing low, whispered again into her daughter's ear. "We</p>
        <p>Julia started up trembling. "But I can't—l can't," she cried not."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Dunston shrugged hr shoulders. Her face looked .cruel in the afternoen light. Her lips were drawn into A straight line that re- minded one of a cat, and her eyes wee bright and contracted, like a beast of prey watching its victim.</p>
        <p>"We? We?" she repeated. , "Why should we have anything to do with it? We need not run any risk. We could leave that," —she laughed sharply—" to Edward." "To Edward ? " Julia _repeated, dully._</p>
        <p>"Why not ? " cried Mi. Dunston ;- ".you know he is mad over you. I .tell you he would flo any crazy thing for you, and why should he not do this? Why should he . not strike Geoffrey out of his path, and leave the way clear—for him and yen?"</p>
        <p>She broke off. didiat sat, still. Everything seemed very quiet, and mechanically she be- gan counting the number of rues in the pat- tern of the carpet under her feet. Edward? Geoffrey? The two names echoe('' together dully in her brain. Edward. and Geoffrey? Azni' Geoffrey had refused her love ---had ignored her and worshipped openly a little pale-faced nobody, who had not half the good looks that she had. And Edward loved her! And Geoffrey stood in his shoes! .</p>
        <p>She got up an crosled the rcom to the window. Mrs. Dunston followed her .and stcod Close behind her. Julia's face grew as hard and as cruel as her mother's as she stared out at the little quiet sunny street. Why should she hesitate? Why should she stop at anything now? Geoffrey was blind to her, an the mad and desperate love for him that had been in her heart was changing swiftly into a mac? and de3perate hatred. -</p>
        <p>Why should she hesitate? Her dark brows grew into a straight line above her dark brown eyes. The ugly gleam of yellow came into them as she stared out across the street that lay bathed in sunshine. She hated Geoffrey. She would like to see him ousted —she would like to see him dead.</p>
        <p>Dead! }Ter mother's low voice Tang in her ears—" If Geoffrey were dead, it would be so easy," she was saying, "and old Sir Ntrriker never ir.‘,”kr.t him to inherit. Ile Always nSêaii to alter his will. Ale could not keep him out of the title, but he could have taken all the money from him, and he would have done if he had not died,_ Oh ! it was abominable luck to lose all that estate and that money. And to think that with care we might get it yet—Julia,it drives me frantic to think of it. Why should we not.get it? Why should Edward not get it?" Julia's face grew harder still. . Suddenly. she turned. "Leave it to me, mother," she said. " Edward will be here directly. Leave it to me.". .</p>
        <p>Mrs. Dunston gave 9ne quick, swift lock at her face, and then, tur_iing, • went from the room.</p>
        <p>Julia. stood long after she had gone, " swing- ing the blind cord, as she always did; in her long, white, restless fingers, staring out at the blinding sunshine, staring and seeing hothing. She did not even see Edware' Verriker crcss the street and wave his hand to her. She did not even see him run up the steps, and stand watching her as he waited, but the peal of the bell through the house roused her with a start. She turned, and going to the mirror over the mantelpiece smoothed her hair with quick, shaking fingers, and straightened the lace on her blouse.</p>
        <p>She had time to see that she had never ,looked more beautiful; she had. ,time to see that the faint pink colour was 'back in her cheeks, and to draw her lips into a smile be- fore he came into the room.</p>
        <p>He was a sinall man, with sharp features and piercing black eyes. His straight hair grew low on his forclwad. and his thick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>stubby black moustache gave 'him a fierce,</p>
        <p>half-foreign look. But the- re was something alse about him which gave him an -unusual look. A physiognomist would have said' it was the strange line which ran straight down his face from nose to mouth. Julia searched his face as he crcssed the room towards her. She saw his eager look change swiftly to unbounded delight when hE saw her, and then he had her in his arms,</p>
        <p>his dark, ugly face close to her beautiful one, and his lips, with their deep, rugged limn, pressed to hers. She lifted herself from him a little sharply and looked at him. A sudden: excitement seized . her. She hated him. There was some- thing in his dark fa..2e and .black eyv s that ;A times filled her with a shuddering horror. And yet—if he was master of Kemscote Hall? If he stocd' in Geoffrey's shoes? if • the titie. bad been his—if there was ,any chance of thE</p>
        <p>title becoming his—what theuf She drew her breath quickly. It as a stake worth playing for. And Edward lovel her blindly, madly—would' do anything, risk anything for her! lie was looking at her beautiful face, and never had it looked more lovely. _</p>
        <p>"Don't," she said ; we—we shall have to give up that kind of thing now." ' "To give up? What do you mean?" no "You</p>
        <p>cried. - Her ev2s lookod steadily into his</p>
        <p>must knew what I mean well enough," she "Now that Geoffrey has come bach, what hope is there for us? What is the use of pretending? We must go back to work— you and I, and forpt each other. We haven't 9nough to marry on. Your creditors will be down upon you now, and swallow up all they cart;, get, and you may be sure they won't leave anything for me, and I—l.have got to go and fight for my living in London 'again. I must go and forget, Edward." Her voice faltered a little, and the -sound of it sent the colour -rushing to his face. "You—you—to go back? " he stammered. "Julia, 'I can't let you go. I can't live with- out you—Julia, for heaven'3 sake don't talk like that. We must manage somehow. My income is small, and I am head over ears in debt, but if you loved me, Julia—" _ _</p>
        <p>She got up, and flung cut her arms. "Love? It is because I love you that I say that there must be an end to everything between us. We cannot marry. I could not lymr to marry like that. We should be unhappy—you might even hate me • in time—oh, yes, you would hate me If I married you now." _ _ _</p>
        <p>• "Hate you'?" He got up and stood beside her. "You don't know how I love you. Oh! Julia, Julia, for heaven's sake • don't talk of</p>
        <p>leaving me !" Julia lcoked steadily into his eyes. "Do you really love me so much?" 5h2 asked. in a "Do you- really care as much as you say? "</p>
        <p>low voice.</p>
        <p>He caught her to him in a frenzy of pas- sion. ' His dark face was cicse to hers again. "Care, Julia? Care? I care so much that I would co anything to make you mine. I care so much that I would rather you killed ma than gave me up. Care? Care? Don't you hplieve I care. Julia? "</p>
        <p>He stared out from under his thick, black brows at the shabby room. A straight, sharp line had appeared between his eyes. The lines about his mouth stood out deep and .ugly. " I wish he would die ! " he broke out pas- sionately. "If only something would' happen —some accident—l wish to heaven that some accident would hanpon to him ! " He broke off. Julia's fingers twisted - softly in his. For a moment the room seemed very still. Then Julia's voice, low and sat, sounded close to his ear. . " Accidents don't happen," she said. . bit- terly. "at least, they don't happen for .our benefit. If they had. Geoffrey wou,ld have died at sea instead of corning back like this, and bringing that girl. We—we shall have to make our own accid'ents if they are to end ns we want them to." • He sat quite still. The lines deereriell about his mouth. His fingers locsened-e:cn hers, and she bent a little closer to him. "If T had arranged that accident at sea." ihe whispered, "it would have ended very differently: Geoffrey would not have ccme back." He sFill did not move. She tried to bed to see his face. but she could (lily see the • pro- file. and something about that sot her, heart besting in a strange. hard, irregular way. . " I wish to gocde?ss I could arrange sor-e accident for him," she added_ " I wouldn't spare him. I hate him too much. I should be glad to see him dead, and you—in his shees." Her voice sank. He turned slowly and fared her. "We are talking wildly," he began. " is here. at Kemseote. the master of the Hall, and the money, and we have got to make the best of ft." " Why have we? " She put her arm rcund his neck. He felt her breath .on his face. and the touch of her threw him out of his balance. as it always did. There was nothing he would not do when her arms were around him. "Julia," he said, hoarsely. " I believe I'd Lill aim. if I could. I believe—l—" He broke off again, as if a sudden horror had forced itself 1.11)nll him. He tried to dis- entangle her hands from about his neck, but she held him, and, lifting her fingers, twined them softly in his hair. "If I had -a chance," she whispered, "I— I—would kill him too. Oh ! Edward, think what it means. Money, title, the estate—all there, with only one life between us and it, and on • the tither side, poverty 'and misery and despair. Edward, how can 'we bear it? Can't we do a nyl vg 1" lie sat still for another moment. Then, 3uddenly throwing her from him, got up an crossed the room to the fireplace. He stood looking down, and Julia fell back, stunned, angry, . cold. She had failed. He was not so desperate over her as she had thought. .THe was not quite so ready as her mother had said. She had failed—failed. There was no hope of getting Geoffrey out of the way Her first impulse was to get..up and put an egd• to everything at once and for ever. The next instant she told herself .it had been the im- pulse of a fool, for Edward had turned to her again, and something strange and terrible</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>r—r- tie suddenly caugh</head>
        <p>r—r- tie suddenly caugh</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>• •</p>
        <p>She smiled a little. No man had ever been so mad over her as this little dark ugly' man, who had so narrowly escapeci. succeeding to the Kemscote estate. No man before had wor- shipped her so wildly, so madly, had beera so blind and so impatient. She looked at him calmly.</p>
        <p>. "If you really cared you wouljn't want to tie me down to poverty," she said, slowly. "If you really cared you would let me go." Let her go! He locsened her suddenly. His fade had grown drawn and grey. The sunshine, --brighl and golden, shewed it , ugly, coarse a strange, powerful, and sinister face. The feeling of cold swept through her again as she watched him. He was her tool.. He would be as wax in her hands, as she knew; and yet, for a moment, she wisheo-- she almost wished she had the strength to send him away, and face the poverty and the struggle and hard work that would await her in Lon6'on, instead oh going on with this other thing—instead of whispering the • words to him that might lead to a frightful end.</p>
        <p>But the fe-eling was only -momentary. A glance round the shabby room, a thought of the miserable life in London. of hopcloss efforts to get work, of weary tramps in wet and slush, made her shudder even in the hot sunshine. She could ii6t go back to that. After being so close to wealth, and having it yet within her reach, she would be a fccl to go back to it. She at down again upon the "couch in the window. .</p>
        <p>"Do you mean—really mean that poverty would be so hard; for you?" he was asking. She nodded her head slowly. Then she held up her hands to him—little white hane3 that were irresistible to him. . "Oh, Edward, you don't know how hard," she cried; " I couldn't—l couldn't live here, crampled ay?.d stifled by a paltry income. Oh, no, I must go back to London. There, ,at least, is the hope that some o'ay I may SUC- ceed, and grow rich, but 'here—what could happen to me here? Edward, I cculd not live. I could not!"</p>
        <p>He paced up and down. The deep lines running straight down the sides of his cheeks seemed dieper and straighter than usual, and gave him 'a queer look. His skin haa takn on a yellow tinge, ant? under his straggling, black hair his forehead glistened. Ile put up his hand and wiped the moisture away. "Julia, I can't lose . you," he cried, hoarsely. "What can I do? How can I kkeep you? Tell me what I am to do ! "</p>
        <p>.-- - He came to a standstill before her, and she put up her hands again and dragged him down.</p>
        <p>.• • "I am no good," he said. "I have idled away my time—spent my money, and. now. I have none left. What am Ito do?" /. She nestled a little closer to him. and put .her hand in his. "It is hard—it is cruel,' she said. "If only Geoffrey had . not come back—oh, if he had only died at sea how different it would have, been!" _ _</p>
        <p>_ He did not move, only his fingers tightened over hers.</p>
        <p>"If he had died how happy we should have hApn." Rho. aricli.d.</p>
