      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>her hands in his.</head>
        <p>her hands in his.</p>
        <p>and stern ad sinister in his face held ".er spellbound. He came slowly towards her. "Uncle Henry never intended him to inherit," he said, abruptly. "He meant to disinWeric him—he meant me to have the money before he met your mother. Then—even then, he would have provided for me. Now—Geoffrey is there, a usurper, an outsider. He has no right. He has everything, and we—you and I—have nothing—nothing ! " He flung out his hands, coming closer to her, with a strange, glassy look in his dark eyes. He suddenly caught her hands in his. "Julia, if an accident happened to him, would you marry me? If anything happened —would you marry me at once? 7</p>
        <p>She lifted her face with her red-gold hair and her ' yellowish-brown eyes to him. lle drew his breath unsteadily. He loved her better than his life—better than anything the world could offer. "I would marry you—l think I would marry you bcfcre it happened," she v.-hisperea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>CHAPTER XII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XII.</p>
        <p>"I have asked them to come early," Mrs. Verriker was saying. "After all, Edward is Geoffrey's cousin, and I should like to see them friends. And I should like also to be friends with his fiancée and her mother. Our presence here must be a bit painful for her, poor thing." Ethers lips curled a little. "I can't under- stand her coming at all," she said, vigorously. " I know I- wouldn't. I shouldn't have the heart. Would you, Olga?" •</p>
        <p>Olga 16oked up. They were out in the gar- den on low seats, under the shady yew hedges. Her head was bent over a bit of neellework.-. _ . .</p>
        <p>" Won-1T you?" Ethel repeated. "Would you come here and see a,ll this—all this lovely garden, and that dear delightful house, which had missed being yours by a hair's breadth? Oh, I know I wouldn't. It would break my heart to lopk at it, and how Mrs. Dunston can come so callously, I don't know She couldn't have been very fond of Uncle Henry, although she was to have marrica him. Just fancy, everything was ready. The lawyers were drawing out a new will, and thc banns . were up, when uncle died, and upset 3verything." "His death must have been a great shock or Mrs. Dunston, poor thing," Mrs. Verriker mid, "she must have . cared." "I don't believe it, mother," cried Ethel. "I don't believe she cared a hang—" •</p>
        <p>" Etlrel! " "I'm sorry, mother. I didn't mean to be slangv, but I feel that I could swear i.hen see Mrs. Dunston. I don't knoiv why." "I should think not indeed. But you are always absurd in your likes and dislikes. I think it is to, a person's disadvantage for you to like them at first. You always change, and so, perhaps, in time, you ma.y like Mrs. Dunston."</p>
        <p>_ " Perhi.ps ! " said Ethel, soornfully •, but her eves fell with a little cutiositv on Ol.za's</p>
        <p>face, and she remained watching her as she bent over her needlework. She had begun, if not by actually disliking Olga, yet by vigor- ously distrusting her, and yet now against her will, against her wish,. she was growing to like her. Even in those few days Olga had disarmed her. She was so openly and so frankly in love with her brother, and that was one of the keys to Ethel's heart. Anyone who loved Geoffrey, or was 10.0 by him, couldn't be bad, was her belief, and there wa no doubt about Olga Or about Geoffrey. AnJ surely he ought to know her, for he had spent Weeks with her face to face under most diffi- cult surroundings; and if there had been any- thing uncomfortable behind Olga, surely in those five monotonous weeks in mid-ocean it would' have come. out? It was one of the erguments in Olga's favour that even after those long weeks, even after their experi- ences, Geoffrey was absolutely and . wholly in love with this pale-faced, dark-eyed girl, who came from no one knew ,where, and who wao Ile one knew what. Ethel watched; her curiously. he (lid t. ot understand her. She said very little about herself, and somehow the few questicrs sh- and her mother • had asked had not been very satisfactorily answered.</p>
        <p>But • Mrs. Verriker had 'shaken her head. "It is not fair to; worry her now,'' she had said. "she will tell' us all about herself in good time, and I daresay lics.r life has been very painful, poor thing." Ethel said nothing ; but she felt that their own lives had been painful, too. yet they were never afraici.. and never shirked speaking of them. Still, in spite of a certain vague doubt and distrust in her mind', she liked Olga, and was growing to love her. "She has always got somebody on hand to dislike or distrust," Mrs. Verrilier said now to Olga-, "it is a wonder she hasn't found something to dislike about you; but you are fortunate. It is only poor Mrs. Dunston who is an -object of suspicion just now." _</p>
        <p>Ethel flushed a little. "If it wasn't for me, Olga," she cried, "mother would be taken in over an over again. She believes in everybody. But as fJr Mrs. Dunston—you have seen for yout self, Olga, that she pre- tends to like us, and all that, and I say it isn't natural. Why, she must hate us. I am sure I should if I had been so close to all this as she was. And yet she visits us quite calmly as if nothing had happened. Even if she didn't care about -Uncle Henry, it isn't natural ..or her to come here as she dces. I believe she is up to mischief." Mrs. Verriker lay back in her chair and laughed, but Olga locked up a 'little quickly. "Oh. Ethel dear, you are too absurd,s'' Mrs: Verriker said. "What mischief could she possibly be up to?" . Ethel- locked vaguely round the charming old garden,. acrcss the great flower - beds and past the terrace wall, where, a peacock siruttqd, spreading his tail in ,the sunshine. "I don't .know," she said. "but I think if I were her I should be inclined' to cut Geof- frey's throat."</p>
        <p>" Ethel ." • "Well," Ethel -looked' up defiantly, " think whAt she has • missed', .and what Julia has missed, all because he was obstinate enougi . to live instead' of drownin,,, as she no doubt wished , him .to do. Just think—why, I kilow. if I were her,. I should hate him; and I don't believe a woman with eyes like Mrs. Dunston's 'would hesitate_ if she had an oppor- tunity of putting him out of the way. She reminds me of a picture of Mary Queen of Scots. I'm sure she would lore to order peopre's heae.'s off." Mrs. Verriker laughed again, but Olga had grown slightly serious. The very mention of harm coming to _Geoffrey was enough to turn her pale; and yet how absurd- this was— as if Mrs. Dufiston could harm him, even if she wished. It was too ridiculous.</p>
        <p>Ethel was nursing her knee and turning her face up to the sky. " Now, if I were Mrs. Dunston," she saio with her , eyes half closed, " I should g. Geoffrey into a corner by himself and dose him with rat poison, or else I should push him down a well, or else—" .Geoffrey's own voice stopped her. "Where are you hiding ? " he cried, com- ing towards them. "Mrs. and Miss Dunscon are here—came early, as you asked them to, arte; Mrs. Dunston says may they both come out and sit in the garden ?." Mrs. -Verriker reso, to her feet, and went forward, holding out her hand. "Of course," she said. "I'm so glad ' w see</p>
        <p>you." Mrs. Dunston, and Julia behind her, radi- ant. and beautiful, came towards her. -</p>
        <p>"We are 'already dressed for dinner," Mrs. Dunstan said, " and feel fearfully, garish; but we coul6'n't resist coming early; as you asked us. I was longing for a stroll through these gardens in the sunshine, ,and so I hope 7011 won't hesticate 'About going to dress when you want to. I simply couldn't resist coming. And it was so kind', of you to ask us." Mrs. Verriker declared she was pleasell; and as Olga rose also to shake hands with them, Ethel gave her a solemn wink.</p>
        <p>" Part or the plan," she .whispered, wickedly, but somehow a queer chill swept over Olga as she looked into Mrs. Dunston•s face. , There wcs something odd in the dark brown- eyes—something alts-t, something startling, something suggestive. Ethel's re- marks were only absurdities to be dismissed lightly, and yet Somehow her words clung stupidly and echoed and re-echoed in Olga's brain.</p>
        <p>And they were true, too. Mrs. Dunsfon and Julia had every reason to hate Geoffrey and Mrs. Verriker ; and yet, to leek at them now; one would have thought they had no re- collection that a few months- ago they had hoped to be there in possession themselves. _ _</p>
        <p>They stood for a minute or two, and tile] Mrs. Dunston suggested' walking across tha lawn to the water garden. "The fountains are so pretty, may we see them ? " she asked'.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Verriker turned at once, anf:. she and Mrs. Dunston walked in the dirntion of the ponds. Julia are Geoffrey a look, but he had forgotten her.' He had dropped into a chair by Olga's side, and had taken up her needlework in his hands.</p>
        <p>Olga was not locking. She had lifted her head and was watching Julia, nnc the Icok that for one brief moment flashed into the dark bright eyes was enough. Olga caught her breath, and fell back. Hatred? There was hatred enough there and to spare—vin- 61ctive hatred that ha&amp; once been love, as she looked at Geoffrey's unconscious face.. "Would you like to come too ? " Ethel was saying; but Julia shook her head.</p>
        <p>" Might we sit down instead for a moment " she asked... "-It is so hot. Even a low neck does not keep one cool." She droppecT. into a seat, and threw back her cloak as she spoke, and Olga watched her with 'IL queer .feeling, half fear, half dislike, that had in it something of jealousy tco. She was so very beautiful. And the strong sun- light pouring upon her bare neck lir/I nothing to reveal—only pure white akin, aid a firm, rounded neck; and there surely W:l3 not another woman in England Who could brave the pitiless sunlight as Julia was doing nowt Olga sighed a little, and looked ()own at her lap. Geoffrey had Oropped her needle- work again. He was speaking tq Julia, and she was sitting up with a new look, a new interest in her face.</p>
        <p>"Rest fora minute." he, was saying. "and then go and get cool by the water. We'll set the fountains going. But perhaps you would catch cold ? " _</p>
        <p>"I? No, nothing ever hurts me," she de- clared ; " but wilt you set the fountains going now? Do, I have only seen them playing once."</p>
        <p>,Geoffrey rose, and Julia got up tOO. He looked down at Olga. "Coming. girlie " he asked. She lifted her heao, but at. that monic.%.r.t a gong rang from the house. "Ugh! Horrors! the . ‘dressing - gong," cried Ethel. " Yon will forgive us going, won't you, Miss Dunston? wc.n't be long. Geoffrey, you must stay and entertain them for twenty minutes while we dress. I'll re- lie' e you then. 'You can 'dress quicker than we can ; ano'' I promise faithfully that I'll give you ten whole minutes." " Oh, thanks," cried Geoffrey. "You al- ways were kind to your only brother. The!! if you don't mind, Miss Dunston, I'll stay and see that you don't pith the flowers until they come and take my place?' Olga folded • un.,- her . work. and went slowly</p>
        <p>across the lawn. Ethel, putting her hang on her shoulder, jumped' -6 small flower - bed at the bottom of the terrace. At the steps she paused to look back. Julia had dropped to her seat again, but Geoffrey was standing looking at Olga. Ethel wavec? her _lhand gaily.</p>
        <p>Now, do —you see how my words are ing true ? " she asked, lightly. " Don't you think Julia Dunston has wicked eyes? And do you think a girl like that would be. plea- sant to us—rtnless she was going to get , some- thing out of us? She must hate us—oh, of course they must hate us ; and yet apparently bow they love us ! Its my belief that they are hatching some foul plot. I shall look for poison in the coffee to-night. Edward Verriker is coming tco, ano just think Low nice it would be for him and Julia if some- body was kind enough to poison Geoffrey for him ! What would you Jo if yo were in their shoes, Olga ? " She looked round' laughing, and then sud- denly stood still. Olga was stranply white. She had stopped at the bottom of the terrace steps, and was clinging to the stonework of the balustrade, Ethel sprang back down the steps to her. ' " Oh ! " she cried', "you are not taking my nonsense seriously. are yon. You can't be- lieve- that I would joke if I really thought such things possible. Oh, Olga, I am so sorry."</p>
        <p>Olga turnec? slowly her white face. "I can't bear-7-even .the thought cf any harm coming to Geoffrey," she. said, with difficulty. "It makes me cold—rven in joke. It makes me think—of that dreadful day—" "Oh, Olga dear, I am•vexed with myself," Ethel cried', kissing her. "I'll never joke again about such thirgs. I wouldn't .have done it for anything. It was a siily thins to do." She looked back for a moment over Olga's shoulder at the figures en :the lawn. "It was .stupid of me, dear," she added. An yet it Lad not been aWO at first, and Olga knew it. There. was reason in the suggestion that Mrs. Dunston and her daughter would naturally hate them, and Olga had seen hatred and. something more in Julia's deep dark eyes. She went up •to dress, shivering in spite of herself. Even if Eth.el's joke were true—even if they did hate Geoffrey, it was not likely that any harm 'could Come to him— oh. very far from likely. _ _</p>
        <p>Ethel put her arm round Olga's waisf; as they went upstairs together. "1 want to be great friends with you," eho said. "You heard what mother said—that I oistrust and dislike everybody at first,- and I'll tell you now that I was suspicious even of you at first—even though • Geoffrey loves you so, and I know he couljn't love anybody but the best. But now I want you to feel—to he, in fact, •my sister. Oh. Olga, it will be so jolly to have one. And there's something I want to tell you—something that I haven't told a soul, not even mother yet; but I want to tell you, beet-Rise—well, sisters always tell each other their secrets, don't they? And you are to be my sister. So I'm going to tell you now. And it's a dead secret, mind." They had reached the door of Olga'S room. "Let me in," cried Ethel, "and I'll tell yon." Olga turned, and there Ethel put her hands on her shoulders.</p>
        <p>"Olga; it's about Mr. Sterry," she said', breathlesly. "He—he told me the other nighi, that he loved me."</p>
        <p>Her eycs</p>
        <p>olp lifted her head sharply. brightened. "Just think of that !" Ethel cried. " But he told •me something else just before he told me that. And I don't know whether I ought to tell you, but I must tell someone, and he didn't bind me not to; and I know you will help me if you can. Olga, he has had a ter- rible life—he has gone threugh dreadful trouble, and not so long ago, either—only six years ago." Olga's heart gave a jump, an then steed still. Her lips were white. Ethel had turned away her face.</p>
        <p>"It was most dreadful trouble," she said, slowly. "I can't tell you all about it now, dear, because there is not time, but I will soon. He—he was accused of murder—of a dreadful murder at a place only a few milcs from here. They accused him of killing the man his sister, was going to marry. He told me all about it .the other night. It broke his heart—it nearly ruined his life; but he was too upset to care what happened to him after his sister died, and when they acquitted him he let everything. go. But now—now, Olga, he loves, me, arm he is going to rake all the old case up and clear himself. He is going to go into things—irito everything—and then, then when he has-found out who it really was who murdered Stephen Henderson he is going to marry me." Olga stood quite still, rigid as stns, aa cold. _ _</p>
        <p>" And then," she echoed, dully, " then be will marry you." She put up her hand to her bead anc.: looked round wiledy. The dead were rising indeed. (To be centinued..)</p>
      </div>
