      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WORKS BOWLING CONTEST.</head>
        <p>WORKS BOWLING CONTEST.</p>
        <p>VICTORY FOR RYLANDS' TEAM.</p>
        <p>-Nothing has been more remarkable in the world of liport of late years than the immense popularity which the good old English game of bowls has achieved throughout the land. In that gratifying revival War- rington !as played a worthy part and if there is one organisation to which may be ascribed much of the credit for the increased interest in the pastime amongst the 'working classes, it is the Warrington Works Bowlint Association. Founded in 1903, it .soon ingratiated itself with the employes of many of the local works, and to-day the association is full of health and vigour and of still greater promise for the future. Saturday saw the .final of the 1909 competition which, thanks to the system of handicapping adopted this 'season, has-been entered into with perhaps greater enthusiasm than any of its predecessors. It was a :Illeeting of the giants' for Whitecross and Rylands's AFarns have both gained distinction in past competi- bms. Whitecross had won the coveted Finlayson 'clip two years in succession and had they triumphed 11 Saturday the trophy would have been their property. hUtthe fates decreed otherwise ! Rylarids's had the f of winning the cup in 1906 and they were peful of victory on this occasion, notwithstanding 'Pe fact that their redoubtable opponents were general Aavourites.</p>
        <p>-II °lle of those brilliant summer days which ave been so late in coming this year favoured Satur- ilay's match and there was a record attendance on the 13°wling. Green, Bank Quay. One would hardly "Peet to find such a fine piece of greensward ii such "..situation, but a splendid green it is and its condi- -.tpll. on Saturday was a credit to the proprietor (Mr. "" • Campbell). The only drawback from the players. 'Pc'int of view was that the short spell of fine,sunshiny' weather had made the green very " fiery " and conse- tillient'y the woods were all the more difficult to con- r°l• The gathering of spectators wag the most•repre- -Bentative ever seen at a works competition. •It talleluded the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. ershaw), Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., and Mrs. Cros- ILeld (who had kindly consented to hand the cup and iiedals to the teams), Sir Gilbert Greenall. Bart., and * Greenall, Mr. and Mrs. Robert Pierpoint, Mr. . And ltrs. James Dodds, Alderman James Evans, ',`,-..°llncillors Locker, Steel and Ashcroft, Messrs. T. .1-I.ooker, Percy Carter, G. F. Freeman,and W. Dennett. .I)Pelogles for non-attendance had been received from lir. Markel, Alderman Burton, Messrs. C. E. Parker, Roberts,W. H. Owen,J. A. Ransome,J. J. Jocelyne, ' Harris, and W. Webster. a The games were followed very closely and loud Ll2Nause heralded any particularly notable feat. The .."Irlers themselves had difficulty in restraining their 'citement: They lavished the most coaxing gestures •11 the oft-times wayward woods and appealed alt tro 1, . yto them to " stop" or "come on. But ough the rivalry was keen, it was friendly and the ars were always prepared to accept defeat with e°°(.l grace. Early on the possibility of the hef luent predictions of the prophets being that ,became evident, and it was realized th 'hitecross would have to play for all e. 17 were worth to add to their laurels. Rylands's rlt business, and although Whitecross fought :1771.37 they were compelled to bow the knee to their t 64.8, who won by 16 points. Everyone agreed that p:Ysdeseryed their triumph, and when their captain, t 0 lavorY, came forward to receive the treasured c(e.PuY he was greeted with enthusiasm. And now to el'ibe the games.</p>
