        <head>MRS. CROSFIELD PRESENTING THE MEDALS.</head>
        <p>MRS. CROSFIELD PRESENTING THE MEDALS.</p>
        <p>limit cf his achievements and Haslam was acclaimed victor of a well-fought game by 15-10.</p>
        <p>There was anotheibatile roY'al between J. Slivin and R. Savory, the captain 1131ands's. TLe cpening stages were in Savory's favour but, the bowlers were on equal terms r:t.l and again at 6. Sa‘ory ran to 9 but Stavin now gave a good display and, led 13-9. Savory got another single but that was all, Slavin being the witaler by 5 chalks.</p>
        <p>M. Gleave accounted for J. Newton after a keen game. Making a good start Gieave was ahead by 4-1, but Newt( n overtook him and at one time led by 8-6. However, Gleave bowled with such judgment that ho had the satisfaction of winning by 1 points. A. Warburtou, one of the most promising of the Whitecross trundler, made a hold bid for success but had to admit defeat at the hands of J. Shaw. WarLurton opened in magnificent style and led 10-4. The p1a 3 ors were equal at 12 and 13 but in the critical moments that</p>
        <p>his contestant at 13. The next point went to Wood: and the spectators watched with breathless interest Gleave had the good fc,rtutie to gain two, and lour applaute signalized his victory by the odd chalk. V. D.Lniels ha I no difthuity in disposing of NV. Jones, who showcd little fight, and was beaten to Olt extent of 15-6.</p>
        <p>J. Appleton was opposed by G. Shaw, and after 64 the third end when the players were equal with 4 each, be always had his man in check. Although Shaw gut aa near as 11-12, Appleton played a witnint game throughout, placed arnajority of 4 to his credit. The following arc the complete scores : ItYLANDS. WITITECROSS: %V. Roughley . 15 ' T. Ellisbn (Capt.) ll H. Hua:phries. H. Eves H. Haslam R. Savory (capt.) .. ...... 10 J. Slavin</p>
