        <p>V. PATRICE:.</p>
        <p>friendly match and were easily vanquished, the visitors winning by 271 (for ( wickets) against the I poor score of 44. Wigan's batting Was fine T. Lynn ' knocked up 130 scarcely giving a chance and he was also assisted • by E. Elsworth who played splendid cricket and compiled SO runs. W. Mann reached 32 after a superb display. Warrington's batting was bad and considering the good wicket . almost • a disgrace. Only one 'player (p. Plinston) succeeded in reaching double figures, his score being 12. Roocroft bowled well for the visitors capturing no less than six wickets. The following are the West Lancashire League tables up-to-date :</p>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION. Club. P. W. L. D. Pts. Prescot</p>
        <p>Warrington St. Ho!ens Recs. St. Helens 9 ... 2 ... 4 ... 3 ... 7 E-Arlostown *Nevitou</p>
        <p>*Two points deducted for playing ineligible player.</p>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION'.</p>
        <p>Prescot</p>
        <p>St. Helens Reas Warrington Earlestown</p>
        <p>Newton St. Helens</p>
        <p>The important match between Warrington and St. Helens Recs. takes place at St. Helens next Saturday. If Warrington win they will be champions of the League; if the result is a draw they will have to meet Prescot in a final straggle for the honour, and if they should be defeated they will have to take second place. Good lack to them!</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District League lands' defeated Latchford Baptists by 20 runs, their score being 49. Beardsell and Newton for Rylands' were the only ones who reached double figures-12 and 10 respectively. McGowan (Ryland') took live wickets for 16 runs and Jewkes five for 12. (Tire (Baptists) captured seven wickets for 22 runs and Grice two for eight.</p>
        <p>Walker's Brewery were easily vanquished by the "Guardian." The later compiled 74 whilst the Brewery responded with the moderate total of 45. Riley for the printers batted well for his 34 runs whilst Paton (Guardian) took five wickets for 13 runs. F. Allen's score of 12 was the ldst show for the Brewer's. Roylanee (Walker's) was the most effective with the ball and took five wickets.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Lines Engineers were splendidly, beaten by the champion of the League (Latchford St. James). The former knocked up a score of 39, to whiah the latter responded in fine style with 92. Wright and Duckworth (Engineers) were the best batsmen on their side, their scores being 16 and 12 respectively. Five of the St. James' representatives reached double figures, but Knight exceeded them by several runs his score being 24. For the Engineers J. McKay took five wickets for 30 runs and E. Deeley three for 22. J. Hallam for St. James,' had the good analysis of six wickets for 12 runs and A, Green three for eleven.</p>
        <p>Wilderspool Brewery secured a fine victory over Whitecross, beating them by 128 to 16. The Brewers batting was splendid, their principal batsmen being W. Weisby (35) and J. Thompson (21). For Wilders- pool W. Howard took six wickets for nine runs and W. Lloyd three for five. While G. Jones for Whitecross captured five wickets for 37 runs.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington Junior League, Holy Trinity only just succeeded in recording a victory over Sankey. Sankey secured 36, and Holy Trinity followed up with 38 for 9 wickets —quite a narrow shave. There was no remarkable batting on either side, but J. Bradburn and Sankey succeeded in com- piling 10 and J. Warburton (Trinity) was responsible for 18. Carman for the Trinitarians secured 5 wickets, whilst Webb for Sankey captured no fewer than 8 wickets.</p>
        <p>Orford's score of 33 secured a victory over Stretton Appleton of 20 runs. Here again the batting was very</p>
        <p>The distribution of the cup and medals which took place on. the green immedi,aaly after the match was the oceasion of a little specch-making, punctuated with approving cheers and good natured inter:ections iron) the audience.</p>
        <p>him " better Ituk next time." The committee was composed entirely of a orkingmen, and they depended oa the kind support of ladies and gentlemen in the town to provide prizes for the main which commenced next Saturday. The Association never appealed in vain, but if there should be any lades and gentlemen present who had wit yet supported them, their lank book was not yet full. (Laughter.) The association made no money out of the handicap, everything being spent on prizes for the player,s. (Applause). Mr. J. WILLETT (chairman of the committee) voiced the thanks of the Association to the visitors for their presence and said be was glad to Eee F o many smiling faces round the green. As to the match itself, there was no douLt that each ttam had played "a storming good game" and he believed that those who had lost</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>"TWO IN."</p>
        <p>Tonge</p>
        <p>Tonge</p>
        <p>did no: wish their victor:ous friends the slightest harm. The vice-chairman and be (the-speaker), bad just com- pleted sevt n sears' work for the Aseociation. .When the organization was inaugurated some people pooh- poohed the idea but the committee had thrown them-</p>
        <p>, I liad,considering the wicket, and of the whole _match J. \Bennett (Orford) was the only fellow who got double figures, his 'score being 16. Jolley (Orford) took 6 wickets for 5 runs, and S. Leigh (Orford) captured 4 for 8.</p>
        <p>The cricket team of the "Liverpool Daily Post and Mercury" came over to Warrington on Saturday and played Warrington Municipal. The mai&amp; was extremely exciting for the visitors knocked up 43 and the Municipal were only 4 behind when the game closed. Only one player (Holland. Municipal) reached double figures, 13 being put to his credit. Holland bowled well for the home team, accounting for 7 wickets, while for• the visitors Woodward was the most successful man with the ball, capturing 7 wickets.</p>
        <p>Saturday was the Lymm "Derby Day," for the Lymm and Oughtrington elevens tried conclusions. The match was followed with great interest and Lymm had the satisfaction of winning, their rivals only com- piling 127 runs against 159 for 7 wickets. Oughtring- ton's best batsmen were J. G. L. Farrar, who scored 17, and H. M. Johnson, who put to his credit 21. W. H. Podinore played splendid cricket for Lymm, reach- ing 59 without hardly giving a change, when he was out I.b.w. G. F. Brazendale and T. Marsh also were conspicuous,*adding 32 and 27 respectively. N. Wood for the visiting team and Brazendale.for the home home team were the most successful with the ball. .</p>
        <p>Bowline. ilylands' No. 1 are to be heartily congratulated upon their victory in the final of the Warrington Works Competition. All the same it was hard lines on Whitecross who had looked forward to becoming permanent holders of the cup. The struggle was well worth witnessing and there were some surprising results. A full illustrated account of the match appears in another part of this issue.</p>
        <p>To-day the annual individual merit handicap bwins and with a record entry of 274 it should be a splendid succt ss.</p>
        <p>Next Saturday there will be a charity match at Eccles between teams representing Warrington and Eccles workshops respectively. The following players will do duty for Warrington :—G. Ball, jun. (captain), A. Gleave, Jack Cleave, Ben Webb, R. Jefferson, W. Randles, H. Dainttth, J. Barton, J. Jefferson, R.Clarke, T. Horrabin, A. Scott, G. Ward, J. Looker, Ted Gleave, W. Patrick, H. Haslam, H. Fairbrother, T. Humphries, S. Ratcliffe, W. Andrews, R. Savory, A. Rosterne, E. Trynan, B. Tomlinson, G. Smith, J.Firth, W. Holloway, J. Gleave (Greenings), J. Broadhurst. Mr. J. Willett will act as referee. .</p>
        <p>Good luck to Warrington C emetery in their match with Leigh Railway to-day ! The encounter is an important one for it decides the honour of being the holders of the Lancashire and Cheshire championship cup and winners of the medals.</p>
        <p>In the final for the second teams' competition last Saturday Whitefield were once more successful,defeat- ing Warrington Cemetery by 36. Whitefield Second team have won this championship on the last five occasions they have entered, which is a record to be proud of. In this game only one player failed. to register double figures, W. Hill only scoring .5 against H. Crossley. The former was 5-0, but upon Crossley getting the jack he ran right to game. J. Smith led Antrobus 12-4, but only 'added 2 whilst his opponent scored the necessary 17. The lowest score on the Whitefield side was 13, and they had eight winners to four on the Warrington side. •</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Era,rsos (captain of Whitecross team) said they had hed a good fight. They fully expscted to will but they had been beaten by good sportsmen and he wished Rylands's every success. (Applause). -Mr. ROBERT PIERPOINT said he felt it a high honour to be asked in the presenca of so many Warringtoniane, ,to propose a vote of thanks to Mrs. Crosfield for her very good offices on that occasion. It gave him great pleasure to do so, because on an occasion like that he desired to pay all the honour he could to the wife of his friend Mr. Caosfield. (Applause.) It was not for him to dilate upon the charming manner in which Mrs. Crosfield had performed her duties. Of that they were at least as good judges as he,and he left them to express their opinion upon that point by the hearty response they would Rive to the vote of thaaks which he now proposed. (Applause). The MAYOR in seconding, said they would all agree with Mr. Pierpoint tLat Mrs. Crosfield had performed her duties in a very charming manner. He must con- gratulate the association upon having such a splendid afternoon for the competition and the winners upon their success. At the same time he hoped that the losers would have better luck next time. The game of howls ought to be very much encouraged and especially amongst the working classes. He enjoyed a game of °owls himself and it had been a source of great pleasure to him to have witnessed such good games that day (Applause). 'I he vote was gassed with great enthusiasm. Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, riaing at the request of gr. Willett to return thanks on behalf of his wife, said he thought his old friend Mr. Willett had made a little slip and it would have been much better if be bad asked Mrs. Crosfield to respond for herself. (Langhter and applause and a voice: "A Suffragette ") That was a sob- ject which he must remind his friend required to be handled a little delicately—(laughter)—and he would not accept the invitation to skate over the rather thin ice of the suffrage question. But as that sugges- tion had been made, and as their lady friends were very much to the fore in these dais, he did not really see why he should be called upon to do duty for his wife on that ec:asion. (Laughter). He had none the less pleasure in thanking with all his heart, his old friend Mr. Pierpoint for the extremely kind and handsome terms in which he proposed that vote of thanks so . gracefully to his wife. lie (Mr. Ctesfield) was there in plece of his brother George, the President of the Association, eh() was busy with the Territorials, and in that capacity he wished to make one or two persenal references. That really magnificent gathering was a wonderful example of the progress which bowls had made in the last fifteen or twenty years in Warrington as in other parts of England. It made oec thiva that the good old English game which they hid heard of from the time of the Spanish Armada—and Le did not know how long before —had really tekcel a fresh lease of life and was no perhaps the foremost sport in the country. (Applause.) As the Warringtnn Works Bowling Association had proved such a great success, and teat was the end of such an important competition, he was sure everyone present was delighted to see Sir Gilbert and Ledy Greenall and Mr. and Mrs. Pierpoint amongst them. .(Applause.) He warred ti thank the Mayor also for the very kind remarks which he had made. If they would for- give him waking a personal allusiop,he must say that they were very sorry indecd that during the last twelve months they had lost two vice-presidents who took an active interest in the nssociatiou—Couucillor W. B. Roberta and Councillor Nield. Having made a/celiac allusion to the death of Mr. John White, Mr. Crosfield said be rejoiced immensely that the Works Bowling Association had been such a signal success. TLey had all enjoyed seeing the splendid skill with which the games were fought and they appreciated not less that fine hearty English spirit which enabled them to give and take good knocks, shethar at bowls or at politics. (Applause): He thanked the assembly most sincerely for the kindnees with sahich they had received the vote of thanks and elso the gentlemen who proposed and seconeed it in such handsome terms. (Applause). Mr. WILLETT said they were all sorry that Captain Crosfield, who had been president of the association from its inception, could not be with them. But although ire was away at camp be had not forgotten them and the members of both teams as well as the committee would Le entertained at tea as his guests. Mr. Willett announced that Lady Greenall had kindly promised to give £5 as the first prize in the bowling handicap. (Applause.) Sir GILBERT GKEENALL said he very much appreciated being asked ti be present to see that match. Sports and open-air exercises were for the benefit of them all and it was good for the nation that they should be engaged in. (Applause.) THE TEAMS ENTERTAINED. After the match both teams and officials were enter tamed to tea by the President (Captain G. R. Crosfield) the catering .being in the bands of _Mr. ..and Mrs,</p>
        <p>solves whole-heartedly into the work and to-day they Campbell and giving the utmost satisfaction. The had as good an association as any in the country, tables having teen cleared, a social evening followed.' (Applause.) It inaight the huge work 4 and the little Songs were well sung by Messrs. Beswick (hrothers),J. works of the town together in friendly rivalry and des- Horton, J. Wrench, and J. Gorst. The accompaniments troyed ill-feelingbetween employees or various concerns. were played by Mr. J. Gorst. During an interval Mr. Mrs. Crusaeld then gracefully presented the cup to G. Bali proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman (Mr. the captain of the wirming team (Mr. R. Savory) who J. Willett,which was seconded by Mr. A. E. BurdAt,ond in returning thanks said that although Whit ecross were carried with enthusiasm. Mr. H. Holford moved a vote expected to win, Rylandi's had had the satisfaction of of thinks to the Secretary (Mr. W. PatEck), which was achieving the victory. They wished th) losers better seconded by Mr. T. O'Brien, the company singing "For luck next time. (Applaase). - • he's a jolly good fellow." The toasts honoured were : Mrs. Crostleld Landed gold med,ls to the in mhers of "The King and Queen: and Royal Family": "Captain Ityhmda's tea at and silver medals with gold centres to G. It. Crosfiel4, president"; "Association and officials"; WhitecroSS team. Hest and hostess, Mr. and Irs. C an?hell."</p>
        <p>°Walley, the Australian forward, is in his way to England, and is expected to arrive in about .a fort— night.</p>
        <p>S. Lees, the popular half back, has signified his intention to assist the club occasionally, as business will prevent his turning out regularly.</p>
        <p>The Committee are expecting to turn out a strong team, which will include the ever-green—Fish—G. Dickenson, Taylor, Tiiley, Brooks and the Welsh contingent.</p>
        <p>A junior rugby team has been formed in connection with the Rylands -Athletic Club and they will com— pete with the St. Helens and District Junior League. The list of fixtures has not yet been completed, but there are already some very interesting matches down for decision.</p>
