        <p>CLERKSHIP TO JUSTICES. pp,ES COTAND CHILD WALL SESSIONAL DIVISIONS. rike, JUSTICES for the PETTY SESSIONAL th 4SIONS of PRESCOT and CHILDWALL require Ile services of a SOLICITOR to perform jointly with q*Rl W. IL Thomas the duties of CLERK of PETTY 411 "'IONS, LICENSING and SPECIAL SESSIONS 11„?f Clerk to the Justices within those Divisions. Rivue Solicitor appointed will be specially required to e his personal attendance at the sittings of the .khurt at Prescot and Woolton, and also at Widnes oir the other Joint Clerk cannot attend, and to pro- 'for'„an office at Prescot for the issue of summonses and the 'le transaction of magisteriul work together with .ribecessary stationery, forms and books. i Iviyl,, salary for the joint appointment s .C 795 of ,C 330 will be paid to the holder of the office now erased o?es Solicitor need apply who has not had experience the w Ab CM" )3q -13.1 cations stating age and previous experience to Meat to The Clerk of the Peace, County Offices, eston,'P on or before the Ist September 1909, HARCOUT E. CLARE, Connty Offices, Preston. Clerk of the Peace. 10th August, 1909.</p>
        <p>PETTY</p>
        <p>,ades Council. T:he monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades 1.011461 was held on Tuesday evening in the I.L.P. iita°lllB, Mr. W. Bullock presiding. There was only a attendance. Arising out of the decision of the tetlll,:;t of Appeal in respect to the Musicians' Union •-tlOOl 11: the Secretary (Mr. E. Plinston) said that •'the etilio_g• had been read into the Act whiefh was not Haley said that the decision seriously ed every trade union in the Kingdom. If he .oth erstml rightly it meant that if men came out and .a4(r men took their places, the dispute was at an end •,,,,any action to help the men was libellous.—The 4a 'etarY read a commnnication from the National ,e4°elation of Prudential Assurance Agents which tained various amendments in respect to the %Iran leco ce Companies' Act, 1909, which has pawl its tievlld reading in the House of Lords.—The amend- tlee.,,tB Were endorsed by the Council.—lt was also aristl'ed that the Council meet every four weeks the second Tuesday in each month. VRESERVING PLUMS.—Clieshire Plums, 2s. 6d. n doz. ; Violet Plums. 2s. doz. ; Prolific Plums, '4. doz. McDERAIOTT, "Cheapest Man Alive." •"4ARKET &amp; BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
