      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>S ‘TURDAY, UGUST ]4th. 1909.</p>
        <p>We should probably be MEETING THE wrong in supposing HARD CASES. that the public are</p>
        <p>following Parliament- ary proposals very diligently at the moment. The taxpayer is interested mainly in the broad lines of the Budget, and scarcely at all in the smalls give-and-take which is made necessary by the character of our Parlia- mentary institutions. What is going on in the House of Commons at present is some- thing that the average student of politics cannot be expected to track with any par- ticular closeness. It is the winnowing pro- cess that is unavoidable in connection with all large Bills—the selection of the line of least resistance, in fact. What may be broadly stated is that the Government is now, as it has been from the first, extremely ready to comply with all reasonable demands to meet all "hard cases," so far as they can be met, and, whilst adhering strictly to the line of its original proposals, to remove grounds of objection which do not involve a surrender. The PRIME MINISTER made a statement on Wednesday which, in any other assebmly than the House of Commons, would have been accepted without demur as being of an extremely conciliatory character. He repeated the statement made the previous day as to the cost of the valuation of land being undertaken by the Goveimment,stating that this was done with a view to the better working of the Act if it shall in due time be passed. His estimate of the cost, we are glad to see, falls very far short of the formidable sums which have been paraded on OppOsi- tion platforms. The whole thing, it seem , can be done for two million pounds, which is 'eighteen millions under the Tory estimate of what it would cost the landlords for one year only. The landlords, as a matter of fact, could supply a valuation of their lands with- out any great cost, the valuation being already down in their accounts as a matter of ordinary bookkeeping. A more important announcement made by the PRIME MINISTER was that, instead of placing a tax on unworked minerals, revenue will be collected on mining royalties and other receipts of money associated therewith. This seems to us to be admirable. It is a proposal made in the first place to conciliate the owners of unworked minerals, but it has the other estimable characteristic that it takes a portion of the unearned profit of minerals that are being worked, which is a step that has long been demanded int.a certain definite and most sensible direction. The motives of a Government are never taken into consideration by the Opposition. Ministers may do this or the other to meet objections on" the other side," and the other side becomes more infuriated than ever. This is what happened on Wednesday. Mr. A.USTEN CHAMBERLAIN tore more than one passion to tatters in denouncing what he called the PRIME MINISTER'S introduction of a new Budget. Mr. BALFOUR followed suit. This is all very entertaining from lone point of view. Everybody knows what it means. The leaders of the Opposition are playing to the gallery ;outside. They are thinking of bye-elections, and even of a General Election, and of inducing the people to rally against what is just now a most popular Budget. Hope springs eternal in the human breast. There has been a turn in the tide; and the Opposition has a despairing idea that it may be induced to turn another way. Meantime, the Government is getting on very well— and the Budget remains the most popular democratic measure of our time.</p>
        <p>Every week that passes BACKING THE sees the popularity of BUDGET. the Budget enhanced,</p>
        <p>and the -efforts of the Opposition to discredit this magnificent piece of statesmanship and to arouse the pity of the masses by holding forth on the sad lot of the wealthy landowner have signally failed. No measure of modern times has so united and fired the enthusiasm of all sections of the progressive forces, for it is based on that unassailable principle that the burden of taxation should be adjusted according to the ability to pay and that, as a natural conse- quence, it is far more reasonable to tax luxuries, large incomes and land values than the necessities of life. We are glad that Warrington is proving itself sound on this great question. It is but a few weeks since the Budget received the support of a record open-air demonstration in point of numbers, but the Progressive Labour League is continuing the campaign with unabated vigour and Monday evening saw another great - gathering at which the Budget was approved with unanimity and cordiality. The League is doing invaluable service in keeping the elector- ate in touch with progressive politics and Monday evening's meeting was one more evidence that the workers of Warring- ton are fully appreciative of the lasting boon</p>
        <p>which Mr. Lloyd-George's heroic financial proposals will confer upon the nation. Mr. Robert Lewis's address was racy, well reasoned, and powerful, and it was obvious that it made a deep impression. The exposi- tion of the land clauses was followed with particularly keen attention, and Mr. Lewis unquestionably had the audience with him in his declaration for" the Budget, the whole Budget, and nothing but the Budget."</p>
        <p>The Board of Trade</p>
        <p>TRADE REVIVING. returns for July make encouraging reading. Trade is once more on the up-grade, for imports, exports and re-exports, alike show recovery, and the general expectations of a period of greater prosperity appear to be well-founded. Briefly stated, our imports show an improvement over July, 1908, of £3,547,573 and the exports of British goods of £1,781,515, while in our merchant trade, that is in what we buy and re-sell of foreign and Colonial goods, there is the substantial advance of £1,063,303. A perusal of the following table will enable our readers to appreciate more fully the excellence of last month's record : JULY TRADE, 1900-1909. Imports. Fxports. Re-exports. 1930 ... £40,200,000 £24,601,000 .0,800,000 1901 ... 43,000,000 24,400,000 5,500,000 1902 ... 44,100,000 26,000,000 6,000,000 1903 ... 45,600,000 25,900,000 5,700,000 1904 ... 43,900,000 21,800,003 5,303,000 1905 ... 41,700,000 27,800,003 5,800,000 1906 ... 48,600,000 33,100,030 6,000,040 1907 ... 52,200,000 40,400,000 7.400,000 1938 ... 46,800,000 33,700,000 6300,000 1909 ... 50;307,000 35,500,000 7,303,000</p>
        <p>In only one year-1907—were imports and exports better than in July, 1909, while as compared with the good year 1900 a very great increase is shown. While the increased imports consist mainly of foodstuffs, grain and flour contributing a rise of .£2,572,739, it is exceptionally noteworthy that imports of raw materials rose by as much as £1,128,000 last month, indicative of increased production in many trades. Of the inorease in exports, iron and steel and the manu- facture thereof, account for £300,625, leather and leather goods for £55,266, and cotton manufactures for £523,717. It is evident that the tide has already turned and is rising rapidly, and the improvement in conditions in the United States is also having a stimu- lating effect here. British capital has not yet taken wings,and the returns conclusively prove that democratic legislation is no wise impairing our credit or hampering our trade.</p>
        <p>It is the land clauses THE LAND.— of the Budget which A LESSON FROM excite the fiercest the</p>
        <p>NEW ZEALAND.</p>
        <p>opposition from</p>
        <p>wealthier classes, and it is freely stated that these taxes will inter- fere with the rights of property and injure the prosperity of the country. Fortunately we have the experience of other countries to guide us. Let us take New Zealand; where there is at present a graduated land tax which will soon rise to something like 3d. in the .E on the capital value of big estates. We do not propose in this country to adopt any- thing so strong. But in all the big cities of New Zealand there is a system of rating on the unimproved value of land, which is lore- cisely similar to the tax on "Undeveloped Land" which the House of Commons has been discussing during the past week. Here ' we have a very good test. For this tax is applied in New Zealand to all its four big cities—cities not unlike our English cities. A return written by the Commissioner of Taxes in the Land and Income Tax Depart- ment at Wellington, and published in the Blue Book issued by the Government, dis- poses of many objections, for he points out that the effect of the Unimproved Values Tax in New Zealand "has certainly been greatly to stimulate the building trade,"and "to cause vacant sites to be put to their best use by expenditure on improvements." This is how the Wellington City Councillors describe the operation of the tax on the life of the city of Wellington: Encourages improvements, stimulate the use of land, secures the unearned increment to those who have added the value, mulcts all lands in their fair share of taxation, and paralyses the old. system under which rental values on lands could by simple manipulation reduce local taxa- tion to a farce. Much, if not most, of the activity of building operations during past year due to influence of this healthy measure, and with necessary reqtrictions to prevent ovei crowd- ing of buildings no untoward result need be apprehended. No combined serious objection. Cost of industrial ventures not increased.</p>
        <p>Really, that report makes English mouths Water. If we can produce that kind of effect by our taxes, why should we not do so?</p>
        <p>The new attitude of the THE DEMAGOGIC Tariff Reform organs SUPERLATIVE, should give the elec-</p>
        <p>torate -material for serious thought. They accuse Mr. Lloyd- George of being a demagogue, but they urge the Opposition to impress upon the nation that Tariff Reform will give the same social reform as the Budget offers, only no one will have to pay. Mr. Lloyd-George they say, is a demagogue ; let us be demagogues too, but let us go one better. Mr. Lloyd-George has dealt frankly and fairly with the nation. He has told them that, for national security and social reform, all classes must be prepared to pay their share. The Tariff Reformers say no one need pay, no one but the foreigner. In Germany, social reform costs nothing to the Germans; the foreigner pays! Strange that the Imperial Government there should be confronted with a fiscal crisis; that Prince Billow should have fallen on the question of Death Duties; that there should be a Prussian Income-tax, that there should even be land-taxes similar to those which the Opposition here are so fiercely denouncing; and that the contribution of the State to the working-man's insurance should be only a fraction of the whole, when the whole can so easily be gathered from the foreigner ! Let us admit that Mr. Lloyd-George is a demagogue. " Demagogue " only means a leader of the people. But the dangerous demagogue is the leader who tries to blind the people, and make them believe that they will get benefits for nothing, while he intends to fleece and spoil them. The Tariff Reformer is a dangerous demagogue indeed.</p>
        <p>Our Conservative SIR EDWARD GREY friends area, fond of pro- D THE fessing the utmost BUDGET. admiration for Sir - Edward Grey, whose masterly and tactful handling of affairs at the Foreign Office has gained universal approval. Sir Edward is entitled to be listened to with equal respect when he addresses himself to home politics, and opponents of the Govern- ment would do well to ponder his dispassionate, logical and, in our view, unanswerable vindication of the Budget, and particularly of the land clauses. With a simple directness which carries con- viction like a lightning flash, Sir Edward also disposed once for all of the myth that the Budget proposals create any "insecurity of capital." To go back on the past would create some insecurity, for banks, insurance societies, . friendly societies, and</p>
        <p>trade unions may have invested money on mortgages: upon the' unearned increment. But the Government have scrupulously abstained from going back on the past. They have even exempted all prospective increment that can be forseen at the time of valuation. Capital knows what it may and- may not reckon upon under the Government's pro- posals. But Mr. Balfour's proposals—a policy of Tariff Reform—will leave every manufac- turer in doubt how far he will be penalised upon his raw material. It is Tariff Reform which would make Capital and Industry insecure.</p>
        <p>The chief reason, so far ROOF GARDENS. as we can gather, why roofs of dwelling- houses in this country are usually made sloping is so that heavy rains and snow may pass away without damage to the building materials. That may have been a necessary precaution fifty or a hundred years ago, but with improved surface substances and drain- acre systems it is now quite unnecessary,and, indeed, undesirable, particularly in the big towns. There, where land is so dear and often the delights even of a backyard have to be foregone, the roof garden is obviously a necessity, and it ought to be compulsory upon those who erect new houses under such conditions to make provision for it by means of an accessible, fenced-in, flat roof. It is a simple matter for the tenant, however poor, to convert the open space into a flower or even vegetable garden. Broken barrels, battered old tins, cracked dishes, and even jam jars can be used to contain soil and a few seeds. When colour and fragrance are borne upon thEo plants, the little space, cooler and cleaner than the street below, will surely give the keenest happiness to the children as well as the parents in the house. We cannot pre- tend that a roof garden may be a great agent of social improvement, but we are sure that it would reduce squalor a little, and it might even assist the children's health. A baby could safely be left lying during warm weather in shade upon the roof while the mother was at work; at any rate, the air there would be better than ta a stuffy little room. Some enterprising hotels and large shops have arranged elaborate roof gardens, and are said to have found them very popular. While the streets are oppressively hot and dazzling, the gardens high above them are cool and shady, and there is air pleasant to breathe. It is not surprising that the outlay has proved itself to be profitable. Very hot weather is the best time to press the claims of the roof garden upon people's minds, and those who can appreciate them should think how easily a little more brightness and charm could be given to slum dwellers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>NOTES AN,TI!S.</head>
        <p>NOTES AN,TI!S.</p>
        <p>The retort courteous at the Warrington Town Council meeting. Alderman (weary of the debate) : "I want my dinner." Coun- cillor (unimpressed) : "You should have got it before you came." * * •</p>
        <p>The belated heat wave permeated the Council Chamber on Tuesday evening and, judging from the little " scenes " which occurred, it did not improve the temper of some of the members of the august assembly. To the public in attendance it was doubtless entertaining to listen to smart passages- at-arms punctuated with cries of " Chair " and " Vote " but the dignity of the Council cannot be said to be enhanced by such proceedings. * * *</p>
        <p>The Unionist have been ready to criticise the Budget, but what policy do they offer the country? They have been very slow to emphasise the alternative up to the present; but during the past week they have come out more boldly. The Tariff Reformers are beginning to say plainly that they will no longer hold their tongues. And what is their plan? Why, to take the taxes off the owners of land and put them on the con- sumers of food. They rebognise that the money must be raised, but they want to raise it by taxing the man whose main food is bread. They would "broaden the basis of taxation." What does that mean? It means taking the taxes off the rich and putting them on the poor. Sparing the land and tax- ing the labourer!</p>
        <p>Great satisfaction will be occasioned by the announcement made at the open-air meeting on Monday evening that Mr. Alexander Ure, K.C., the Lord Advocate, will address a great meeting in the Parr Hall towards the end of September. Efforts were made to arrange that Mr. Ure should visit Warrington in the earlier part of the year, but a multitude of engagements stood in the way, the Lord Advocate being in great demand as a speaker on the land question. Warrington progres- sives will rejoice that they are shortly to have the opportunity of hearing this eminent advocate' of land reform, for Mr. Ure is undoubtedly one of the first authorities on this momentous subject and his opinions always carry great weight. * *</p>
        <p>The Borough Member's interest in sport is proverbial and Mrs. Crosfield appears to he endowed with equal enthusiasm for the advancement of heal,thful recreations. On Saturday, accompanied by her husband, she attended the final of the Warrington Works Bowling Competition—the battle par excel- lence in local bowling circles—and the members of the contesting teams had the pleasure of receiving their medals at her hands. On Thursday Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield came over for the ladies' main in connection with the Cemetery Bowling ,club and Mrs. Crosfield again gracefully performed the ceremony of distributing - the prizes. She also tried her skill at the game and handled the woods so well that the ladies are hoping to welcome her as a fellow-competitor next year. ' • * * *</p>
        <p>We remember a time in the dim and distant past when the Town Council gaily voted for the provision of several addi- tional public bowling greens. The bowling fraternity could scarce conceal their joy. Such thoughtfulness on the part of the city fathers appealed too good to be true. • And so it has proved, for up to the present not one extra green has been laid,and there is no immediate prospect of one either. The question was raised at the Council meeting on Tuesday and Councillor Pemberton castigated the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee for setting the Council at defiance. But the Chairman (Alderman Bolton) only smiled blandly and said that when some of the committees which owned land would hand over a few sites, his com- mittee would see what could be done. It is high time someone was spurred to action, for the one green in Victoria Park has proved such a boon as abundantly to justify , the authorities in embarking upon an extensive scheme. Bowlers have not yet resigned all hope and we have no doubt that further concessions would be appreciated to the full.</p>
        <p>The attitude of the Opposition on the valuation question has inspired a member of the House with the following lines : The Tories clamoured that the State,</p>
        <p>Should pay for valuation; When, prompt to yield to reason's plea, Asquith announced that he'd agree, They merely said "D -nation." * * *</p>
        <p>Lord Nelson, who was eighty-six years old on Saturday, has been a peer longer than any man living, for he succeeded his father, who held the title only eight months,seventy- four years ago. Lord Nelson descends from the Sucklings, of Barsham, of whom the most Potable were Sir Jonn Suckling, the poet, and that Captain Maurice Suckling, uncle of the hero, through whose influence the nephew first went to sea. Trafalgar was fought on the anniversary of a successful action by Captain Maurice with the French, neqrly fifty years before, a circumstance to which Nelson alluded at the commencement of his last and most memorable battle.</p>
        <p>Lord Lansdowne compared the Govern- ment to a "swooping robber-gull." He little thought that he was supplying the other side with a happy and effective illus- tration. Lord Lansdowne's "robbing gull" does not fish itself, but swoops upon another gull that has caught a fish, and confiscates it. So, Sir Edward Grey pointed out, the ground- landlord, who does not produce wealth himself, swoops down upon the man who has built up a great business, and confiscates his earned wealth, converting it into the land- lord's unearned increment. But the Govern- ment could only be compared to a gull which took from the robber one-fifth of his spoils, and employed it not for its own advantage, but for the good of "the community of the gulls at large."</p>
        <p>The Health Committee were again taken to task at the Town Council meeting for their refusal to grant licences under the Poisons and Pharmacy Act, 1908, to two local seedsmen, for the sale of certain poisons used for horticultural and farm purposes. Notwithstanding the adverse vote of the Council at a previous meeting the Health Committee consistently maintained their attitude, and we believe they did it from conscientious motives and with no desire to deal unfairly with the individuals concerned. Their contention was that it should be made more difficult to obtain dangerous poisons and that the sale should therefore be restricted to qualified chemists. The Town Council again thought otherwise and referred the resolution back. The Health Committee have since -reconsidered the matter and decided to compromise by grant- ing the licences for the sale of poisons, other than sheep dips to which the greatest objec- tion has been made. * * *</p>
        <p>It is amazing that noble peers do not realize the folly of the tactics they have adoptedito embarrass the Government in their financial proposals. This week publicity is given to letters from two Earls to the secre- tary of the Cheshire Agricultural Society. The Earl of Haddington, in sending a sub- scription of £5, says: Owing to recent legislation I find that I must reduce the amount of my principal usual sub- scriptions. and I am afraid I may have to reduce that to the Cheshire Agricultural Society to three guineas in the future. The Earl of Derby sent no subscription at all, finding it necessary "under present cir- cumstances "to curtail the number of sub- scriptions given by his late father. Notwith- standing the advice of candid friends in the Unionist Press, the Earls still fail to see that this method of fighting the Budget will only stimulate the determination of the Progres- sive forces to see the measure carried through. ** * *</p>
        <p>A man does not work for nothing. even for his wife.—Judge Bacon. After the Budget is passed this country will remain, perhaps, the one country of all others in the world in which the possession of property is most enjoyable and property most secure.—Sir Edward Grey, M.P. There is no country in the world so free from the cant of an aggressive militarism as England is.—Field-Marshall Sir George White.</p>
        <p>Speeches like that delivered by. the Duke of Portland are worth their weight in gold to the Liberal Party.—Mr. Winston Churchill, MP. _ __ _</p>
        <p>Ido not think the people of this country are ever going to 'deprive themselves of the right of saying the last word upon questions of great national importance.— Lord Lansdowne.</p>
        <p>In this contest we have some unusual allies. The dukes have been helping us; men with private incomes of from £lO,OOO to £30,000 a year have been telling the people that if they are taxed they will no longer subscribe to charities and agricultural shows. " If you demand your rights," these people say, "you shall no longer have the scraps from our table." "Give us our rights,' people cry, "and keep your scraps."—.34r. Herbert Samuel, ALP.</p>
        <p>• ** * How often are we assured that it is impossible "to make people sober by Act of I Parliament " ! Constant repetition has caused the assertion to be . unthinkingly accepted as gospel in many quarters, but this week there is a striking piece of evidence to ,the contrary. The Lord Advocate, in answer to a question in Parliament, gave the figures relating to drunkenness and crime in Scot- land. These indicate that since the increase in the price of whisky due to the Budget came into force, there has been an extra- ordinary diminution in the number of apprehensions and committals for drunken- ness under the various heads -as compared with last year. The figures are as follows : Total Committals to Apprehensions. Prison. May June May June 1908 4.361. 4,404 1,578 1,742 1909 2,965 2,872 1,138 1,131 The figures speak for themselves. It is also worthy of note that in London the sales of spirits are stated to have- gone down in dramatic fashion at many points where the increased charge was made, and the sellers of whisky are already repenting of the drastic raising of prices to the consumer. At rail- way refreshment bars, where the price of the glass of whisky became fourpence immedi- ately after tha introduction of the Budget, threepence is again being charged.</p>
        <p>The much discussed -sky-sign on Messrs. Henry Milling and Co.'s premises formed the subject of a full-dress debate at the Town Council meeting on Tuesday. This con- spicuous advertising device was erected in ignorance of the fact that it contravened a local Act of Parliament but the Corporation, instead of insisting upon its immediate removal, granted a twelve . months' reprieve. Now that period has expired and on being served with a notice to pull down the sign Messrs. Milling have applied for permission to retain it. The Street Improvement Com- mittee decided that the sign be permitted to remain for one year only on payment of a £lO acknowledgment. Many members of the Council considered this to be the infliction of a hardship upon an old- established firm, but with every desire to treat Messrs. Milling with fairness and cox-</p>
        <p>sideration, there is a principle involved which cannot be overlooked. The sign is an infringe- ment of the Act and if this is to be permitted in one case, other firms have a perfect right to ask for equal privileges. We admire Messrs. Milling's enterprise but we cannot contemplate with pleasure the prospect of a multiplication of skysigns which, however admirable they may be from a business stand- point, are certainly not ornamental. The Council, however, referred the matter back to the committee and the result will be awaited with some interest.</p>
        <p>i LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>A Show of Gloxinias. We are asked to announce that the conservatory in Bank Park (near the Drill Hall) which contains a fine show of gloxinias will be open to the public on Sunday afternoon and during next week. Open-Air Politics.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Ramage (National Reform Union) will be the speaker at an open-air meeting to be held near Arpley Cannons, under the auspices of the Progressive Labour League, on Monday evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Local __Presbyterianism.</head>
        <p>Local __Presbyterianism.</p>
        <p>Mr. William-Reid, the newly-appointed minister-in- charge of St. John's Presbyterian Church, and Mrs. Reid swill be welcomed at a s ciatgathering in Cairo- street schoolroom, on Thursday evening. Athletic Successes.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes police and tradesmen's sports on Thursday evening, T. Lightfoot, of Warrington, was second in the quarter-mile fiat race, from the 27 yards mirk. G. W. Mat, Crotfields' RC. (63) wus first in the half-mile fiat rece. Union Bank of Manchester.</p>
        <p>We are informed that Sir Joseph Verdin, Baronet, of Brockhurst, Northwich, and Garnstone Castle, Weobley, Herefordshire, has joined the Board of Directors of the Union Bank of Manchester, Limited.— Nursing Association.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington District Nursing Association will be held at the Town Hall on Monday evening and it is hoped there will be a large attendance of the public to hear something of the work of this excellent institution. Shrewsbury Fete.</p>
        <p>Shrewsbury Floral Fete, the grandest of its kind in Great Britain, will be held on Wednesday and Thurs- day next. The chief feature will be a magnificent flower show, over V. 200 being offered in prizes. A musical treat will be provided by the regimental bands of MAI. Coldstream Guards,ll3l. Irish Guards, and the Shropshire Yeomanry Cavalry. There will be balloon ascent, horse leaping competitions, and a host of other attractions.</p>
        <p>Music in the Park. Crosfield's band will play the following programme of music to-morrow, Sunday, at 3.15 p.m. Hymn, se!ected ; march, "Entry of the Gladiators," Fucik ; &amp;election, "Gems of Welsh melody," J. 0. flume; trombone solo, "Lend me Your aid, Gounod ; grand selection; " Cing Mars," Arr. Round; cornet solo, selected; Chorus, " All we like sheep," Handel ; "God save the King." Mr. G. F. Bedforth will conduct.</p>
        <p>BABY CARRIAGES AND Go-CARTS.—Clearance S de. All prices redtved.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Show.</p>
        <p>We would remind our readers of the annual show in connection with the Warrington and District Agricui- tural Society which will be held at Walton Park on Thursday next. The entries a.re well up to the high standard of previous years and the hunter classes promise to be exceptionally good. There are no fewer than sevent3 entries in the four riding classes--which is probably a record for this season's shows. The leaping competition is also expected to be very keen. The band of the "Queen's Own" Yorkshire Dragoons will submit a choice musical programme and, given a fine day, the show should be a grand success.—An interesting adjunct will be the Walton Flower Show, which is doing a good work in encouraging horticul- tural pursuits in the village. Brave Girl Rewarded.</p>
        <p>Yesterday afternoon there was a full muster of the Police Force in the Recreation Room at the Police Buildings, when the Chief Constable, on behalf of the Police, presented a gold watch and chain suitably inscribed, to Miss Mabel Marsh, the sixteen?yearold daughter of Mr. James Marsh, 120, Pierpoint-street, in recognition of her plucky conduct in assisting Con- stable Faulkner, who was brutally assaulted by two men and a woman in a passage at the rear of Pier- point-street, late on the night of Saturday, 22nd May. Amongsti those who witnessed the presentation were Mrs. Keeling, wife of the Rev. E. B. Keeling, of Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, of wl ose Sunday school Miss Marsh is a member, and Councillor Wilson.</p>
        <p>A " False Alarm." The fire bell rang shortly after eight o'clock on Monday evening and a large crowd was soon hurrying to the Fire Station, only to discover that the alarm was a false one. As it is three months since the Fire Brigade was called out to a fire,the committee hit upon this plan of testing the men, for the members of the brigade did not know that the bell was to be rung. The information was that the " fire" had broken out at Atherton's Quay. In order to see how the brigade turned out, Councillor Dickenson was stationed close to the Fire Station and the other members of the com- mittee awaited events on the river bank near Messrs. Monks, Hall arid Co.'s works. The men got to work very promptly 'and a satisfactory practice resulted, the ample supply of water from the river thoroughly test- ing the engine and applicances.</p>
        <p>TRAVELLING TRUNKS, DRESS BASKETS, SUIT</p>
        <p>CASES, etc. Prices to suit all.—J. S. GARLA'SD, 51, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>British Women's Temperance Association.</head>
        <p>British Women's Temperance Association.</p>
        <p>At the kind invitation of Mrs. Garnett, several of the members of the above society along with friends amongst whom were the Mayoress (Mrs. Forshaw), and the ex-Mayoress and her daughter (Mrs. and Miss Smethurst), spent a very enjoyable time at Hall Nook, Penketh, on Thursday afternoon. The weather was Ideal and an inspection of the beautiful gardens was thoroughly enjoyed. The seats which were arranged about the lawn provided rest for some, whilst others enjoyed a game of croquet. Tea was .served in the dining room and a short meeting was held in the drawing room when Mrs. Simpson gave an account of her visit to Castle Howard, where the members of the National Executive Committee had spent six days as the guests of the Countess of Carlisle. Miss Bessie Dutton and Mrs. Owen thanked Mrs. Garnett for her hospitality also Mrs. Simpson fcr her report. On. leaving each received a bunch of flowers and expressed themselves well pleased with their outing.</p>
        <p>GOOD SECOND-HAND PIANOS, £5 55., ,C 8 Bs., and .Cl 2 12s. Others at higher prices. Fully guaranteed. Pianos tuned and repaited,—J. It. WOOD, 47. London rd., Stockton Heath. Advt.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. R. Daintith. Testimony to the deep respect in which the late Mr. R. Daintith, of Sankey-street, was held, was afforded by the large attendance at the funeral which took place at the Cemetery on Saturday afternoon. The Rev. J. Morgan Price, curate of Holy Trinity, officiated. The chief mourners were : Mrs. R. H. Daiiitith, Messrs. Joseph Daintith,John H. Daintith and Richard Daintith (sons), Mrs. H. P. Turncr,Miss M. E. Daintith and Miss Maud Daintith (daughters), Mrs. R. H. Daintith and Mrs. John H. Daintith (daughters-in-law)., Mrs. John Ashley, Mr. H. P. Turner (son-in-law), Harry Turner (grandson), Mr. Henry Daintith (nephew), Messrs. John L. Davenport, F. Garnett, George A. Smith, Charles Leigh, Joseph Podmore, Arthur Booth, H. Whittle, W. Miller, Thomas Gilbody (Ditton), Andrew Gardiner (Hough Green). Wreaths were sent by the following :—" Bob and Jenny,"` " Tissie -and Harry," "Joe and Pollie," "Jack and Dot," "Richard and Emily," "Mary Ellen and Maud," Mr. and Mrs. Charles Leigh, Mr. and Mrs_ Gardiner, Mrs. Birch Browne, Miss E. Billington, Miss I. Green, Miss E. Jackson, Mrs. Dobson, Mrs. Barber, Mr. and Mrs. Herbert. Denham, and Mr. Horobin and family.</p>
        <p>CYCLES.—Eqd of season. Clearance sale. Great reductions.—J. S. GARLAND, Bridge at., Wcrring- ton.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
