      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Mr. .1. W. Hayward (chairman) A!derman Bennett Dr. Markel and Mr. A. J. Joll.-y. THEFT OF OLD IRON.—William Hardman, 18, Cumber- land-street, Frederick Massey 25. Cumberland-street, and Henry Shufflebottom,l7, dumberland-street, youths, tvere charged with stealing on the 4th of August from A woiks in Knutsford-road apiece of copper tape and an iron pulley valued at 2s. Gd., the property of John Mainwaring.—The Chief Constable stated that about 5.30 on the afternoon of the above date a constable vho was on duty in. Knutsford-road saw the three defendants go into Joseph Brier ley's yard, at 279 Knuts- ford-road. He thought their actions were somewhat snspicious and accordingly made inquiries and with the assistance of Sergr ant Meacock the defendants were arrested. They were charged with stealing the metal and stated that it bad been given to them for work they had performed. Inquiries were however made and this statement was found to be incorrect. The metal had been taken from some brickworks that were being demolished. As far as the police were concerned they did not want to press the case. He (the Chief Constable) thought it was a case in which the defendants should he bound over to come up for judgment if called upon. Brierley's wife who purchased the iron from the defendant's at first denied all knowledge of the iron but subsequently admitted that. sl.e had bought it. The prosecutor raid he did not want to press the case. The Chairman in binding the defendants over for three months to come up for judgment if called upon, said be hcped it would be a warning to them. A POTATO TRASSACTION.—Robert Blanchard, Lloyd- street, was charged with stealing on July 24th, a load of potatoes value 45., the property of Richard Ormerod.— The Chief Constable explained that on the day in ques- tion Richard Ormerod of Bridge-street, Ashton, stored a quantity of potatoes in the Horse and Juckey yard. On his return from serving a customer he found one load valued at 4s. bad been stolen: The police were informed and it was ascertained that the potatoes had been sold at 12.30 the same cl 'yr to a Mrs. Scholtz, wife of Edward Scholtz, a chip potato dealer, of Winn ick- road. by the prisoner and another man for 4s. The prisoner carried the potatoes to the shop and said he could supply Mrs. Scholtz regularly. Detective Metcalf spoke to arresting prisoner who on being charged said "I had only three pints of beer out of the transaction." Prisoner was sentenced to 14 days' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>MoNDAY.—Before qe Mayor (chairman), Alderman Smethurst and Messrs. W. Roberts, S. Wright, H Thornton and G. Pilling. A VIOLENT PRISONER.—Ann Kimble, of Dolman's. Jane, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in that neighbourhood on Sunday, also with assaulting P.C. Gibbs and doing damage to P.C. Tolley's tunic. She pleaded " Not guilty.'—P.O. Tolley stated that about 5 o'clock on Saturday he heard the prisoner shouting and making use of obscene language. He ordered her into the house, but a few minutes after- wards she came out again and he took her into custody. He had to get the assistance of P.C. Gibbs to convey her to the Police Station on a handcart. On the way sbe tore witness's tunic and did damage to the extent of a shilling.—P.C. Gibbs said that prisoner run a pin into his finger twice.—Prisoner was fined 20s. and costs (21 us.) or 14 days. AN IMPUDENT Yourn.--John Hargreaves, a youth of 18, was charged with doing damage to a plate-glass window in Hamilton-street to the ext«nt et £3.—A constable stated that at About twelve o'clock on Satur- day he was in Hamilton-street when he heard•of crash- ing of glass.—Prisoner: You are a liar. You weren't there.—The Clerk : Be quiet. —Prisoner would not desist from interropting.—The Mayor (sternly) . Hargreaves, you must behave yourself.—Prisoner: I don't care.—He was then hurried down below. In the meanwhile the Chief Constable said it would be wise if the Court were cleared, as it was only bravado en the part of the prisoner.—The Mayor: A very good sug- gestion. Clear the Court !---After the Court had been cleared prisoner was again brought up and the constable proceeded with his evidence and stated that when he took prisoner into custody he threw himself on the ground and dared witness to lay his hands upon him. Later be commenced kicking and bahaved like a mad- man. With the assistance of a civilian witness succeeded in bringing prisoner to the Police Station.—The Chief Constable stated that prisoner had been fifteen times previously before the magistrates, the last time Leing a fortnight ago for stealing papers when he was sentenced to seven days' imprisonment. -Continuing, he said he regretted to say there was no encouragement for them to do anything for the prisoner. He was incorrigible.— Prisoner was sentenced to two months' imprisonment</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Messrs. H. Roberts (chairman), J. Rymer Young, C. Jackscn Holmes Ind Dr. Markel. Two MONTHS FOR THFFT.—John Rutter, a stranger, was charged with stealing a load of potatoes value 4s. 6d. from the Horse and Jockey yard on Saturday, July 240.—The Chief Constable stated that Richard Ormerud, of Ashton, left the potatoes in the Horse and Jockey yard, and on returning missed them. It was afterwards found that prisoner and another man (who</p>
        <p>was at present serving sentence) had sold them to a chip potato dealer. Prisoner absconded, but was arrested the previous night, and admitted the offence. Prisoner was assisting Ormerod.—A sentence of two months was passed. DIDN'T LIKE THE WORK.—George Taylor and John Berry, workhouse inmates, were each sentenced to 21 days with Lard labour for refusing to perform their task of breaking stones at the Union. STABBED wrrFt A HATPlN.—Margaret Morgan, of no settled abode was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Wcllfleld-street the previous day and also with assaulting P.C. Armstrong.—Armstrong stated that he was in Wellfleld-street the previous night when he saw the prisoner who was drunk and creating a dis- turbance. He spoke to bar and requested her to go away but she sat on the floor and asked him who he was. She then drew a hatpin from her bat and stabbed witness under the left eye. Witness then took her into custody.—Prisoner was fined ss. and costs or seven days.—Prisener " Thank you."</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. C. White (chairman), Alder- man Smethurst, Councillor Dr. Joseph and Mr. J. W. Hayward. MATRIMONIAL TROUBLES.—RaIph Hollingworth, a tiler, of Knusford-road, was sued by his wife for being an habitual drunkard, in consequence of which she asked for a separation. The defendant did not appear. Mr. A. Browne who appeared for complainant, stated that the case had been first adjourned for three months. During that time defendant bad been very seriously ill and had to he attended to by his wife. His conduct had been of course a little better and at defendant's own request the case was again adjournedandduring that Vme Mr. Firth (who was now on his holidays) had written a fetter saying that the first week after the second adjournment defendant's conduct was better,but during the second week his conduct was very bad. He had been abusive to him (Mr. Firth) when he called upon him. Mrs. Hollingworth took in lodgers and the wages she received from the defendant for the past four weeks were 55., 255., 22s , sad 9s. '1 here were four children to keep. The day after Walking Day defendant smashed a looking-glass and told cem- plainant that he knew bow to break windows and catch- ing hold of a 'broom troke four of the kitchen windows and then got a number of saucers and threw them down the yard. He had been in the Workhouse ten day with the delirium tremens. The complainant could not live it h him and would make her own living by taking in lodgers. He accordingly asked for a separation.—Mrs. Hollingworth said that during the eight weeks' adjournment defendant had been worse. Hefted grossly insulted Mr. Firth and herself and he had smashed her nose. Last Saturday night, she and her family bad to barricade themselves in their bedroom and the next morning defendant smashed a chair to matchwood. She could not live with him any longer. Ile had not gone to bed sober for six months. Defendant had often boasted that he had " bested " the magistrates and Mr. Browne. He also said he would not go near the Court. Complainant asked for a week. A separation order was granted, allowing complainant 15s. per week.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before Messrs. C. White (chnirman), W. Roberts, J. Rymer Young, W. T. Williams, and Dr. Naden.</p>
        <p>A LIKING FOR TOMATOES.—Richard Nicholson, a stranger, was charged with stealing it quantity of tomatoes, valued at 3d., the property of Robert Sutton, from his shop in Foundry-street—The Chief Constable ststed that P.C. Gilchrist was informed of the theft by Nlr. Sutton and given a description of the man. On coing round the mark( t he saw the plisoner and took him to Mr, Sutton uho identified him.—Mr. Sutton said the prisoner went into his shop at 3, Foundry- street, and in his presence took the tomatoes from the window and ran out. On being arrested, prisoner had eaten the tomatoes.—P.C. Gilchrist spoke to appre- hending prisoner who, on being charged with the offence, replied "Yes."—Prisoner was sentenced to 14 days' bard labour.</p>
        <p>SNt ART PENALTY.—Henry Statham, Orford-street. Warrington, was sent to gaol for six months with hard labour for improper conduct in Arpley-road, Wilson- Patten-street, and Queen's Gardens on various dates in June and July.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY.—Before the Mayor (chairman), Alderman Smethurst, and Messrs. A. J. Jolley, W. Furness, H. Thornton, and G. Pilling. NEIGHBOURS' QUARRELS.—Ellen Stockton, of Hale- street, was summoned by Edith Chadwick, also of Hale- ftreet, for assault.—Mr. A. Browne appeared for the complainant and said the assault was quite unwarrant- able. Mrs. Stockton threw a toy carriage at complainant sit hich luckily mis ed her. She then stripped up her sleeves and hit her in the left eye scratched her and pulled her hair.—Defendant said the bother was caused through Mrs. Chadwick's husband threatening her child with his belt.—Mr. Browne denied this.— Defendant was bound over to keep the peace for six months on two sureties ordered to pay the costs of the case (Bs. Cd).—Mary Broderick, 11, Philip-street, sum- moned Mary Foley, Philip-street. for assault. There was also a cross-summons.—Both parties were ordered to pay 103. each or seven days.</p>
        <p>THREATS.—Elizabeth Garmtt, 40, Warburton-street, was charged with using threats to Edith Green, 13, Antrobus-street, whereby she was afraid she would do her some bodily injury.—Plaintiff stated that she was walking down Market-street on Saturday night when the defendant went behind her and asked her what fun bhe had been having out of her on Walking Day. Plaintiff told her to go away as a crowd was g4thering round, and defendant then pulled her shawl off her head and began to threaten ber.—Defendant was bound over to keep the peace for six months with sureties and to pay ss. costs.</p>
