      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 14, 1909.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 14, 1909.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>THE SALE OF POISONS BY SEEDSMEN.</p>
        <p>Lively Debate.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council wts held at the Town Hall oa Tuesday evening under the presidency of the Mayor (Alderman Forshaw). There were also present : The Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), Aldermen W. Bolton, T. Burton, James Evans, and D. Tinnion ; Councillors T. H. Ashcroft, T. Birtles, J. Breaihurst, J. G. Craik,,J. Dickinson, J. Halliwell. Dr. Joseph, P. T. Lashford, J. Lord, J. Pemberton, 11. Roberts, W. Rogcrs, J. Sankey, H. Singleton, J. Skelland, T. S. Steel, J. Fulton-Smith, 0. Toole, G. N. Vernon, awl W. J. Wilson ; Messrs. J. L. Whittle (town cle:k), T. Longdin (borough surveyor), J. Dees (water engineer), W. S. Haddock (gas engineer) and F. V. L. Mathias (electrical engineer). THE LATE COUNCILLOR NIELD.</p>
        <p>Mr. ARNOLD NLELD wrote to the Mayor as follows: " On b(-half of .Mrs. Nield and familyi ask 30n to convey to tb6 Mayor and Council their great approcia- tion of the kind resolutlon passed by them, and their sincere thanks for symrathy generally extended to them."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LATE COUNCILLOR W. B. ROBERTS.</head>
        <p>THE LATE COUNCILLOR W. B. ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR reported to the Council the death of Councillor W. 13. Roberts. He said Mr. Roberts had not been a member of the Council very long, but be had shown great interest in municipal work and rendered valuable services to the committees on which he served. Mr. Roberts was a young man and had be lived he would, with his exceptional ability, have been a great help in the work of municipal government of this town. They regretted his decease and felt great sympathy fur his dr. idow end son. The Mayor concluded by moving a resolution placing on record the great loss which the borough has sustained by Councillor Roberts's death and express:ng deep and siucere sympathy with the widow and son.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROGERS seconded. Councillor FuLToN-SmiTH, in supporting, referred to to the super-abundance of good nature which char- acterized the late Councillor Roberts. Another beauti- ful trait in his character was his unostentatious charity. The number of good deeds he did, if known, would surprise a great many people. Alderman JAMES EVANS also associated himself with the vote of condolence. By the death of Councillor W. B. Roberts the Cour.cil had sustained a great loss. (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>The. resolution wits passed in silence, the tnemters of the Council standing. EDUCATION CD AIMITTEE. •</p>
        <p>Councillor Vernon uaa appointed a representative member on the Education Committee to fill the vacancy occasioned by the death of Councillor W. B. Robeita. The recommendation of the Education C3mmittee for the appointment of Mrs. Mary Elizabeth Holmes and Mr. Linnteus Greening as selectel members of that committee was also confirmed. ,</p>
        <p>SALE OF POISONS BY SEEDSMEN. BREEZY DISCUSSION. . _</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Health Committee, the Town Clerk reported that the Council at their meeting on the 13th Instant had referred I ack the minutes recording the refusal of the committee to grant licences.under the Poisons and Pharmacy Act, 1008. the committee again considered the applications, and resolved that the licences be not granted. Alderman JAMES EVANS proposed and Alderman BURTON seconded the adoption of the minutes. Alderman BOLTON thought they should have some explanation why the Health Committee had refused to grant the licences. He always understood that %Ilion the Council referred minutes back, it meant that what the committee had been doing was wrong and that they bad now to do something that was right. He moved,as an amendment, that the minutes be not confirmed. Councillor .ROBERTS : If the Council confirm tha minutes of the committee, how will my resolution, of hich I have given notice, to grant licences to Messrs. George Starkey and Thomas Pierpoint, stand? The Maim?. : It would put your motion out of order if these minutes were confirmed. Councillor ROBERTS said that in that case he would second the amendment of Alderman Bolton. He held no brief for anybody, and the only question which Ni-a-ighed with him was that of simple justice and fair play. He did not attribute in any degreb any improper motives to the committee in refusing ' tho e licences bat he d:d say that they had misconstruei the intention and effect of the Pharmacy Act of 1908. Councillor Roberts then went into details with regard to the history of the question, and con- tended that the Act of 1938 was really an enaqing Act which granted the right to seedsmen under the super- vision of the local authority to sell insecticides, weed killers, etc. As to the question of possible danger to the public, he said there were many people employed in chemists' shops, and who sold goods over the counters, who were not qualified pharmaceutical chemists. Precisely the some conditions had to be observe 1 by seedsmen and chemists in regard to the keeping and sale of the poisonous materials. The danger wae.not in, the purchase itself, and there was no more safeguarding iof the individual in getting it from a chemist's shop. There were very few people who would commit suicide by drinking sheep dips, insecticides, or weed killers. (Laugher.), They went for more d.ngerous poisons in a much smaller compass at chemists' shops. The Council should further consider the convenience of the public. He had had a garden for 20 years, and he hid never bought an insecticide or a weed killer except from a seedsman. He had never dreamt of going to a chemist's shop. The seedsmen were the people wilo had established the track., and had had the trade up to now. The pharmaceutical chemists had never had the trade, but they were trying to get it, and they were trying to 11R0 their powerful monopoly to the disadvan- tage of smaller tradesmen who had not the same back- ing. Up to now no fewer than 205 Councils and health authorities had granted the licences to tradesmen. As an act of simple justice and fair play to the two trades- men they should be allowed by-licence to retain a trade tahich they themselves had created.</p>
        <p>Alderman floTrt•l,7 : is granting a monopoly that I object to.</p>
        <p>Alderman BURTON said the committee did not intend their sympathy to overrun their duty in this matter By granting more licences for the sa,le of these deadly poisons they wera making it easier for people to disstroy not only verminous or weed life but als ) human life. It was ft.r !lime raasons the Council refused to grant the licences. There were saffi;ient chemists in the town to Apply the poisons.</p>
        <p>Councillor tO AP: asked if there was any record of the number of suicides in Warrington which could be attributed to the purchase of poisans from seedsmen. Councilor ASHCROFT thought the committee had ignored the Council. Alderman JAmi:i EVANS said the committee had given the matter most serious and lengthy consideration largely on the lines Alderman Burton bad indicated. Under the Act the committee had a right to sly whether aiiple provision was made by the chemists for the needs of the community in regivd to the sale, of these pois nous materials.. The committee were s itis- fled en that point. At the same time they all syma- thised With the parsons who would be debarred from the sale; the committee were simply safeguarding the public interests. If, however, fresh reasons were given to the committee, they would reconsider their posit,ion. At present he had heard nothing fresh. , Cotincillor ROBERTS: It is very easy to say th it. Aldera an EVANS : I belie eit is true. Councillor RORERTS : Yes, but nobody else does. Alderman EVANS You traversed the same ground largely at the last Council meeting. Councillor ROBERTS: I did not. Alderman EVANS : I have heard no new relsons yet, from the standpoint of the public safety, why these licences should be granted. Councillor Dr. JOSEPH said that Alderman Bolton had asked why the committee dared to come back with the same resolution, and in reply to that he (Dr. Joseph) said they could not alter their convictions. The mnjority of the comrniteee who voted in favour of the vexed resolutions had strong convictions, and he thought they bad good reasons for those convictions. Ile held no brief for anyone, and if he had consulted his own personal everyday feeling he should have voted in favour of tire two gentlemen having the licences They were worthy respectable gentlemen whom he bad known for Many years. He wished to be no party to the responsibility of passing a measure that might in the future be calculated to lie dangerous. To make the sale of poisons easy was dangerous and pernicious, to his mind* The object of the Act was to restrict the sale and m.•ke it more difficult to obtain the poisons. Amenie could be got in one-pound packets which might poison dozens of people. It was an innocent-looking powder, pieasant to taste, and that was why it was coloured blue,but to his mind for the sake of additional safety it ought to be coloured red. (Rear, hear). Councillor ROBERTS: Do you suggest seriously that it is possible to buy one-pound packets of this danger- ous poison? because T am going absolutely to con- tradict it.</p>
        <p>Councillor Dr. JOSEPH : Well, that is as I under- stand it, although I am not a sheep farmer. Contiuu- ing the doctor said he thought over the matter in com- mittee and out of it, and he was quite convinced, know- ing as he did how things were carried on, that the committee were right in the action they were ta'ting. His father was a chemist, and he was brought up in the air of poisons, and he had an idea of the dangers that used to exist.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION sad the committee contene.ei that they had charge of the health of the inhabitants and it was from that point of view they acted, more particularly on the advice of Dr. Joseph and their own medical officer of health, and, of course, on the explana- tions which had been given by the Deputy Town Clerk. They didmot wish poisons to be sold as it were indis- crirnanately or without that due precaution which would be exorcised in a chemists' shop. It was on these grounds that the matter had ben considerel and he did not see that any hardship had been done. They wera not creating a powerful monopoly. The chemists already had a powerful monopoly—and justly so because the sale of poisons should be a mlnoooly that WBF, at any rate, in the hands of men who would exercise it with care and judgment. Conncillor CRAIK thought the Health Committee had taken a right view of the case. Chemists had to pass their examinations, and take their diplomas, and it was owing to their knowledge of poisons that the advan- tage of restricting the sale came is. A leading chemist had told him that he had known of a case where a woman asked for sheep dip to put on her child's head If she had used it in that way the child would have been raving mad in an hour. Councillor FULTON-SMITH said that if they went to Garston any day of the week they would see no end of sheep dip and loads of arsenic on tire quay unprotected. There was no restriction and that made it all the more difficult for him, not being an expart,'to know what was the best thing to be done in the circumstances. He was convinced that if a num was determined to commit suicide he would get some poison to carry out his end. Councillor STEEL thought the licences might he granted to seedsmen under conditions which. would 'sufficiently protect the public. Councillor SKELL AND supported the action of the Committee on the ground that they ought to make it easy to do right and hard to do wrong. Councillor VERNON supported the amendment and said he did not see that there was any: more danger in an unqualified person selling poison in a seed shop than in a chemist's shop. Poisons were sold by unqualified persons. _</p>
        <p>" Alderman TINNION : In Warrington. Councillor VERNON: Yes. I could name one case in Warrington.</p>
        <p>Councillor SINGLETON said he used more sheep dip than all the members of the Council combined. Ile had known dogs who had had a belly full of the sheep dip and it had not poisoned them. Some of the remarks which had been made that evening about the matter were all rubbish.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD pointed out that a certain informa- tion had been received from the Privy Council, and he thought the matter might be further considered by the Health Committee in the light of that communication. Alderman TINNION euggested that Councillor Roberts should withdraw his motion with reference to granting licences to Messrs. Starkey and Pierpoint. The com- mittee might bring forward certain propositions which would meet with the acceptance of the Council. If Councillor Roberts was not satiEfied he could give notice next month.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: I shall not sit down under a condition of things in which the chemists of Warring- ton will get a monopoly of the plums while the stones are left to the seedsmen. If the Health Committee will give an assurance that in the reconsideration of the matter they will do justly by all the parties. Councillor Dr. JOSEPH : That word justly has come out to often. We don't mean any. injustice to anybody. Councillor ROBERTS: I am going to say what I feel and what I think about the matter. (Cries of "Vote.") The question put to me was whether I would withdraw the notice.' lam stating conditions. (" Vote.") You don't close my mouth in that way Dr. Joseph, don't you fancy it for a moment. If the Council IS willing to listen to my conditions—(Cries )f "Chair.") I am not abusing the privileges of the chair. Councillor PESIBERTON : I think RS the Council has asked Councillor Roberts if he will w.ithdraw, he has a right to say on what conditions he will withdraw. Councillor ROBERTS: I did not raise the question as to whether the motion should be discussed or not. The question was raised by Alderman Tinnion and put to</p>
        <p>me as a question ani lam replying to that. If it is not convenient for Alderman Tinnion to hear it, I cannot help it.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION: It is not convenient to sit here, as at two previous meetings, and here at least twenty separate speeches from Councillor Roberts. Councillor ROBERTS: You are one of those individuals wham Winston Churchill described as guilty of terminological inexactitudes. When the Mayor says I am out of order I am willing to sit down. At present am trying, in spite of a good deal of opposition and interruption, to give the conditions under which I am prepared to withdraw my notice. Councillor Dr. JOSEPH : Don't impute motives. Councillor ROBERTS: You talk like a lot of children who don't know their own minds. On the amendment being put to the meeting it was passed by 13 votes to 7.</p>
        <p>_ • Councillor ROBKRTS then withdrew his notice of motion.</p>
        <p>BOWLING GREENS.</p>
        <p>At a meetin; of the Town Itall, Parks and Baths Committee, the Town Clerk submitted th 3 following resolution from the Sites and Buildings Sub-Committee of the Education Committee on the re3olution p5553d by this Committee at their last meeting with reference to the land known as "Graces' Pottery," Winwick-road: "Resolved, that in view of the overcrowded stote of the schools in this district, the Town Council be requestel to reserve the land in question for the purposes of a public Element ary school, and that they be also asked to acquire any addition tl adjoining land that may be necessary for that purpose." It was resolved that the Town Clerk be instratztel when negotiating for addi- tional adjoining land, to include Ettlicient land for the purpose of placing two howling greens there. Councillor TOOLE pointA out that the Government Inspector did not consider the site a very good one for a school. Something ought to be done with the land, which was a disgrace in its pre3ent c audition. The MAYott : Samethitig ought to be &amp;me with it certainly. , ,</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON considered that the wishes of the Council in regard to the provision of additional bowling greens were being sA at d filnce, and it was very improper. • Alderman BOLTON said that as soon as the committees which had the possession of land would turn it over to the Town Hall, Parhs, and Baths Committee, they would proceed with the provision of bowling greens. The minutes were approved. THE PRICE OF GAS.</p>
        <p>The Gal Committee resolved that the price of gas be reduced by id. per 1,000 cubic feet throughout the ares 'of supply as f ram the Ist of November nmt. The Assistant Secretary reported fully upon the proposed system of discount to slot meter consumers, and it was resolved that an additional 5 cubic feet of gas per penny be allowed to slot meter consumers within the Borough, and that the meter wheels be altered accordingly,; also that a proportion- ate additional quantity of gas per penny be allowed to slot meter consumers outside the Borough, and that the meter wheels be altered accordingly. Ciuncillor BROADHURST contended that the conces- sion to slot meter users ought, according to the previous resolution of the Council, to have taken effect twelve months ago. Would the slot meter users receive the benefit of the additional penny reduction? The TOWN CLERK slid that last year the committee reduced the price of gas t ordinary consumers by 2d. per 1,000 and this yrar they were reducing it by another penny, making 3d. The concession to slot meters was equivalent to a reduction of 5,11. per 1,000 and the meter wheels were being alte7ecl so that the consumers would ge t the benefit immediately.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said there was a great deal of dissatisfaction among the blot meter users who nnmbere.d 10,000. Councillor LORD asked how many feet of gas per penny the slot meter users would receive as compared with the ordfhary consumers? Mr. HOWARD (ttsiistant secretary) said the present quantity *as about 38ft. per penny for ordinary con- sumers and 33ft. for slot meter users. The minutes were approved. THE BRIDGES QUESTION.</p>
        <p>_ The General Purposes Committee resolved that the Bridges Sub-committee be dissolved.</p>
        <p>Ttie MAYOR movel and Alderman BOLTON seconded, the adoption of the minutes. Councillor PEMBERTON pointed out that the General Purposes Committee as a whole would now form the Bridges Sub-committee. Alderman Smethurst had taken an enormout amount of time and trouble with regard to the bridges question and it would be a pity if he ceased to be chairman of the committee which dealt with the question. He (Councillor Pemberton) was sure the Mayor would not wish for a moment to take from Alderman Smethurst any office he was entitled to. The MAYOR: I had not the slightest idea of that. The TOWN CLERK pointed out that the matter could be -dealt with in committee and the minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>• ~:, .. ' ott It %%. . - • ,`. 3- • ...4; • •• t. -- .1.: • Y..i.ii • • o•.: CI 11 a 9 ' 1/46. 1 '.-- 4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>POLICE AND THE ROYAL VISIT'</head>
        <p>THE POLICE AND THE ROYAL VISIT'</p>
        <p>The Watch Committee resolved :—"That this com- mittee place-on record their entire satisfaction and appreciation of the arrangements made by the Chief Constable on the occasion of the King's visit to War- rington."</p>
        <p>the minutes were proposed by the MAYOR and seconded by Alderman SmETHURST.</p>
        <p>Couneith;r SINGLEtON said this resolution was all very well in its way, but what about the men? At both Liverpool and Manchester the men had been recognized tor the extra services they had done. Tie MAYOR: They have hid an extra allowance and have teen thaiked by the Reception Committee. The minutes weie adopted. MESSRS. 'MILLING'S SKY SIGN.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Street Improvement and Lighting Committee, on July 15th, it was resolved, that Messrs. Milling and Co. Ltd. be Eervea with a notice to remove the sky sign from their premises in Horsemarket- street. At a subsequent meeting an application from Messrs. Henry Milling and Co. Ltd., for permission to allow the sky sign over their premises to remain, was read. It was resolved that the sign be permitted to remain for one year only on payment of the sum of 21.0. Councillor ROGERS thought it was most unfair to Messrs. Milling, who were old established tradesmen,to attempt to force them to pay £lO a year for the privilege of having a sky sign on their own premises. He moved that the minutes be not confirmed.</p>
        <p>Councillor BIRTLF.S seconded. It was -it---great hard- ship on the firm after being permitted to erect the sihn in the first instance- The TOWN CLERK: No. no. Councllor BIRTLES : Well it was put up. He con- tended that £5 would be ample. Councillor PEMBERTON said the proper procedure on the part of those wbo objected would be to alter the byelaw s which the sign infringed. The sign was erected without the consent of the Corporation but he believed it was done in ignotance that any byelaw was being broken, and the committee went out of their way to a11..w the:u to leave the sign up for twelve months without payment. At the end of twelve months it had not been taken down. If the Council were going to allow one tradesman to have such a sign, how could they prevent others ? While the byelaw remained as it was, they hid n) option in the matter.</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK: It is not a bye-law, but the erection of sky signs is prohibited by law. It is illegal and further you Cave,pawsed a resolution that no sky signs beyermitted in Warrihgton, _</p>
        <p>-Councillor PEM BERTON eoniended that the Cgmmittee had treated Messrs. Milling most rersonably and had met them_in every way po.ssi.hle. _</p>
        <p>• Councillor CRAIK said that when the application came before the Committee the sign had already been erected in ignorance. Messrs. Milling said that if the Committee would allow it to remain for twelvemonths, they would he willing to take it down-on the expiration of th .t period. The present resolution made it look as if people could break the law with impunity for the sum of £lo._</p>
        <p>Councillor SINGLETON considered that the firm were being unpstly treated. Com-101110r Dr. JOSEPH was of opinion that sinh an advertisement as the sky s ga afforda I ought to be worth £loa year. Al&amp;rman JAMESEVANS maintainedadvertising I hdaetv the sign wh oc which Messrs. Milling's premises was not as great a source of tdhaengCeorunac lo as many Inalan‘yred. other They permitted hoardings to be erected in front of people's property, and they were an eye sore and a disgrace. The TOWN CLERK said the Act of Parliament which the Corporation obtained in 1899 prohibited the erection of sky signs, and the Council could not consent to them. They might, however, abstain from taking pro- ceedings for an infringement of the law, ili stations could be erected if the Council consented to them, but sky signs were absolutely prohibited. The £lO to be paid by Messrs. Milling was in tbe nature of a penalty, in consideration of the Corporation abstain- ing from taking proceedings. Councillor FULTON-SMITH : You have condoned an offence against the law. The TOWN CLERK : It is not a felony. Alderman JAMES EVANS : I want the condoning to be continued. We ought to help and not to injure anything in the way of advertising, within reasonai)le limits, especially by local</p>
        <p>Alde'rEDAß TIN-NION said the question was whether the sign was dangerous. If it was safe—and he believed it was—he thought the firm ought to pay something and he believed they would be willing to do so. But if it was dangerous, the Corporation had no right to allow it on any consideration. Alderran BOLTON said it was an illegal act and the Council had no right to recognize it. If the firm had applied for permission they would no doubt have been allowed to erect something in a modified form. Councillor ROBERTS said the Council had not always carried tout its duties in the spirit which Alderman Bolton referred to. It was not many weeks since one of the Committees sanctioned an encroach- ment which was up to this day certainly only there by permission of the Coincil and was capable of being removed on notice. lie susgested that this particular clause of the Act of Parliament should be reconsidered. The Council had power in the wrong direction. If they had got power absolutely to prohibit hoardings it might have been used to great advantage because the hoardings one saw—not particularly in Warrington but in many places—were a far grelter sourco of possible danger than a sky sign erected over the premises of the owner and secured in such a way as to be without the slightest possible pre- judice to the public. The committee Irld acted abso- lutely illogically.</p>
        <p>Coitncilror P-EMBERTON : The committee did not smction the sky sign. It was put up without their sanction.</p>
        <p>Councillor RoßEnrs: Councillor Pemberton has spoken once upon this question and he is making an absolute mis-statement of fact. 'his resolution proves that the committee sanctioned the sky sign. The Towtq CLERK read the resolution of August 11th, 1908, which was to the effect tint no sky sign he allowed to be erected from that date and that on Messrs. Milling's undertaking to remove the sign when called upon permission he granted for it. to remain for a period not exceeding twelve months. Councillor LORD said they must either insist on the sign being removed or get the Act altered and treat everybody alike. Personally he did not think they ought to interfere with sky signs, ,especially when they had been erected, but if they allowed one firm to put up a sign they must allow everyone. The voting was as follows : For the amendment: Aldermen Tinnion and James Evans; Councillors Steel, Sankey, Vernon, Skelland, Ashcroft, Wilson,lTalliwell, Lord, Pemberton, Roberts, Birtles, Singleton, Broadhurst and Rogers. Against: Alderman Burton, Councillors Dr. Joseph Craik and Lash ford. The amendment was therefore carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a fair attendance on S.inday last. Mr. D. McEachern presided and spoke upon "The great love of the Father," which, he said, was better than the idea of a stern and punishing father. Mr. W. H. Wallington gave the address upon "Man, his powers and his relation- ship to God." Man had great powers, great thoughts, great love, compassion and helpfulness. His goodness forbad him giving to a needy brother a stone for bread, bat he would give him of his best bread. So God gave that whicii far transcended the highest in man. Man received all his highest from God.—Mr. Payton read the lesson and Mr. W. Besxvick led the prayer with special reference to Mr. Gresty's great trial and pain. The hymns were joined in &amp;artily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>"The Sermon on the Mount" in Nationa</head>
        <p>"The Sermon on the Mount" in Nationa</p>
        <p>Life.</p>
        <p>In the course of the Rev. Thomas Miller's discourse on Sunday morning in Bewsey-road Church on "The sermon on the mount" he said that if that principle were carried out it would be for the good of the nation, and not as some had said to its detriment. Whatever a Christian might be in politics he must follow that principle. The aim of the nation should be the realisa- tion of brotherhood. There must be the recmnition that those distinctions of life were imaginary, just as the line of the Equator was imaginary. Racial and national distinction were only imaginary, because we were all the children of God. The policy of the last few weeks in reg ird to armaments wai discreditable to a nation in the 20 11 century. When the nations realised that the principle of the sermon on the mount was what was intended by Christ we should have a more saner policy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>" The Angelus."</head>
        <p>" The Angelus."</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. C. Letheren continued his cpurse of sermons on Millet's great picture" The Angelus "at Golborne-street Baptist Church on Sunday evening , when his special subject was " The bospel of the Commonplace." Io the course of his sermon Mr. Letheren asked the congregation to consider the Servi- tude of the Commonplace. Was it not a complaint common to all claszes that life was one dulloleadly round of monotonous duties and trivial tasks? Duty was such a humdrum affair and rich people found the round of pleasure as little satisfying as the poor the treadmill of labour and must over be seeking some new thine, only to find that there was nothing new under the Sun. The second principle was to consieer the Spirituality of the Commonplace. Commonplace:mess was not a complaint that they could eseape by running away from it. Men thought that the remedy for it wes travel and °hinge, but wherever one went he carried it with him. It was only the seeing eye and the responsive heart that recognized the romance, and tragedy, and adventure, and conifliet that made up their common life, it was admitted in this sphere that it was in portraying commonplace people and things that literature and art had achieied their greatest triumphs. The boots that lived as litera- ture were those that were truest to life. A noble writer once said that the heart of Leing happy was the art of discovering the depths that lay in the daily common things. Great pleasure might be rare, said another writer, but it was the enormous variety of minor pleetsures that made up the happiness of life. The last principle to consider was the sanctific%tion of the commonplace. It 'MIS reserved for sanctification to bring to men the supreme secret which when they had learnt it, would make it impossible to regard any life as commonplace. It was in that sphere of the common place tIAt sanctification lived and had its being. Why did God select from among women to be His mother the wife of a village carpenter, and for His training through thirty silent years a place of which men said. " Can any good thing come out of Nazareth." Were net the simple parables of Christ the very highest example of the art of extracting greatness and grandeur out of common things? In conclusion, the rev, gentleman said they were endowed with such spiritual powers and capabilities that there was no single experience of theirs that was not invested with supreme significance; no detail of their daily life that would not influence their eternal destiny.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>COMPENSATION CLAIM SETTLED.</head>
        <p>COMPENSATION CLAIM SETTLED.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, on Wednesday, Mr. W. Hutchen appeared for Mrs. Isabella Jackson, of Craighead-road, Blantyre, Lanarkshire, to ask for £2O down and £1 a week out of £7O which had been paid by the Coffin's Green Company in respect of the death of her husband. The applicant was nineteen years of age, and had a child nine months old. It was alleged that the man was injured on April 19th, and be died on May 3rd. It was a question whether the widow was entitled to anything, but the matter had been settled for VO. His Honour granted the order asked for.</p>
      </div>
