        <p>t tha 'The members of the abave Society assembled British Workman Public House, Horsemarket-stree,4o-e Monday, the occasion being the 38th anniversary o' was establishment of the Society. The attendance by exceedingly good, the meeting being presided eyecup- ti.e President (Mr. Frederick Massey) who ported by the Vice-president (Mr. William treasurer, Mr. James Parr ; trustee, Mr. Iv 1"„--;0,1 Eckersley ; auditor, Dr. Joseph Adams; the ge°'' secretary, Mr. Lawrence Weir, and others. jest Tbe'CitAirimAN in his opening address said the was time he had the privilege of holding that position fp, in 1900, the c 'pita' at that time being £2,140 25- of Since that period of nine years the progreldej the Sociegy hid been wonderful. They had, aut is to the funds no less than £1,749 19s. 51°' -bof spoke volumes with regard to the financial positioo the the Scniety. During the year he was sorry to sra death claims had been heavier than in previous Yebe3,o But notwithstanding the amounts paid they had Os' able to place to the credit of the Society a very saod factory and substantial gain, which he felt sure be appreciated by all present. treir) The GENERAL SECRETARY (Mr. Lawrence • &amp;bay presented his annual balance sheet, which showed the Society's funds now amounted to £3,90 25. :419 as against £3,681 198. 3d., the previous year, 3,r551 of £205 2s. 104 d. The numerical condition srea_,",` rt as compared with 1510 the previous year. The 'tb further showed that the Society had paid away in ue claims £lO9 78. as against £lO4 the previous year, sou that they had invested in the 'Corporation toupoly mortgage close upon £4,000. The Secretary ia „"der elusion briefly pointed out the advantages gainei u ffe-oto their newly constructed table of benefits and thee they had upon the class it was intended to resSiP Several claims had bean paid for 25 years' member; sit during the year and the manner they had been ue with had belM well appreciated. The auditors Messrs. Peter Edgar and Joseph Ada% reported as to the correctness of the aCCOnntS 5 various books. deo Mr. William Clegg was unanimously elected pees and Mr. James Gillooley vice-president for tbe,Y;iii Mr. David Harris being elected auditor in ,place or was Peter Edgar whose resignation through ill-heatht be received by the members with very much regrue2oet having been the auditor for upwards of 20 years wit a single break. A vPy interesting programme was gone through (120,1 ing the evening consisting of matter effecting the geat'oek welfare of the Society. The following member',:iliani part in the discussion :—Messrs. Thomas Miller. W,..jey Massey, Joseph Ashton, John Jones, William Eeie'" Joseph Mather, Thomas Tirnperley, David Harris. . A vote of thanks was accorded to the retir ed e president, Mr. Frederick Massey, for the abl f the courteous manner he had conducted the business 0 Society during his term of office. ~ left tb° Mr. FREDERICK MASSEY in response said ee - 00 se office with much regret, indeed the work had be pleasure throughout.</p>
