      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SPANISH PRETENDER.</head>
        <p>THE SPANISH PRETENDER.</p>
        <p>BUDGET PROTEST FIASCO. en. Most wit hout exception the public meetings held in n,,:parvonshire during the last few weeks to protest 07:inst the Budget have proved a fiasco, there being an Ofrhelming preponderance at the meetings in favour I„ts„Proposals. brtn"2349gor, Llandudno, and Criccieth the opposition Ost marked, whilst as Carnarvon the meeting was tahr°ll6d because scarcely a dozen Conservatives -**td uP,</p>
        <p>Don Sainte de Bourbon, who succeeds his father, Dan Carlos, a 3 the Pretenier to the Spanish throne, has issued a manifesto to the Carlist party arid a praclarnation announzing his claim to the plaoe at present filled by King Alfonso. Dm Jahn e, waose edu:atiori has been mainly English, is held by many Frew-Amen to be a midi better Barba than the Duke of Orleans and if there was ever any ilea of a restaratioa of the tawarzhy on the Other side of the 'Straits of D3ver there are those W:11 waal I tura to D )1 Jain altha agh he legal title undoubtedly rests with the Orleans family. te, Thus Dan Jaime may cone day and himself fighting for two thrones at one and the same time.</p>
        <p>PENSIONER'S PROBLEM. Richard Jones (72), an Army pensioner, applied to the Stockport magistrates yesterday for a maintenance order obtained by his wife in 1886 to be varied. He stated that his wife received- the old ago pension.— Jones said he had paid his wife ss. a week out of an income of 'lss. 6d., and had also to contribute Is. a a week to a friendly society. He was thus left with 9s. 6d. a week out of which he had to pay 9s, for his board.—The Dench refused the application.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 14, 1909.</p>
        <p>What music so cheering As that of the chase? THE RHYMING SIGN-POSTS.</p>
        <p>The rhyming sign-posts, too, are his. A pathetic interest attaches to them as we remember that they were the amusement of his days of blindness. Through the closing years of his long life he was totally blind and his last volume of verses, "Arley in idleness," contains the fruits of his thinking through the darkened 'years; one of them a sonnet which may be read beside that of Milton on a like sad theme. We read the sign-posts with a better interest as we remember the genial soul whose cheery spirit rose above his loss of sight. No cartway save on suffrance here, For horse and foot the road is clear To Lymm, High Legh, Hoo Green and Mere. And again This road forbidden is to all, Unless they Wend their way to call At Mill or Green or Arley Hall. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>If proof were needed that literaryigenius is contagious it might be found in the fact that the old Squire's housekeeper took to litera- ture and made herself famous as the compiler of a directory and a good cookery book. And who knows how much more good literature may have budded forth under the encourage- ment of those attractive finger posts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <p>''</p>
        <p>SUMMER PILGRIMAGES.</p>
        <p>ARLEY : THE LAND OF THE RHYMING FINGER-POSTS.</p>
        <p>"Your Warrington Chapters are admir- able," writes a correspondent, "especially to beguile our wet Saturday afternoons. But now that summer has come why keep us to the town? Why not give Warrington an elastic interpretation, and take us out to see the beauties and antiquities of the open-air." Some "Summer Pilgrimages" have long been simmering, but we were waiting—well for the Summer. Bright days have come at last. So here's away,.*</p>
        <p>Cheshire is the cyclist's elysium. Turn your cycle loose and it will turn as by an instin et to the rollered roads and leafy lanes. This time we are going to Arley, the land of the rhyming finger posts; a place where Cheshire romance and Cheshire beauty blend in happy harmony. Not the least commendation of a trip to Arley is that the cyclist can reach it by a road unspoiled as yet by .the motorist and his tribe. Yes, there is such a road, and the pilgrim who would find it has but to dip thro ugh the dingle from the old London-road and follow the finger posts that point to Arley, arrested by the first that blossoms into poetry, Nofroad except on suffrance here, etc. We dismount and turn to the right into the private road. Across the broad expanse of park we get our first glimpse of Arley Hall, a fine example</p>
        <p>Photo 'forge. ONE OF THE RHYMING- SIGN-POSTS. No cartway save on suffrance her!) - For horse and foot the road is clear To Lymm, High Legit, 1100 Green and Mere.</p>
        <p>of domestic architecture. Many memories cluster round it, far too many for the half- day cyclist, and we must gives him just enough of them to make the house interest- ing without imposing an antiquarian weari- ness.</p>
        <p>, , , ‘ • ~, , ~," *k.•*".f•rig2lllPl/4",N, ‘,,,, : . XII .4 t . •''''''.:-..7t-....- 4 . 4.4 41i.i.A..-.1..;.'.- ."•'.*;'4.i''') . ,". i.' I, . 7 ~..;•47*-::4.:. z, lii.,:tf."-41.'''' s $4 .',,';',.: ;;;,),,.,_..,..itir,V,:, ":",:i.;,;t1,, ~.0,11. , ‘S.,` ....,:, . * • 6.__WAY.W.t,r4.7:Siell. ':e:,: A— .... ‘:• „ 4104k,.,4_,k;i:. &amp;t.:1,•:',..:54R4-4,1:;:„:. slialit, -. , .',..„ :.":;•!i':,!...14,,,',.,.1', .155*. t',. ':*'::'.,, -,', ....-,. s:,''' -:Y1'• ',:f.,:-.:*:.:'-5.;::.`.14,--:::,',. , .-.#4,1*3*.t: .„,,,,4p_..,,-,,f.5i:::,,,,, ;:•,-.,. : ~:,::,...., '::. „„;,,,,,,,A,,,,,:.....„„:„.,,,..„,,,..„ ~..or‘ ...„,...„.„,„..„..„.•,,,,-,..:.. :.., ~:,:...,. ~,,,, t:'107%,•••:‘.. tt,..-i !.:..* ~.Vi 'i:,.' ,• :7 42,i.,4„1,,......,., ,ii ...:,-..f : ! ~i,,,:,'• it•'•:',7l7-V..,•‘ ~ .*.,.•‘• ‘,:•.';, It'L'srsiffeff...Wkikt. „ .°'s . ',...!*'''''''„,;•...---, -' 4.t.V.-.. ~ - - (*-ANA-• ~M,;:::*1 ~. 9 ... 4,4:,.-4,.... -.740e. 'l* '.*-1';e;.•-' '''•,;” *.''''i4,.. ;:''' ~..14,1'. . ,',1,1'". ' .e• . .',.. 't.kc..'*; .., '7,.: '..', •''.V)4;::-;'..lZ-AztT. i.'-...4-i. . .'; v._ .... -.:ii.:40!.„ .!-:::,.,i,,.. --,...,,,..:A• -..-., ,2,,Ni.,,,1.,t,., ~::, - -, :•'%:. --; ' ~...-1'"..::;070::;:4;,:;4N'':'''.•;!::::Z.?:M71,.*4-Thj-' :: ~4,e4ii.,,,,,,,.?.,:''';'. - • • ...,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>WARBURTONS OF ARLEY.</head>
        <p>THE WARBURTONS OF ARLEY.</p>
        <p>The Warburtons of Arley are one of the oldest of Cheshire families. In early days they made their home at Budworth, but migrated thence to 'Warburton the town founded by Ethelfleda on the banks. of the Mersey and not far from Warrington. In the reign of Henry VII., Piers W arburton, tired of the flooding of the river, removed to Arley. This Piers was quite a personage in his day and took a hand in the stirring events of Bosworth Field when Richard 111. came so sadly to grief. This was not the only memorable field on which a Warburton played a part, for the old ballad that recounts the doings on Flodden field tells how Trustye Trafford kene to trye And wighty Warburton out of Cheshire All came with the Earl of Derbye. _</p>
        <p>The Warburton thus sung was Sir John, a son of the worthy Piers. The old family ngme, we ought to say, was Dutton, but on settling at Warburton they took the name of the place. If the pilgrim can endure the mention of one exact date we may say that it was in 1469 that Piers Warburton chose Arley as his new abode. And he chose well.</p>
        <p>Vre take our stand as near as can be to the hall and look around. Three hundred wooded acres surround us and charming vistas stretch on every hand. Between the trees Arley Pool glistens in the sun and as we survey its placid waters it would seem that every charm of nature, save that of the mountains, clusters here to give us welcome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>HALL.</head>
        <p>THE HALL.</p>
        <p>The ancient hall was of wood and quad- rangular in shape, surrounded of course by the usual moat. After nearly three centuries its beauty was completely spoiled by a casing of common bricks. There arose at the beginning of last century an Egerton-Warburton (the male line had become extinct in 1813 and a female branch of the family came to possession) whose good taste could not endure the ugly pile and who demolished the hall and made a fresh beginning. After nine years of labour which- began in 1833 the present dignified hall was complete. But like all men who engage to build he felt ere the completion, the cares and pains of buildership. In a very pretty sonnet he wrote: Before my hall I stood :_with.sated eye</p>
        <p>And hea‘Ty heart upon the pile I gazed, - Which care and cost, and years of toil had raised From turret's base to glittering vane on high. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Cold critics came. well pleased. its faults to spy, .--•- • -</p>
        <p>Those very faults w-hich smooth-tongue-d- flattery praised While country folk stood silent and amazed, " All "my heart whispered, " All vanity." _ _</p>
        <p>But the-worst canie out in some ()flier lines Who thinks himself he can his house erect Employs a noodle for his architect. This self-disparagement, notwithstanding, the trained eye finds much to admire in the stately home of Arley. It is not a show place though the suite of rooms which fill the south front are noble chambers and the library with its autographs and rare volumes is a place to linger in, especially if the visitor has access to the family charters with their glimpses of eight centuries of family history. The R. E. Egerton-Warburton who built the hall merits some further mention here, for his spirit hovers over the modein Arley. Born in 1804 he lived to a generous old age. Widely read and scholarly, he was a poet of some distinction, and being endowed—as we suppose a Cheshire squire should—with a love of the field, his sport inspired his earliest verses. And there are few hunting verses in the language so stirring: The dew drop is clinging To whin-bush and brake, The -skylark is singing "Morrie hunters, awake." Resounds the glad hallo; The pack scents the prey, • Man and horse follow— Away! Hark, away! Away! never fearing, e'er slacken ywr pace;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>GARDENS.</head>
        <p>THE GARDENS.</p>
        <p>The gardens have peculiar charm. Laid out in many styles they are divided by hedges or walls. Most to our fancy is the fine vista of clipped yew trees stretching to the distant park, and the sunken bowling green screened around with a dignified hedge of the same trees. Even here we cannot escape the poet or his song, for he buried his mare in the garden and gave her a worthy epitaph:</p>
        <p>Restored Co life were Marigold illy ;flare, 'Mid all the flowers which deck this garden fair, Not one in beauty could with her compare. I laid hr bones beneath the greenwood tree,</p>
        <p>And we-pt like childhood o'er my A.B.C.</p>
        <p>Near the- hall we visit (bp permission) a large barm, used now as a riding school. Massive oak trunks reared from the ground and pegged together at the roof form the structure and the beams are still sound and strong though they have borne the stress of the storms of at least five centuries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>THE. VILLAGE GREEN.</head>
        <p>THE. VILLAGE GREEN.</p>
        <p>But we have not done with Arley when we have seen the Hall,and indeed to those whose love of nature is greater than their love of antiquities, the best is yet to be. Passing round the park, we make our way to Arley Mill and Arley Green, and rest fora moment under the broad oak that shelteks the northern end of the green. A true toulist would have measured its spread by a series of one-yard strides and noted it as one of the broadest spreading oaks in his native land; but the generous shade induced a fit of idle- ness, and we did no more than examine the cave-like hollow of its trunk and decide upon the number of schoolboys who could hide within.</p>
        <p>The green is such a place as fancy pahits of the village of Arcadia. Those who are dreaming dreams. of Garden Suburbs ought to visit it at least twice a year to keep their ideals at the proper altitude. In the centre is a broad square of green sward. On one side a row of cottages decked with flowers, on the other the school, whilst at the lower end the waters of the lake complete a scene of idyllic beauty. The village school, we ought to say, is a relic of the days of Piers Warburton and was anciently a timbered barn.</p>
        <p>The late Squire of Arley, so often mentioned in this sketch, delighted in the green, and used to play his part here as a fine old English gentleman among his tenants and retainers. He set up a Maypole and revived the fun and frolic associated with it, much to the delight of the country lads and lassies. He himself led off in the fun and thoroughly enjoyed it, but the visit of " undesirables " who came from long distances and made the May festival a deb anch ledl to its abandon- ment.</p>
        <p>The old school formed out of the timbered barn is a picture of loveliness. We did not see th e scholars, for the reason perhaps that they are on holiday. But happy boys they must be who come to Arley §chool. Real trees to clim b,real birds' nests to inspect real grass, a real pump in the middle, real swans to tease, real water to fall into—what a paradise! We did not see the schoolmaster either, but we should imagine that like the Scotch peasant of whom we have read he takes off his hat every morning to the beauty of the world. That at least is what we do as we take a last survey of the tranquil green and turn towards Warrington and the setting sun. A. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>BUDGET.</head>
        <p>THE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER ENTHUSIASTIC MEETING AT WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Unanimous Support.</p>
        <p>The Budget received enthusiastic and unanimous support at an open-air meeting held near Arpley Cannons on Monday evening at the instance of the Warrington Progressive Labour League. There was an attendance of about 2,000 and the speeches were listened to with keen interest and punctuated with loud cheers. Mr. Robert Lewis, of the Lancashire and Cheshire Liberal Association, was the principal speaker, and be gave a lucid exposition of the Budget proposals, particularly of the land clauses. Mr. NV. H. Sherburn presided and others present on the platform were: Messrs. E. Wynne- Jones, E. England, and J. Howes, jun. The CHAIRMAN said the Budget was a bold Budget, but that would not be a recommendation if it were not a fair one. As well as being bold and fair it was a democratic Budget and that could not always have been said in the past about the Budgets. One of the principles of the Budget was that the nation or the State should re-assert its absolute control over the land. So little had been said about that for sometime that some landlords bad come to think that they were absolute owners of the land. He wanted to get that mistake rectified as soon as possqile and it was for those who realised what that meant to stand it no longer. Selfish interests of .individuals should not stand in the way of public progress. From some quarters the Budget would receive as it had received, uncompromising opposition. The Budget maintained that responsibility must always accompany privilege. 600 years ago the land was responsible for the finances of the State. Those who had the privileges and drew their emoluments from the land had to fad the where- withal to carry the State along. During those 600 years we had been ruled by landlords all the time, because it was scarcely possible for anybody but a landowner to get into the House of Commons, let alone the House of Lords. During that time the responsibility in a great measure had been shifted from the land, although the land had got more and more valuable, and placed on industry. Those who had got the privileges wanted to shirk the responsibilities. Did they think that was fair? (VOICES: "No, no !") Another principle was that the Budget proposed direct taxation and not indirect taxes., Indirect taxation meant that we had got to pay something,but they must bury it somehow, so tbat we woukl not see what we were paying. The land- lords had objected to the Budget on the ground that they would not be able to pay their servants. They said it was a very bad principle to take away riches from the rich because they would not be able to pay them so much wages. Of course, that meant that the more we paid for taxes the better it would be for us, because those who had the money would be able to pay more wages if they liked. (Laughter.) Tariff reformers were fond of alluding to German conditions. It was well to bear in mind that according to the income tax returns in England ode million people paid 000,000,000 income tax. In Germany ftve-and-a-half millions of people paid nearly £600,000,000. That would tell them about landlordism in Gerinsny versus landlordism in England. (Applause). Mr. ROBERT LEWIS, of Manchester, in seconding, said it was becoming rapidly a question as to who was to be master in the House of the nation—the people or the Lords. He said "the people," and it was about ' time the lordly aristocrats of this country got to</p>
        <p>understand that we were lodtent to tolerate there s ornaments, but they were not to dictate the policy of this country. The workers who had to bear the large share of taxation were the people who were to settle what the legislation was to be, and what taxes were to be imposed. Last week the "Daily Mail" was advising the Conservative party to throw up the sponge. To--day it was begging of them to keep their tails up. (Laughter.) He hoped they would. It was very much better to fight a dog with its tail up than a wet sponge well squeezed. (Laughter.) It was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>GOING TO BE A FIGHT</head>
        <p>GOING TO BE A FIGHT</p>
        <p>and he knew who was going to win. (Applause.) Deal- with the previous measures of the Government, Mr. Lewis said that whatever his hearers' opinion on the Education and Licensing Bills might be, he suggested te them that the elected representatives of the people, who had come fresh from being face to face with the electors were better judges as to what was needed than the lordly aristocrats who never bad to face a popular audience. What did the Lords do? In the first case they so mutilated the measure that the Govemmentwere obliged to drop it. In the second case they settled it, not in the House of Lords but at the house of Lansdowne. When it got to the House of Lords it received practically no discussion ; they had settled before-hand that it was to he thrown out. He would Mr° the question as to who was to be the ruler of this country—Buckingham Palace or Lansdowne House—to be settled. It seemed to him that the time must quickly come when Lansdowne House must be put on one side as the determiner of the policy of any party or Government. (Applause.) This year the dis- cusston was about the Budget. The first year it was the poor children who would be without religion. Last year it was the widows and orphans who would be without food. This year it was the market gardener and the poor workingman who were going to suffer enormously because of the proposed depredations of the Government. (Laughter). They never thought the House of Lords was such a sympathetic philanthropic set of gentlemen as the Tories wanted them to believe. (Laughter). When a subservient House of Commons voted for Chinese labour in South Africa a willing House of Lords readily supported them. Now they said to the workingmen " We are your .friends. We are your divinities. We think of your trouble—and we give you Christmas beef." (Loud laughter).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>TilE DUKES</head>
        <p>TilE DUKES</p>
        <p>said they would no longer subscribe to hospitals and .charity. He had been looking up the lists and it would not make one of their fortunes if all they gave to every charity was counted up. Then they said that if the Budget passed, the whole country would be ruined. Oh, let it be soon! (Laughter.) Dealing with the causes of the increased expenditure, Mr. Lewis referred to old age pensions. In spite of Tory opposition the Govern- ment were able to pass that measure, and on January Ist between 600,000 and 700,000 old people became pensioners. If the Government did not deserve praise for any other measure, they merited the heartiest thanks of the community for having solved a difficulty which had long existed in that matter. Then they wanted money for • Dreadnoughts. They wanted the trade and commerce of the conntry to bo adequately protected, but while they were anxious to have a full and complete army and navy and a thorough and efficient navy they did not want to throw money away. Every Dreadnought they built was a waste to some ektent. They could put money into a mill and it reproduced itself by the work of the worker, but if they spent a million pounds on a warship, at the end of twenty years the vessel went on to the scrap heap, and the men who had lived on her all that time bad not added one ounce to the wealth of the community. The Government said "We will have enough, but we will not waste money on the provision of unnecessary ships." They also wanted money for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>SOCIAL REFORAI--..</head>
        <p>SOCIAL REFORAI--..</p>
        <p>labour exchanges, insurance against sickness, unemploy- ment and into rmittent employment. They wanted to bring into operations scheme that the man who wanted work and the work that needed to be done should be brought together as quickly as possible. The Govern- ment were also going to provide a fund for the develop- ment of England; they wore going to.try and develop the biden resources of the country and bring out that which would be most h elpful to -the community as a whole. Alluding to the tobacco tax the speaker quoted the remark of a Lancashire man who Was paying two- pence per week extra an d who said he did not know of any insurance company. wh o for twoponce a week would provide old age pewrons _ for himself and his wife. (Applause). The Government were also laying hold of two great monopolies which rightly belonged to the Stlte—land and liquor. Those were monopolies which the State had never really. parted with. The liquor licence was not the brewers' or the publican's property. it belonged to the State, and the State had absolute right to insist upon any condition it liked in granting a licence. The Government said this monopoly bad been paying too little into the National and local exchequers. The added demand of the Chancellor. of the Exchequer on "the tr ado" would not amount to 3d. a barrel on all the beer that was brewed in eveg part of the country. And vet brewers were putting oh 12s. a barrel-3d, for the dov ernment and 116.-9 d. for themselves. (Laughter.) That was tariff reform. (Renewea laughter.) These were the men, the lordly millionaires, the enormously rich brewers, who cried out" This Government is a wicked, vindictive Government." The Budget Protest League darenot come Out into the open air and discuss their opposition to the Budget, because the case for the Budget WAS SO splendi (fly buttressed by that filch was right, true and good. The case for the opposition was hopeless and they knew it. Some of the land- owners were asking why Mr. Lloyd-George did not ' keep to his lawyer's office? Because the nation wanted a _ BRAVE CHANCELLOR OF THE EXCHEQUER,</p>
        <p>and they found him in the man who led \Vales, and had led it for years to victory. (Applause.) The speaker explained the land proposals of the Budget. The Government proposed that on the unearned increment they should take a certain tax—not on the land. There was no new land tax imposed. The land was paying now on the 1672 valuation, except the half which the urban population contributed underthe Agricultural Rates Act passed by a Tory Government. The Government were moderate and modest in their demand. Out of the value which the community alone created they were going to take only £1 out of every £5. If they had done what he (the speaker) thought they might do with perfect, justice, considering the State as a whole, they would have taken £4 and left the land holder XL Agricultural land would be exempt from the tax as would also small holdings which were due to individual energy, industry and experience. But the man who by reason of the enterprise of the community had in the past pocketed thousands and hundreds of thousands of pounds would in the future be called upon to "stump up.' (Applause). The man who kept land in reserve in order to screw the uttermost farthing from the people would have to declare its value, and on the value so declared he would have to pay a tax of one halfpenny in the £ to the national exchequer. (A voice: "Ho ought to pay more.") The man who grumbled at that was positively unreasonable. There were questions opening up before us that had got to be solved—questions that were dear to the heart of every social reformer. The Budget was THE "MORNING STAR"</p>
        <p>that was coming to us. Who was content to see England going on, as it had been going on ?—the rich growing richer, the poor poorer. Was there any man whose heart had never been saddened by the sight of honest workingmen seeking work and unable to find it? The Government wanted to solve that problem, and the Budget would help them to solve it. It would not do everything, but it would do scmething. It would not solve it at ones., but it would make a beginning. The Budget spoke tope to the workers of the land. It said "Cheer up" to he toilers of to-day. It said to those who had been put on one side by reason of taking parish relief, and had been prevented having old age pensions: " Your day is coming !" It said to us all "Bask to the land," that the land should bear the burden and not industry ; that it should give to the State that which the Creator meant it to give,and that which the Creator meant for all should not be the private holding of any one man. The Budget had put heart in hope into hundreds of thousands of people. People did not usually get enthusiastic about paying taxes but they had got enthusiastic about this Budget. He asked his audience to work for the Budget and the • day would come all the sooner when it would ho placed on the Statute Book,—the finest</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>CHARTER OF E3fANCIPATION</head>
        <p>CHARTER OF E3fANCIPATION</p>
        <p>this country bad known for a long time. Some of the wealthy said that if the Budget passed they would take their capital into Germany. Let them go; they would only be going home! (Laughter). There was not a country on the face of the earth to equal Britain, and the man who talked some other country up and ran his own country down was not a patriot but a small-souled tariff reformer as a rule. (Laughter). They believed in this country and they wore going to have the charter of their liberty. They were going to have the Budget, the whole Budget and nothing but the Budget. It was the first step to putting on the land the ditties the land should bear and saving industry from the burden it had so long borne. (Loud applause.) The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried with enthusiasm and unanimity, three ringing cheers being given for the Budget. _</p>
        <p>Mr. J. HOWEs, jun., announced that towards the end of September Mr. pre, K.C., the Lord Advocate, would address a meeting in the Parr Hall. He reminded the audience that now was the time for the registration of voters and all information would be willingly given at the Liberal Club, Bewsey-street. Mr. E. WYNNE JONES, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Lewis and the chairman, said that in the " Exaniiner " of Saturday _last.. Mr. Arthur Moun- field in his WarringtM: Chapters" mentioned that when James Pennant came to Warrington in 1773 land in Latchford, averaged £3 per acre. There was a place in Latchford to-du where the ground rent for a house, shop and yard was £2O. At that rate the price would average not £3 per acre but £lOO per acre. Who had created that value? The people of Warrington, by their industry, and it was only right that some portion of it should go to the relief of the rates. (Applause.) Mr. E. ENGLAND seconded and said the Gorringe case quoted by Mr. Lloyd-George was only a sample of what was going on almost any day in Liverpool. There the land was practically in the hands of the Sephton and Derby families. In Warrington land was fetching as much as £l7 a yard in Bridge-street and that value had been created solely by the progress and needs of the people. The Corporation wore getting wise in their</p>
        <p>generation and were starting 75 year leases in order tkat they could have the benefit of the increased value made by the community. The vote was heartily passed.</p>
        <p>- - - ---- - - - - .... ~.:„..! . . :...., .f.'-',' ..:, ' '... • _ _ - :,:. -, . . .. ' 444 T. V R-11 ADY FIRT:ID• ..... ./ .- ~. -4 ,-. Mr. Joseph Guinan, 34, Lower - Dorset Street, Dublin, writes . " I cut and bruised myself severely , by falling from my bicycle when I %, % ii• was pedalling at a fast pace. I went 11 1 V.. home and tried the usual homely , I 71,1.: remedies, but as my wounds and I i 4 ... bruises showed no signs of healing fiA ": ..• I got some Zam-Buk. This grand jil balm soothed the injured places /IA - . and healed all the cuts and ~,...r., .1 bruises perfectly. lam now - • -et- .. cycling again, but I always ... ...::...r.:.•- r. -.• -:, carry a box of ...r- . :-*-----.." am-u - ZBk ... .1 -414., • --V-e'll, ~,.. ...' i I. ) • Ore. ' : - • ....- FORCUTS .... 4,...:.)24.. ar0.....;,,,,,,:, • 4,,:„...:::. •,0,--.;-i 4.,..- ::. • ...,,.,,,-. ...„,„---; ',a-BRUISES'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>MEETING AT PRESTON BROOK.</head>
        <p>MEETING AT PRESTON BROOK.</p>
        <p>An enthusiastic open-air meeting was held at Preston Brook on Saturday evening in favour of the Budget. There was a good attendance. Mr. Hubert Congreve presided, and Abe principal speaker was Councillor J. Middleton, of Oldharti. Amongst those present were Mr. Will Ford (secretary of the Knutsford Liberal Division) and Mr. G. W. Jackson.</p>
        <p>The Cirairman dealt ,at considerable length with the Budget, which, he said, laid down on broad generous lines how the finance, that was necessary to carry on the government of this country, could be raised on Free Trade lines, and not having recourse to Protection. He did not think that there could be any doubt about it, that the Budget when people understood it became popular. (Applause). Within the last few days we had had some very notable signs of that. In tbe first place the great Tory organ "The Times," which bad now become a sort of refuge for most antiquated theories and which supported privileges and landed interests, had hauled down the flag and told its supporters that it was useless to go on fighting the Budget. It also stated that to defeat it in the House of Commons was now practically shown to be impossible Within the last day or two we had had the Daily Mail" practically saying that it too was sick of the Dukes and the selfish rich men who apparently could do nothing else but shout the moment the hat came round. The object of the Budget was to raise the necessary funds for social reform and for the additional cost which, unfortunately as he thought bad been forced on the country owing to the demand for increased armaments. The chief among the social reform objects was old age pensions and that was the one thing which the Tory party apparently took the most objection to. But there was a great deal to do in the way of social reform in addition to that. The general condition of the people in this country especially in the towns needed serious attention (Hear, hear). Here the speaker described some of the awful condi- tions under which people lived and said that in a rich country like th's it was absolutely, unjust that such conditions should prevail and it made one's blood boil to think of it with all that vast expenditure going on for luxury at the present time. Continuing the Chair- man said nobody cried more for armaments than the Tory Party and yet the moment the Liberals gave old age pensions which they (the Tories) promised, and increased the armaments there was a tremendous fuss about saying. Mr. Balfour told them that if they taxed the rich they were destroying the capital of the country. 'I hat, the speaker thought was a most extraordinary proposition for a man in his position to make. In the first place capital did not con- sist of money only, but to a very large extent it consisted of the land and labour. He maintained that a tax ought to be put on the land. He did not think it would have any bad effect whatever, but if we went and put heavy taxes on the workingmen it would prevent them from getting sufficient food, clothing and • proper education. Then we would be destroying our capital in a way that we could never replace. (Applause.) The capital depended upon a well-fed and well-trained labour class. If they took Mr. Balfour's tax they must put it on the poor and not on the rich. The rich land- lords said if we put a tax upon land they would dismiss their men. That was the only way they could meet it. The speaker did not think they would dismiss their gardeners, butlers, footmen and chauffeurs. They could not dismiss the agricultural labourers, because if they did they would stop the sources of their income, and be thought he could prove that those very rich man would not be ruined for the sake of avoiding taxation. If wo carried that argument a little further it would mean that the workingclasses should pay taxes so that the rich men would have a certain amount of money to pay for footmen &amp;c. Tho Budget proposed to impcse certain taxes which were not barren taxes, but were fertile and which would yield more and more as time went on. (Applause.) Those taxes did not bear unfairly on anyone. They did not bear on the industries, and they did not, bear unfairly on the poor. Dealing with the land clauses, the Chairman instanced that a few years ago some land was valued at £87,000 and when the Manchester Corporation wanted it for a reservoir a few years later they had to pay £253,000. The owners of that land did not do anything to increase its value, and for the nation to take a fifth was a very just proposal. Concluding, Mr. Congreve said that they wanted to see such a system of taxation as would bear fairly upon all, and he contended the Budget laid down that principle. (Loud applause). Councillor.Middleton who is a very entertainins and lucid speaker, told the audience that the Budget* was very popular to the great working classes of this country. They had for a long tiine advocated the imposition of taxes on land and now they had the_ satisfaction of finding them in the Budget that had been introduced by a man of considerable financial ability on behalf of a Government which, he thought., might rank Bf. being intellectually one or the finest we had ever seen in our time. (hear, hear). The opposition against the Budget had been mainly for the purpose of getting rid of the land clauses. It was very reassuring and satis- factory to have the declaration of Mr. Winston Churchill—(applause)---that if the Budget became law it would be with the land clauses in it. (Applause). It had been seen for a very long time that there would have to be a large increase in the revenue of the country and it was that which caused their opponents to exult before their time. The Tories said "We have got them now. Free Trade finance is finished and Liberalism is vanquished. There are no new sources to get taxes from. There is nothing for it but, for the Government to clear out and then we shan't be long. Tariff Reform will come." The Tories had their rejoicings then: the Liberals had them now, for the Budget was a vindica- tion of Free Trade finance. The money would be applied to necessary and desirable objects. THE OLD AGE PENSIONS ACT</p>
        <p>was the most 'glorious piece of social reform that had been passed in anybody's time. That Act put us ahead of all the nations of Europe in regard to our treat- ment of the aged. Some sneered at the pension being so small as ss. Supposing it were cnly 55., it was ss. more than any other Government had provided in the history of our country. The old age pensioners did not sneer at it. With regard 'to the taxation of large incomes, Mr. Middleton said that the super income ought to bear the super tax. If we did not have a super tax we would have to put up with a supper tax. (Hear, hear.) That was the alternative that was offered to us. They believed that the Government proposal was a downright honest, fairminded and just one. Continuing he said that the Dukes seemed to be worse than anyone else for grumbling. There was one noble Lord who had declined to give £lO for a scholarship to enable a poor lad to receive a better education than he would other- wise obtain. (Shame). He could not be so badly off when his estate VMS valued at between 3or 4 million pounds. Concluding he said he thought the Lords would swallow the Budget, for the people would insist that it became law. (Applause). Councillor Middleton then moved a resolution recording entire agreement with the Budget. Mr. Jackson in seconding the resolution asked his felloiv workingmen how much surplus they had that could be tr.xed. (Hear, bear.) The Budget bad put the burden of taxation upon the shoulders that could bear it. If some of those poor millionaires and some of those people who were not quite so rich could not spare it, how could the workingmen spare it? The Budget taxed wealthy Liberals as well as Conservatives and yet most of them were not grumbling. Mr. Ford supporting the resolution mentioned that the Earl Egerton of Tatton had written to one Tory meeting and stated that " the Budget infringes the liberty of the people." To that the speaker answered "What about Rostherne?" (Applause.) He also said that the tax on land was not on agricultural land. The resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>CHEAP GAS v. ELECTRICITY. WIDNES COUNCIL'S CHOICE.</head>
        <p>CHEAP GAS v. ELECTRICITY. WIDNES COUNCIL'S CHOICE.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Widnes Town Council held in the Town Hall, on Tuesday, it was reported that preliminary steps were about to be taken to pro- vide a site upon which to erect an electricity generating station, for the supply of which the Council have obtained Parliamentary powers. It was also decided to obtain expert advice on a scheme prepared by the gas engineer.—A letter has been received from a private firm intimating their intention of applying for a pro- visional order to supply electricity in the borough, and Councillor G. Davies expressed the opinion that the Council should allow this firm to proceed. He did not think that they would " strike oil" in an electrical sense in Widnes owing to the cheapness of gas; and he was afraid that if the Corporation undertook the supply they would be involved in groat expenditure. It was resolved that an interview be given to the repre- sentatives of the firm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>THREE MONTHS IN INDIA, SYRIA, AND PALESTINE.</head>
        <p>THREE MONTHS IN INDIA, SYRIA, AND PALESTINE.</p>
        <p>TEA AT ABU TEWFIK'S.</p>
        <p>Abu Tew—fili's--iea7l name is Machoul (the spelling, here as elsewhere is guesswork Abu means father. It is another instance of the flowery ambiguities of Arabic, that when man has a son he 13 addressed as "father of "—that son! Tewfik himself was a lad of , twelve; seemed strange that his name should e pre-eminent in the family. My , cousin kept forgetting to add Tewfik, and calling "Abu, shall we go here or there," etc.; which must have sounded very odd to the stout Syrian, though he never lost his courteous gravity. Mrs. Machoul received us in a small but charming Damascan parlour. A Syrian floor is raised about a foot above the level of the courtyard and a passage-way at the entrance of the room admits of all the dust being swept into this lower level, and so out into the courtyard. The floor was marble-paved, and strewn with three beautiful Persian rugs, and we sat on a divan or sofa at either side of the room. Mrs. Machoul brought us delightful confections of her own making on a tray, and we were glad to find she spoke English even more fluently than her husband. During the conversation I happened to ask whether they:had any local ghost-stories, and Abu Tewfik's face became wooden whilst he appeared not to know what we meant. Guessing that he feared we were laughing at him, I related a short English ghost-story as an example of what We wanted, and when they both saw that to us these things were not ridiculous, their manner at once changed, and we listened to three of the most remarkable stories I have yet heard.</p>
        <p>By Truda H. Crosfield. (ARTICLE VI.)</p>
        <p>has now become a mere habit,and is supposed to be nerve soothing like smoking or chewing betel-nut! I said in French to the Algerian that we so regretted we had not had time to visit the tomb of his illustrious father, and that we had red of the latter's exploit in quelling the massacre of 1860 with deep admiration " Oh,but I, I," said the prince (aA, he was styled) tapping himself ow the chest "I discovered a plot amongst the Druses, to murder Christians and I stopped it !" He didn't look as though he could stop a hen crossing the road and had none of the fire and command his old father must have had. Dr. McKinnon had been to Palmyra, the scene of Zenobia's empire and downfall, with the Rev. John Kelman, of Edinburgh, a year or two previously. I mention this because Mr. Kilman, who is best known perhaps by his exquisite book "The faith of Robert,Louis Stevenson" has written a charming account of these travels also. Dr. McKinnon has lived and, worked for 25 years in Syria, and possesses a name to conjure by both there and in Palestine. We were fortunate in making the acquaintance of his assistant, Dr. Turnbull, who was travelling to Jerusalem for his holidays, and who offered to combine forces with us, which not only mutually diminished expenses, but was of the greatest advantage to Us as he had a thorough knowledge not only of the coin- age and the fair thing in prices, but of the language. Our last visit at Damascus was to the great Mosque, whose glorious mosaics were unfortunately ruined by fire twelve years ago, and to the tomb of Salauddin near by,that Saladin with whom Scott's novel the Talisman has made us so familiar. By the tomb lay a gilt wreath placed there by the Kaiser "from one Emperor to another."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>WITCH STORY.</head>
        <p>A WITCH STORY.</p>
        <p>They were rather witch than ghost stories, and occurrences of the kind, Abu Tewfik assured us, were of common knowledge far and wide amongst the villages, and even the towns of Syria. He related a personal experience, and though it happened 25 years ago, he said thd remembrance of it was as fresh as though it had taken place last week. Walking up a mule-path one hot summer's day Abu Tewfik arrived on the top to see a woman sitting on.the edge of a well in the cornfield, combing her hair. There was nothing unusual in this except that the more she combed, the more hair fell coiling and coiling into a basket at her feet! It sounded</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>HAURAN RUINED CITIES.</head>
        <p>THE HAURAN RUINED CITIES.</p>
        <p>We left Damascus at_ 6 a.m. on Monday, sth April,bidding a regretful farewell to Miss Dill and Miss Logan,Abu Tewfik and the girls All morning our train crossed the Hauran, that wonderful upland plain where once Og, King of Bashan ruled. The plain is covered with myriads of flowers and there is little cultivation. Mainly it is grazed by flocks of goats. Every now and again we passed the ruins of what had once been a stone-built village. Huge square lintels and door posts still stood here and there, often backed by the wretched turfed hut which the Bedouins</p>
        <p>_ I</p>
        <p>• - , /4 , - . • . , i .. - - ..... . . ' - . . *- • . .- .--. .. ... ~,,..,..„,:o.,,,(tysipt:i.;:il'.::::‘,":it:.--..m5.4,-;-., '.. --7 ....-_,,,,,,,xi, .•• ~. ~,;,":"'.-.....,,, ~,, ..,,,,,,fi„...;.?.,....,,,,,,,,-;,./.,o„.ezt-: ~,,t,::::-.,:,..,,,,. -,..,,, ..,. ~,,,...,.4-....,:iti1,,::-...,,,,.,7.::;,,,..,:......,,,,;;;;„.F,.., -"'" - .'-'-'.',',' 1.--. . 7 -;"' ''', .^.I ; --.A., ',,'-'.l'..' .*,.. .: ;...-...;',4.,.„,..,.,,'-:,,: '.'. -.,,,,. "„-,, -.•,?:,-fi4e,' '' ,'..„c1.4., ',.,- , firt,t',.,,-- ..-:::.,..",::,,;:::..,....,:-.,,,,,,,,,,, &lt;•,,..,:'''".4.'''''..,::7 : ....,:e.-- ..,*:: : .'°.::, ••• '' •,•,:":."".",:: ' .5. %;.';:f9.',f'vg7.,.'r: ''.t.."...;.'..f., . , ~.E,,,,,r0• ..;;'...` ~.•.1 '. . - . •- ', -.„'; ,&gt;.-- , ....,..:4,, -",-..'...0' .... -ft 0-.44 ..;,..,- ~,,m,•.4'•..•;i45..„..14,t, ~4.4..„ "&lt;-/.. .. • .:Arr ~,,w-0.'.• .."..., „0.4........;. ' ,0" - ........,,....iii:'::,. .... ~. .... r . ~.. . - ;..„0,..• • - ,_..air, ,:,':.:•,.)..;; :1, ~.,,,, . . 0 , ...;., . ,;•.- a ' ',..,.'.:;,;;;,..:i4;:.+:-i'V''',,itlrke. ~,, ",,i• 4: . '.... ::. ' ' , , , . • - ',, ' ''' . '-.:!:•*lie S ~,, . . iii .„4-,,,.,,.:...„,..f ~'".•,.•,•, -.,,,.' --4 , ~s. : „..• •-,.., •• , •-9,i1,.,. ci, - •-.7,.'0,,..e.;;;1if,f,ii;.f.,,,7,7'.-,i,•,:i.;:,.:-...4,..i.- , 4,,, ,H,g-, , • -- ...,....;-7,..••••.!,•'',A.,..-•i1iq7::.,•., .."-',. ~...:.i 1 '.-..-' , •,-7,..4•'.1.1740-.,..**'''',l,i7,.„,iii;:i4w-il::',t, • :;.. , .. ....„...,.„.. ~..... .; ... .. , . , .. .... ~. „. . ..... ,x- —.....„..„...,-,...,.., . , , •. . , , ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>VIRGIN MARY'S WELL, NAZARETH.</head>
        <p>VIRGIN MARY'S WELL, NAZARETH.</p>
        <p>remarkably like Pelleas and Melisande. Abu Tewfik then imitated how she leaned round sideways and parted her hair to look at him. "And as she looked her eyes grew bigger and bigger, till they were the size of cow's eyes." (This is a particular mark of witchery in Syria, for it occurred in two of the other stories). "When I felt her drawing me to come forward,l was very afraid; but I remembered one must not anger these spirits, so I tried to back away slowly. Then 'a mule came up the .pathway behind me, and when it reached the cornfield, it too, saw the spirit there, and,knew it was evil." Abu Tewfik then imitated how the mule pawed the ground and gave a violent snort of rage; whereupon in an instant witch, hair, basket and all disappeared. "When the Muleteer came up, he having been below and seen nothing, he spoke to me and asked why I so trembled; but for over a minute I could not reply. Then I told him what I had seen, dnd he said 'Yes, I too have seen her sitting there.'" Abu Tewfik added that he recog- nised the spirit as resembling a woman in one of the neighbouring villages, but that he knew that she was not real by her eyes, her hair, and her sudden vanishing. GREEN RIDERS. _ _</p>
        <p>_ Some years before this, he being then a lad of 15 or so, was set with his brother to watch the vineyards by night. The boys had a large cloak to share on the ground, and had lain down to sleep about 9 o'clock, when they suddenly heard singing near by. Looking round,they beheld riders and horses of human size but coloured green, who were riding not over, but through, the vines and the hedge which here guarded them. The singing pro- ceeded from these green people, and the boys were so terrified that they - gave a loud scream. The man who owned the vineyard heard them and came running with his gun. He, too, heard the singing, and saw the riders. He waved his arm to the boys, calling to them not to fear; then fired his gun and figures and singing vanished. Mrs. Machoul related a still more wonderful occurrence to us, but as I fear to exceed the patience of the unbelieving, I will content myself with these two instances. As to any explanation; if one may hazard one, it is that the witch woman's soul had learnt the secret of projecting itself in its astral bofly, whilst still belonging to the material world. And if one doubts whether there be such a thing as the astral body (or etheric double) I can only quote the words of Shakespeare, than which no truer were ever written to my thinking :—" There are more things inleaven and earth than are dreamt of in our philosophy.</p>
        <p>make for theinselves. Or'patched tents would be set in the midst of these cycicepean ruins of earlier days. Deraar junction in the midst of this plain stands a mile from Edrei, the capital of King Og. I suppose it was here they found his giant iron bedstead. Any- way, the Rev. W. Hannawa, a distinguished archeologist whom we met, told us that thirty years ago, whilst he and a friend were exploring Edrei, they came on a hole down which they crawled. It ran slanting-wise into the earth, and suddenly they found themselves in an underground street. There were houses hewn in the ground either side, and actually a market-place and a palace. One room in this palace was roofed with a, single slab of jasper. But the Turkish govern- ment did not permit exploration. The train now began to wind down about 2,000 feet through gorges. The heat refraction off the chalk cuttings was simply—scorching. The air danced like quicksilver, and far below one saw the river and longed for it. We were thankful to emerge in the early afternoon on the cool shores of the lake of Galilee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>HERO'S SON._</head>
        <p>A HERO'S SON._</p>
        <p>_ I have not yet spoken of the Victoria Hospital, where Dr. and Mrs. McKinnon and Miss Mann received us most kindly and entertained us to dinner, and where we met a son of Abd-el-Kadr, a melancholy-eyed middle-aged man who played a great deal with a string of amber beads. This is a wide- spread custom and had a religious origin, as Mohammedans repeated the name of God thirty times on the beads, and then that of the prophet an equal number of times, but it</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Tlit PASSOVER AT TIBERIUS.</head>
        <p>Tlit PASSOVER AT TIBERIUS.</p>
        <p>It looked sweet and peaceful: the grassy banks and sloping hills, vivid with green moss reminded me of Scotland or the Lake District. The waters were very green and clear; :the steep cliffs down which rushed the Gaddarene swine, lay on our right, and snowy Hermon closed the end view. Our very boatmen had gaunt serious faces with black beards and shaggy hair, such as one has often seen in pictures of the disciples whilst they were yet fishermen. No doubt the type has changed but little. We were rowed across to Tiberius where we met Dr. Turnbull who had preceded us. After a stroll round the ruined Roman wall of the little town—a bathing resort in Herod's days —we called on Dr. Torrens of the Scotch Mission, who kindly arranged for us to wit- ness the Passover that night in the house of the chief Rabbi. At 9 o'clock we entered an upper chamber in the whitewashed house, and found all the family assembled. On a table in a corner lay the bone of a leg of lamb, unleavened bread, resembling wide thin wafers, and a heap of" bitter herbs," looking much like water cress or spinach. On a mattress lay the Chief Rabbi, a very old man who evidently had not long to live, but whose black eyes still had fire in them, whilst his emaciated face was full of dignity. He looked very different to his stout son and grandson, both of whom shoved but little reverence in what they were doing and saying. The women were of no account and sat in a corner, watching theFt noisy menfolk some- what scornfully, we thought. The old Rabbi had to.be propped up against the wall, for he was dying of a weak heart. His hawk- like nose and proud sad old face were unfor- gettable, and his wife was a most spirited old woman. A boy of twelve was deputed to recite what the symbols meant and why they were there eating them that night; he answered all the questions and read long passages from the Talmud with enormous fluency and long breaths. Then the men and boys would break into a crooning chaunt, rocking to and fro; I never heard anything</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>more untuneful. The baby even, was not spared, but had his little mouth crammed with "bitter herbs," till the poor mite yelled! We were offered some wine, which was good but extremely heady; and carrying off samples of the Passover bread as a final present, went out and admired the full moon shining on the placid waters of the Lake of Galilee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>PEACEFUL LAKE.</head>
        <p>THE PEACEFUL LAKE.</p>
        <p>Next morning we were rowed over to Capernaum about 6 o'clock. In place of yesterday's scorching heat, there was a grey, quiet, cloudy sky. We found a quiet monastery, inhabited by one franciscan and a few workmen on the site of the little city. As Christ had predicted,: Capernaum has been blotted- out. But the remains of the synagogue have been dug up. It must have been a handsome one; . carved limestone fragments of 'pillars were lying abbut, and the black and white marble flooring was visible. The nice old German brother showed us the site, and we admired his beautiful green garden and shady maples. Then re-embark- ing we were rowed along the shore. Pink oleanders starred the bushes which fringed the water; wild flowers and blowing grasses, tender-leaved mulberry trees and steep grassy hills made up a perfect scene. An almost visible peace lay over the country- side, as though the footsteps of our Lord had left an ineffaceable impression, We rowed past Bethesda, a lovely nook in the curving shore, where a splendid rush of water gushed forth beside an old ruined mill, and where the prophet Jonah is said to have lived in retreat before he was sent on his embassy to Nineveh. The shore of Gennesareth is about two miles long, and covered with flowers, grasses and aromatic shrubs. It is marsh- land, bordered by a white pebbly shore, and just beyond Yes Magdala, buried in a thick shady walnut grove. It was wonderful to think that our boat must be rowing quite near the spot where the disciples were cast- ing their Aets, when the Christ came walk- ing to them on the water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>OUR DRIVING TOUR.</head>
        <p>OUR DRIVING TOUR.</p>
        <p>• The placid, blue-grey lake scarce showed a ripple as we were rowed home, but by one o'clock all was changed. A fierce wind was lashing the lake, and roared round the hotel, as we took our departure in a ramshackle break, canvas-roofed, and drawn by three horses abreast. Our driver wore a long and narrow canary-yellow garment, much like a nightshirt, and of course the usual red fez on his head. The keen wind grew ever stronger, and aDpeared to circle all round the compass till it faced us, when of course it stuck there, and chilled us to the •bone, although we wrapped ourselves to the chin in our travel- ling rugs. We had to,rise about 1,000 feet, and passed shepherds scattered with their flocks amongst the shrubs and boulders. It was here we saw one man smoking cigarettes as he sat on a stone and watched his flock; and this struck us as incongruous as hearing the gramophone in the tiny street of Baalbek! Our road wound in and out of hills, the soft earth track being pleasant, but the metalled road most jarring. It was not well laid and we had no springs. Fleas, flies, and bad roads are the chief drawbacks to travel in Palestine. Our companion Had his eyes glued on Baedeker whenever we looked round,but how he could read with the jolting, I don't know. Far behind, we saw the hollow of the lake, for it lies 700 feet below sea level. On , our left was the fertile corn-growing plain of Jezreel, and we were amongst the Galilean hills. We passed through the sup- posed Cana of Galilee, beautifully situated amongst orchards of olives and pomegranates. But it lies high and the wind was chillly,and we were benumbed before, at 6.30, we topped the last rise and beheld Nazareth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>NAZARETH.</head>
        <p>NAZARETH.</p>
        <p>It lies in a shallow basin on top of the hills and is the nicest, Cleanest town we saw in all Palestine. The valley is hardly half a' mile long and is open to all the winds of heaven and yet lies sheltered from them by the rims of its hill, whilst to the East the minded cone of Mount Tabor protects it. We spent the night here and were shown the .sites or sights next morning by the father of Dr. Scrimgeour of the Edinburgh Medical Mission there. The hospital was very full, and one saw sad cases of opthalmia and disease in the waiting-room. A tragedy had happened in the night, for a man had been brought in with a serious gunshot wound to which he had succumbed, saying to the last that he had accidentally shot him- self though as Dr. Scrimgeour said, this would have been nearly impossible as the wound went straight through his side from left to right. Someone had done it, but the poor fellow bravely denied this to avert the blood feud which itiould inevitably have followed. The most interesting thing in Nazareth is the so-called Virgin Mary's Well. It is about the only spring in the village, and it is practically certain that she and tier Son must have gone thdre to draw water. The spring is faced with a stone archway and the women bear away the water' in earthern jars on the head. One is shown the supposed site of the carpenter's shop and the view out over the tiny green valley 'to the distant hills is lovely in the extreme and must often have pleased the eyes of the boy Jesus when he rested from work to look out.</p>
        <p>THE BEAUTIFUL PLAINS OF PALESTINE. We drove off about 2 p.m. next day, and speedily came upon one of the finest views in Palestine. Below lay the superb plain of Jezreel, purple, brown or green according as it was plough land or waving corn; Tabor, little Hermon, the hollow of the lake of Galilee, Gilboa, the hills of Ephraim, and lastly, long, hogbacked Carmel, lay around us. As we crossed the plain and gradually drew near the latter, we saw the hollow near its top where all archeologists agree that Elijah must have had his competition with the priests of Baal. It is the only place on Carmel where there is a perennial spring of water, wherewith Elijah could have drenched his altar, aud close below at the foot of the steep hill runs the tiny river Kishon. The early and the latter rain is still an institution in Palestine; the latter (no pun intended) came down upon us as we drove over the exposed plain: its myriad teely drops drove like flashing darts, and even the canvas side to the hood did not quite keep it out. The horses had to stand tail-on, till the first fury of the squall was over. But then the sun came out, the birds sang, and scent of leaves and flowers wafted gratefully from the refreshed earth,and we all three simultaneously cried that it was like England, Ireland and Scotland (as we belonged to those three countries). We were driving through little hillocks covered with luxuriant dwarfoaks, and suddenly realized that it was here Deborah had lived, and that Barak had besought her help. We ate oranges and drank some of Mrs. Abu Tewfik' s delicious sherbert out of a thermos kindly lent to us by Mr. and Mrs. MacFarland. The air giew much softer as we approached the sea. We were disappointed in the Kishon, but our driver said the sluggish stream could become a raging river sometimes. That night we spent in a German hotel,at Haifa, and started after lunch to continue our driving tour. The sun was gay and green loamy breakers rolled up in endless BUCCessiOn on the fawny g9ld</p>
        <p>''4'o4tneßgiEtt</p>
        <p>RUINS OF AN</p>
        <p>walking. We heard the remnant of the Samaritans at worship. There are,.only 140 ' left of this ancient sect and their temple is an effaced ruin. It was built on the heights of Gerizim behind us when the Jews refused to receive them in their temple at Jerusalem because, during the capivity, the Samaritans had become a mixed race. We met Dr. and Mrs. Wright of the medical mission, and a, Syrian pastor and his son Moses, an intelli- gent boy of 14, home for the holidays. These two dined with us and imformed us that the chief industry of Nablous was soap-making, and that they had seven small firms in the town! But the article manufactured was decidedly primitive, to judge by a specimen I procured. Far the most interesting spot in Shechem to our thinking was Jacob's well about a mile from the town. Even archeo- logists seem to agree that Jacob's sons probably did use this well, and there is no doubt it was here that our Saviour sat and conversed with the woman of Samaria. It is about 70 feet deep and smoothly faced with stone all round. All day our road wound upwards through the hills of Ephraim and Judea. Why Judah ever con- sented to inhabit such bare and sterile hills I cannot imagine, for they look as though they had never known green since the beginning of the world! We often got out and walked , short cuts to ease the horses and passed crowds of Bedouins returning from Jerusalem with their wives and children. At midday we halted by a stream and broken bridge and sat in its shelter for our lunch, for the wind was cutting. Spreading a plaid on the stones we had a comfortable seat and- enjoyed our eggs, chicken, bread and oranges. Presently some Arabs came along and halted a short way from us, for it is not etiquette amongst the Arabs to watch others eat. We offered them some meat and chocolate, the latter being an experiment. They examined it doubtfully but evidently liked the taste, for a young man in the party exclaimed " tiyeb " and ate the lot. This so annoyed my cousin, that she got Dr. Turnbull to say he had eaten more than his share, at which the young Arab laughed delightedly, whilst an older man inquired "Shall I hit him over the head with my club ?" Rain once more swept over the bleak uplands of Judea. Villages at times appeared on the hillsides, of much the same colour as the rocks and earth. Olive trees grew everywhere with scanty crops around them. The shallow upland vales might be delightful in warm sunshine but we were literally transfixed with the cold though it was April.</p>
        <p>14,*4:743.,</p>
        <p>4</p>
        <p>• ,</p>
        <p>• ' ..••••••„:, ' • s• ..•• .; • • 1 ,•••• • , .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <p>sands of the Mediterranean as we drove along the Plain of Sharon. No wonder the Philistines loved the rich plains rather than the sterile hills of Palestine. But the wind was strong and dusty and finally rain again swept down on us. We wound hito the hills and spent the night in a dreadful inn at Zimmerin a Jewish colony established by Baron Rothchild for Russian, Polish, and other refugees. There was a horrible feeling that nothing had ever been cleaned or swept since the inn began. The spoons had egg incrustations on them, the milk was blue, and butter rancid, and we were thankful to leave at 5.30 next morning, though it was cold and rainy. Our road was now but an earth track, heavy and sodden with rain, so that often the rims of the wheels were buried in soil and I felt sorry for the horses. Cold sheets of rain , blew across our way from the hills of lEphraim to Cesarea on the sea. Occasionally we bumped through watercourses. At last it cleared and we drew up at Ziblon, a wretched Arab village, for coffee. Maud and I felt so sorry for a poor young Arab, plainly suffering from opthalmia in one eye. His manners were extremely .good for all his ragged clothing. We offered him a ginger- bread and chocolate. He touched lips, fore- head and breast twice with such dignity (this is the Arab salute) ; then, wishing to make some return he offered us tobacco from his pouch; but when we refused, showing we had cigarettes of our own, he sprang away with graceful rapidity and returned with a live coal! This poor young man looked very sad and had been born and bred in that savage and forlorn village. On we drove again through sloughs of black soil, till at last we reached a metalled road, and lunched in the carriage, for the limestone around us was covered with knots of writhing black centipedes! The wind was strong and cold, rain threatened, and the salt blew away; to add to our troubles, the thermos fell off the low carriage-seat, and broke We passed many Arabs in picturesque crimson, white or brown striped burnooses; and finally we saw mounts Ebal and Gerizim and reached Nauplia, the ancient Shechem, about 4.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>SHECIrEM.</head>
        <p>SHECIrEM.</p>
        <p>It was a relief to eriter a big clean German pension! Our first thought was for tea, and so benumbed were our hands, tat in drawing my cup towards me it upset all over myself, whilst the next minute our companion upset his all over the table cloth! After a 6orn- plete change on my part, we went forth and explored the mysterious town by twilight. Half the streets have houses built over them and thus form tunnels which were wet and greasy after the long rains, and very bad</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>JERUSALEM.</head>
        <p>JERUSALEM.</p>
        <p>It was about 4 o'clock when the-red roofs, the domes and spires of the Holy City came in sight; and it was almost with a shock that we realized that the rounded hillside facing us across the steep valley of the Kidron was the Mount of Olives. And here, in Mr. and Mrs. Hensman's homelike and comfortable pension, we spent six days, as full of interest and history as any I have ever enjoyed. I do not propose to describe Jerusalem in any detail however, as it is so well known and accessible, and as this paper is too long already. But the main features I should at once declare to be the Temple area and the MouAt of Olives. Jerusalem itself</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <p>became an heap of stones, its present walls and seven gates are the work of Moslem or Crusader, and even the site of the glorious temple is crowned by a Mohammedan mosque; but the Mount of Olives reaches up to the sky with pure outline as it did in the days when Christ walked in the outer court and looked ,across the valley to it. Some- where on that skyline the Ascension really took place, and half-way down it, there is an ancient gtove of olives now railed in' and planted with sweet scented flowers, which, if not the spot, is very near that world-tragic garden• of Gethsematte. The site of Calvary and the Holy Sepulchre has been debated for centuries ; but most English people prefer to see it in the spot selected by General Gordon- when surveying there. This is a little rounded hill outside the present walls, with an abrupt face on one side roughly resemioling a skull. The hill is open to the wind an sky, and thick with grasses and flowers. At one side is a quiet sunny garden under the rock, and here they show you a tomb, hewn in the hill-side, which, if not the holy Sepulchre, must be very like it. The bees hum in the flowers outside, one or two dark cypresses lay their shade on the garden, and there is that same wonderful peace which lay on Galilee. One likes to recall this beautiful spot, whether it be the exact one or not.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>JERICHO AND BETHLEHEM.</head>
        <p>JERICHO AND BETHLEHEM.</p>
        <p>We here formed a parti carre with the Rev. Mr. Unsworth, of Bolton-le-Moors and Nevada, an-elderly clergyman who delighted us with his American stories told with a gentle drawl. We all four drove down into the deep, valley of the Jordan where the Dead Sea, 1,700 feet below sea level, lies but 15 miles from Jerusalem. I never yet beheld such utter wild barrenness as on that drive. Scarce a blade grew amongst all those stony hills, and the plain looked indeed as though struck and devastated by lightning. We passed ihe reputed inn of the Good Samaritan half-way; and saw the windings of the brook Cherith with St. George's Monastery to which refractory monks are sent for some months as punishmeitt, accessible only by a path in the steep and frowning rocks above. Jericho—the modern one—has almost tropic vegetation, as have the banks of the Jordan, but everywhere else there is dry sand, withered grass and naked stony hillsides. The Mount of Temptation is sup- posed to be the steepest hill of all, rising very sheer above the ruins of ancient Jericho. The Gernians are excavating these ruins, and we rambled amongst themisun-baked mud walls of the tiny roofless houses. Few rooms were over 10ft. across. The outer walls of stone were standing, however, which was rather ,a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>!lENT, JERICHO.</head>
        <p>!lENT, JERICHO.</p>
        <p>disappointment! As to the Dead Sea, its taste was as bitter as that of the worst medicine. We saw some tourists bathing, who said it felt very buoyant,but they would not risk lying full length on its surface! Driving home next day we passed through Bethesda, lying on the Eastern slope of the mount of Olives. Ilere there is green shade and. pleasant cornfields. We went down into the so-called tomb of Lazarus, dug out of the ground to a depth of thirty feet or so and containing an inner chamber with shelves for several bodies.</p>
        <p>These tombs became tiring, for the country- side bristles with them, and they are all very hot.and entail bending double and moving in this position down along shafts. Dr. Turnbull's Baedeker contained 60 pages on Jerusalem and with true Scotch pertinacity he set to work to carry out every one of them. We visited the pools of Bethesda •(reputed) Hezekiah, Siloam, and the tombs of Prophets and Kings, but we struck after that, and he did the tombs of the priests alone! Our last excursion was out to Bethlehem, six miles away. My cousin and I rode two steeds which must have ordinarily worked in station cabs, for their hoofs at the canter came down with the crash of artillery on the road, and we soon relapsed into a jog trot. Bethlehem is a large limestone village,wholly given over to the mbther o' pearl industry and said to be very turbulent. The 'Church of the Holy Nativity is large and ornate, but it is divided into three sections each belong- ing to a different sect—the Latins, Greeks, and Armenians; and the guide told us that on feast days a Mohammedan soldier is obliged to stand on guara in the centre of the church to prevent . the factions fighting. They show you the Grotto of the Nativity below the church, but it is of course filled with lamps, tinsel and pictures as all the shrines unfortunately are. After all, the actual site does not matter. It is the open hillsides, the land and stones which alone remain as when our Lord saw them, and for these, if not for the interesting multi- tudes of many races which jostle each other in the towns and villages, I should strongly urge all who can do so, to spend a month on visiting the Holy Land. Next morning we bade farewell to our travelling companions and took the train down to Jaffa, whence a small steamer brought us back to Port Said. We spent a Sunday in that sunburnt port, and then boarded the P. and 0. Persia, passed poor Messina in ruins, came in for a great rolling in the .Bay, and landed in the 'Thames on the Ist of May, in a snowstorm! As a fellow passenger said "So like England !" Dear old England, all the same.</p>
      </div>
