      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</p>
        <p>[By Redcap.] ------- Diseases and Ailments (Continual.)</p>
        <p>SCALY LEGS. I have previously dealt with this disease ill 61 query column. However, I will repeat the remedy an' also give you the etuses so that you will be able have a more complete list under the heading cll " Diseases." The cause of scaly leg is a parasite. neglected these parasites accumulate and the fow1! legs soon become unsightly. Dirty and wet run.: often .engender this disease. A speedy reined,. it, obtained by applying the following treatment :--f wash the legs with soap and hot water, so as to sol'e; the scales. Then take a mixture of powdered su1101,„^ and paraffin oil, and work it into the joints of a little scales with a nail brush. Afterwards apply vaseline to take away soreness. Carry out lbe'e n directions judiciously, and improvement will B,,°°d follow. About ten days will completely cure aus case.</p>
        <p>• BUM DLE-FOOT. This disease is caused by fowls having to fly fr°ll,l high perches on to a hard floor; sometimes throug, constantly walking about on runs covered with rong_o cinders. When kept under such conditions thqt bruise their feet, had as a consequence purelen. matter forms under the skin. Treatment:—Fonieat with hot water and then rub over with vaseline. the bird affected cooped up and fed well. If the C 3,1,0 is a bad One and a quantity of pus has formed in A, foot, then apply a bread poultice for several nigu ;t* This will draw all the matter downwards. Then (16 should be lanced and poulticed again. After"ar „ bathe frequently with warm water that has bee diluted with Condy's Fluid. It is useless to use a stecr bird with bumble feet. They are very little use f° breeding purposes.</p>
        <p>EGG-BOUND. This is not a disease, but an ailment caused bY,_ egg breaking in the oviduct. It is very often a lit less egg. These are harder to pass than an egg 4. is shelled. Constant straining therefore bursts ill, The contents of the egg then runs away, but the use , SA.S is left inside the ovary passage. Another ca „di when an extra large egg gets crossways in the ebE passage. It is easy to tell when egg-bound. The het; run about with their tails down, often goiiig ot nest and constantly straining. Treatment : Fell° the vent with hot water. Then apply a little of t best Lucca Oil. Then extract the skin of the efl from the oviduct (in cases where it is a shell-less sgg' with the finger. When it is caused by a large egg' dip a feather in salad oil and pass it into the eg passage. Use great care, and give the hen o'eri assistance. Very often when negleeted they rePktui: themselves with straining. In fact it causes many cases.</p>
        <p>CRAMP. IL is This affects both chickens and adult fowls. brought on by being on bard floors, or, through c° wet clay soil, Sometimes overheating in the roostilire place is the cause. It not only affects them in t,.„ legs, but often in the back or loins I have 101°`..„ laying heni thus affected lay for several days 11 succession, perhaps while cooped up or when Pill:, in a basket. Treatment :—Bathe the parts web-, *f hot water to which has been added a table-spoonful. mustard. Then dry and rub well with embrocatil. During the time give them an occasional tonic Pi „ Feed well on the best soft food mixed with cut-gree- bone.</p>
        <p>VERMIN. ' .11 Lice, fleas, and red-mite are the three insects willt;a infest poultry. Now where fowls are so infected 'tat', very detrimental to their growth and usefulness. occasional overhauling will always repay for 11 extra trouble. Keep the dust bath wie,r mixed with insect powder. Powdered is also also a good insectiside and should P-5 used in the dust-bath. Poultry houses, posts and walls should be treated with hot /wash and parrafini, I cannot urge my readers strongly oa these points. Fowls are • constande'r allowed to remain in such dirty conditions. No will: og they do not pay. On these yery hot days (sc'eth. waited for) it would pay to give every fowl a "gike It would necessitate the services of two people. :11`, a washing tub of warm water, well diluted ynt,;ol good 'disinfectant. Then give every bird a EV:MUT so as to kill the germs. Then place ctr. table near by and rub over with, an old towel. scour wards place the birds in a run where the sun dry them. I am confident that if this process relies carried out,. together with a thorough cleansing 0-1" a houses, etc.. it would pay. Be careful in selectiaet suitable day. Have them all done in time i° - thorougly. dry before evening. I would not reeet:ll, mend you to repeat the process often. I should s‘bl.le that one thorough cleaning as. suggested would th, sufficient. My contention is that this side of season's work is neglected. And if this could be do„ it would help the birds in getting into " tiFt°,,Peat condition before winter. The fowls would do t, 0; it. and the egg-basket would bathe best judge.-Ir.' coNsmirrtos. of This disease generally exists where a large number t fowls are kept. I mean of course when they are Ake,pg wI, under- unhealthy conditions, such as overcrolio neglecting the destruction of vermin and so on. symptoms are eating a great deal, yet not fattepit iTo going light and hanging about. When such ltri nOticed I would advise you to kill them off. Feu' 13 farmers should aim at preventing this terrible disea bt. by constant attention to details in managelliell "Prevention is better than cure." CATARRH or common cold is caused through undue exposure,b°c; draughty houses, and want of proper ventilation..id,.; experienced most trouble through the cold east The old saying "it is neither good for man nor Abt;j." could be very well supplemented with " nor ig If catarrh is not arrested it brings on roup sru'e dis- still more difficult to cure. The symptoms are d charges from the nostrils, dulness of the eye, 'try general mopy attitude. Treatment :—lt is neeesfibis to give a stimulant in the form of roup pills. With will help to ward off disease. Bathe the head ss very warm water and Condy's Fluid. CHOLERA. ltrY This about the most terrible disease that pou heir to. When once they get the germs only 8,11:0 days elapse and the worst stage is noticeable. 'J.o 0, it is incurable. It is all a question of preventioff Therefore if any of the stock are smitten kill theinii a and bury at at once in quick-lime. You can.,te by fowl with cholera by the green fluid it passes, al s° the sleepy look and terrific thirst. Every part .11 anatomy becomes inflamed and the sooner you kill better. ACCIDE NTS. de fl It may seem bomewhat inconsistent to JflClgos chapter on accidents under the heading of disea'all and ailments. However it is more convenient for _to practical purposes to deal with it here. frequently happen where a number of fowls are k'efes Comb tad lobes get torn through fighting, sometinara through getting fast in the wire netting. E'estoes often injured through the same cause. Feet and .45 get cut with broken glass. In cases of broken .wi-for or broken legs it is advisable to kill and dressoa table purposes. But many of the minor accidents 100 be cured. I cannot speak too highly of the v 3 0 of Condy's Pluid in cases where accideno happen. Frequent bathing with this fluid in water generally completes a speedy remedy. In -o elusion, I have not dealt with any disease, but 1.05:5 I. have mentioned are the chief. ones. Other distre- are generally coherent with those mentioned. r-17"--„05 fore, the remedy for them is implied in the rail- to ." treatments recoimnended. I want my read tha clearly understand that if proper care is taken jLI. MANAGEMENT fi of the stock, the risk of disease is redticd e - _</p>
        <p>minimum. Pardon/me for repetition. But ele'!):1,1 ness, good food, grit and pure water are the neces essentials for the well-being of your stock.</p>
