      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>Moore. ICCMENT TO A GOODS TRAIN.—As the 5.45 p.m. St. t London express goods train was proceeding at,toween Acton Grange and Moore on Thursday evening .ut ?23 a portion of the train left the track owing, r ..,.supposed, to the axle of en e of the wttggcni eon."`"ng• The permar.ent Wr.l S clamngid for a heasiderable distance by the broken waggon trailing th",°re it ultimitely left the rails whilst travelling over 04; l‘ater troughs just t eyond Moore Station. Three • el' Waggons mere also derailed. The station officials •sed the occurrence ani caused both lines to be *aftected immediately anal a breakdown gang was on Scene in a very short time., Fortunately it was tr-111,12.4 that the down line was not blocked and the wt. t Was run on the single lite until about 11 o'clock the other line was again clear.</p>
        <p>Risley. irscLUR GALA DAY.—The Friendly Society of the Yal Order of Oddfellows "City of Refuge' which is e'.(l at the Noggin Inn, held their Club Gala Day on 13aturday, when an excellent turn out of the club 14enlhers with horses made a very pleasing spectacle 41,the country lanes. The route taken this year was via ~..rcqs-lane through Croft round Newahurch returning .7: the Noggin Inn about six o'clock where an excellent 4711er had been prepared for 60 persons. The proces- ipl was headed by eleven horses decanted in a very th.easing manner after which came Leigh Borough Band, ,tp the President and Vice-president, Mr. Peter Potter ,llr. Charles Jackson, in their full regalia of white Vwns and naked swords, representing punt.) and justice. ari followed the club members decked with sashes er Itz`l which the older members bought up the rear in bjgcnettes provided for the purpose. Thin( en new 111ber5 were inducted after dinner had Leen partaken A. new feature in the shape of prizes for the best tf:4ed and cleanest horses brought forth keen competi- lintl as it was difficult to say which was the best. nti:eyer, Mr. John Wood, jun., carried off the first pe—e, Joseph Whittle, 2nd ; Char:es Harrison 3rd: ter Noon, 4th ; and Tom Barlow, sth. A splendid Ith `14,2 favour.ed the proceedings and the festival concluded dancing.</p>
        <p>Sankey and Penketh. t,P.II2.DLEns' FERRY SAILING CLUB.-The fourth race t, the sea-on was sailed on Saturday afternoon over t,ge usual 25 mile course from Fiddlers' Ferry to New .TrY for prizes presented by the Commodore, Mr. eo °anis Bennett. The weather was everything that be desired, a strong north-west breeze blowing, 44,n the following boats started over the line :-lone, li1:1 (Thompson and Knowles); Nina,4.25 (J.Whittaker); ufda, 4.25-30 (Warburton Brothers) ; Thelma, 4.25.30 t„ Warburton); Great Orme, 4.25-35 (W.Lloyd);Maggie, (S. Aleacock). The lone early established a strong th4.'4, which she maintained until the finish, Maggie el*niug a good second with Great Orme third. Thelma ctire,u.ncled in the Ferry reach trying to steal a march by it:ting across a bank, whilst Hilda grounded off Hale ti_ad through not keeping the channel. Corrected 'Ares at finish with allowances:-lone. 7hr.l3mln. tsec.; Atztlie, 7hr. 92min. •)Osec.; Great Orme, 7br. 29min. q;,oanmel Rustage was the acting officer for the day, net Mr. W. Bennett acted as timekeeper. it4RDEN PARTY.-The President of the Wesleyan ;;L,erk's Bible class (Mr. C. E. Parker) gave the members itZ,their wives and friends a cordial invitatinn to a ert.:len party at Penketh Lodge on Saturday. The party e4,l7„ed at 3 o'clock and were warmly welcomed by Mr. le „all's. Parker. During the afternoon '(Tames of croquet, &amp;c., were indulged in, and ats o'clock in.,Party to the number of 70 sat down to a first class 1‘....he and fork tea. Afterwards a mixed cricket match -Q Played which afforded much amusement to the 4ctstorB. Mrs. Parker and the Rev. E. J. Gawne, who I'a3 elle of the party, were selected captsins. Mrs. ttirlicer's side won by 48 to 34 runs. Rounders and other games were continued by the visitors, whilst era enjoyed walking round the grounds. A most Jr,)Yable function was brought to a close about th° dock, when the Rev. E. J. Gawne proposed a vote a A/:ake to Mr. and Airs. Parker, for their hospitality. tar; Davey-Smith seconded and Mr. J. J. Foden, arc:e- rn, of the class, supported the vote which was cordially Air. and Airs. Parker responded and said it 447 given them great pleasure to entertain the class ear they hoped they would all be spared to come next P.IMROE LEAGUE FETE.- Beautiful weather %Zebred the annual demonstration in connection with Sankey and Penketh Habitation of the Primrose -,7ague which was held in the Vicarage Field, Sankey, on ;Aqui-day afternoon. The programme included athletic I)?tte, which resulted as follows :- 0 Yards (Youths under 15).-1, J. Tabberer ; 2, W. /; -3, W. Rigby. IL7 Yards (Girls under 15).-1, E. Garnett; 2, M. Zard, 3, S. Leigh. •,- 'jtr,•••=o Yards Handicap.-1, E. Glover; 2,T. Sparks; 3, Yards 38° Yards Sack Race.-1, J. Stewart; 2, S. Harrison; c,a• halley. 2cllladron Rer...e.-Mr. Woods' team. 1.1,3 Yards Egg and Spoon Race.-1, AL Davenport ;2, Ott; 3, M. Howard. ag-of-War.-Air. Welsby's team. t.`un Yards Handicap.-1, T. Derbyshire; 2, T. en • , nington ; 3, T. Silvester. enrried Ladies' Race.-1, Mrs. Lester; 2, Mrs. ;elhorn ; 3, Mrs. Field. 0 Yards Handicap (Open to" Newton Division).-1, 'it'ennington ; 2, E. Glover; 3, T. Sparks. 3, alf-taile Handicap.-1, S. Harrison; 2, J. Stewart; riS. Bingham. e„ntB' Committee Skirt Race.-1, 0. Johnson; 2,3. ,land. 3, 3. Brockleburst. ,Ladies' Committee Race.-1, Miss Clarke; 2, Miss lll• 3, Miss Fi irclougb. LPlow Bicycle Race.-1, E. Gregory; 2, T. Cash; 3, J. .vesev 11 1;13.8 Potato Race.-1, W. Rigby; 2, H. Goulden ;3, 'abbe rer. A 're- Owen distributed the prizes. lorjirt political meeting was presided over by Council- 41Pulton-Smith and the principal speaker was Lord Inet, the Conservative candidate for the division. hB4O contended that the Chancellor of the Exchequer liti‘ti rushed the Liberal Government into Socialism, that which the workingman would have more to lose ttil anyone else. vote of thanks was passed 'to Lord Wolmer on the cijuiPosition of Mr. Robert Pierpoint seconded by Sir th,,bert Greenall, Bart. The Chairman was thanked on It,ol,9tion of Mr. Bottomley seconded by the Rev. J. iktt dOties and supported by Mr. Owen. One of the tactiots of the afternoon was a sketch entitled</p>
