      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Examiner: Saturday, August 14, 1909</head>
        <p>The Examiner: Saturday, August 14, 1909</p>
        <p>"Tariff Reform v. Free Trade" by the Earlestown Primrose League Sketch Party. The Penketh Tannery Prize Band played selections. BEGGAR'S INGENIOUS STORY.—At the Warringto Occasional Court, yesterday, before Colonel Buckton, James Nolan 14, Lincoln-street, Warrington, was charged with begging on the 12th August at Penketh.— Mr. C. E. Parker said he heard of the case of begging. The man had repotted that his wife was dead and a lady in Penketh has given him ss. and started a book for the purpose of getting money to bury his wife. Witness suspected it was a false story. Prisoner came to his house and told him that he wanted about 14s. to put him in a position to bury the woman. Witness sent for the police and was arrested.—P.C.Houghton said he bad received complaints from several people about the man going about getting money. He was a single man and lived in Warrington, and not in Angel- yard, Penketh as he stated.—Prisoner said he was very sorry he had done such a thing, but he bad been out of work for a long time and he had got a widowed mother to keep. The landlord said that if he did net find the rent he would turn them out.—The Chairman: If you had said you wanted work and had not told them that you wanted to bury your wife, it would have been different. It is a very serious matter.—There was a record against prisoner and only as recently as June he was sent to gaol for one month on a charge of false pretences.—Prisoner was sentenced to 14 days' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>meat for a term of two or three years, on such terms as may be mutually agreed upon, pending the provision of other accommodation by the Council.—lt was further resolved that the whole question be referred to the above-named sub-committee, and that the Medical °dicer of Health and the Surveyor be requested to visit Isolation Hospitals and to fully consider plans (to be prepared by the Surveyor) for the erection of a hospital in .this district.—The minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>. Hugh Ferguson, J. Rogerson, John Warburton (Seiforth), T. Ball, William Houghton. J. L. Jones (Moore), Arthur Wright.(Padgate), NV. R. Earp, NV. Monks (Moore), A. Andrew (Teignmouth), C. NV. Phillips (Daresbury), S. S. Croxton (Woodchurch Farm), E. J. Turton, H. K. Okell and M. Cuthbert Beckett (Landican), J. L. John. son (Preston), Roylance, and Sumner. Colonel Ireland-Blachburne is abroad and was there- fore unable to be present and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes. one of the borough magistrates,.was prevented attend- ing owing to a prior engagement. Miss Bessie Dutton of Moore, also regrets she was unable to attend the funeral owing to absence from home. The funeral arrangements were in the hands of Mr Ralph Johnson, of Itylands-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>GARDEN SOCIETY'S SHOW AND FETE.—The members of the Stockton Heath Garden Society are hoping to have a record show next Saturday in Hill Cliffe Park. The arrangements which have been made are excellent, there are a splendid lot of entries and competition is certain to be very keen. Additional attractions will be provided in the shape of a fancy dress parade and sports, and all that seems necessary to secure the large crowds and the success anticipated is the fine weather. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., this yeir's President, has taken a warm interest in the Committee's work, having presided at several meetings and given the Committee the benefit of his valuable and large experience. Lady Greenall will open the show at 3 o'clock. At a quarter to tcso there will be a fancy dress parade assembl- ing at Victoria-square. This will ba headed by the band of the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment (Territorials), which will afterwards play in the Park. Prizes are offered for best fancy dresses, original and comic costumes, &amp;c. There will also be sports on the field, commencing at 3.45. The attractions in the park will include a football kicker, alpine railway, swing- boats, &amp;c., and the Society's Orchestra will play for dancing. Apart from a fine floral exhibition there will be Something to interest and amuse the visitors till dusk. Hill Cliffe Park is easy walking distance of the Stockton Heath car terminus, and is a beautiful place of itself where a pleasant afternoon may be spent. Admission from 2.30 to 4 o'clock is Is., and afterwards 6d., children half-price.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ROYAL STURGEON CAUGHT.</p>
        <p>About ten o'clock on Saturdayl:some Widnes youths who happened to be in the district of West Bank Dock, Widnes, noticed something pecular floating about in the River Mersey in the vicinity of Runcorn Bridge. Pro- curing a small boat they went to the spot from whence they had seen the object, and were greatly surprised to find there a huge. fish. The tide was out at the time, whech rendered their task of capture much easier. Upon closer examination, it turned out to be a royal sturgeon. It was placed in tho boat and taken to the shore, large crowds of people on either tanks of the river watching the proceedings. It was afterwards conveyed to the Mersey Hotel, an I upon being measured it was found to be lit. Bin, in length and 31t in circum- ference. '</p>
        <p>RUNCORN HORTICULI UR %.L AND ORNITHOLOGICAL SOCIETY SHOW.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>The seventh annual show of the Runcorn and District Horticultural and Ornithological Society wai beH in the Plantation Pleasure Grounds on Saturday at rnan. The weather was glorious and there was a record atten- dance. The exhibits were staged in two large marquees. There was considerable increase in the cottage section of horticulture and poultry and bread, and a slight decrease in cage birds and butter and. honey. The quality all round was excellent. The show was opened by Mrs. P. R. Allen. The judges were;—Horticulture, Mr. R. Hanagan,Castle Park gardens, Frodsham ; poultry, the Rev. T. W. Sturgess' Northwich ; cage birds, Mr. C. Houlton, St. Helens; bread and butter, Mr. H. White, Liverpool. A now feature which proved highly attrac- tive was juvenile spot ts. RAILWAY MEN'S PARADE AT WIDNES. WORKERS AND A LIVING WAGE.</p>
        <p>The annual parade rnd service took place at Widnes on Sunday afternoon of the Widnes Branch of the Amalgamated society of Railway Servants. A good muster of members assembled at Peelhouse-lane and marched in procession, headed by St. Mary's Prize Band, to Victoria-road Chapel, which had been kindly placed at their disposal. Mr. Mex Mospratt, J.P., C.C.. took the chair, end Mr. W. G. Loraine, organising secretary, addressed the gathering. The Chairman, in extending a welcome to the men. said that Widnes was a great and important industrial centre, similar in many ways to other centres. It was in certain ways unpleasant, but it had many redeeming points. Ile pointed out that the futility and impossi- bility of any industrial towns prospering with mt the aid of the great industry of transport, of which the railway was the meet important. He ventured to say that there were few ineustries in which men were more liable t,, terrible calamity than the lot of the railway men, and the onus of provision for their offspring should not rest entirely upon the shoulders of the members of the society, but upon all sections of the community. Mr. Loraine said the great fundamental principle of their organ'sation was brotherhood. To improve the lot of railway workers they realised that only by com- bination could they hope to ameliorate the conditions under which they laboured. They had agitated at St. Stephen's for reforms, which had resulted beneti.nally fcr their workmen, as well as the travelling public, whereby it was safer to travel long journeys. A tre- mendous point had been gained by the teduction of the hours of labour. At present 120,000 railway men were in receipt of wages under £1 per week, and they were striving now for a living wage concession. This step had been looked upon as revolutionary, because of the misery a strike would entail: Serious steps were neces- sary to secure for the men a living wage on account of the responsible character of their work. Capital should have a fair return, but he did not believe it should have more than its share at the cost of the loss of life or limb. The Americinising of British railways had brought larger engines, and two men bad to tackle the work of six men in the past. Similar instances were quoted, and it was in tint direction that the society were agitating for reform, which necessitated the spending of a great deal of money. A collection was made towards the fund for the 'widows and orphans.</p>
        <p>In the peaceful and picturesquely situated church- yard at Daresbury the remains of the late Mr. John White, J.P., of Moore Hall, one of Warrington's lead- ing and most highly respected public men,were laid to rest on Tuesday afternoon. The funeral was one of the most impressive ever seen in,this district. All classes, rich and poor alike were there in large numbers to pay their last affectionate tribute to a true friend and counsellor who will be sorely missed. Signs of deep mourning were everywhere apparent, and all the blinds in the village of Moore and Daresbury were lowered. In the early morning there had been a private celebration of the Holy Communion, the Rev Wilfrid Stanton (President of the Wa:rington branch of the English Church Union, of which the late Mr White was for many years the chairman) being the celebrant. The funeral took place at half-past two, but lon., before that time hundreds of people had assembled in the church and the immediate vicinity. As the cortege approached, the church bells rang a muffled peal. At the head of the procession walked many of the tenantry of the, various estates of which the late Mr. White was agent, and following the carriages came the Mayor and members of the Cor- poration of Warrington and representatives of many institutions and public bodies. The coffin, on which rested a large floral cross, was Met at the church gate by the officiating clergy and the surpliced choir, and as . the mourners slowly passed along the aisle the opening sentences were read by the Vicar (Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones). The beautiful hymn "Peace, perfect peace " was then sung, and the psalm" Lord Thou hast been our refuge" was chanted. The concluding hymn in the church was the solemn and impressive one—" Now the labourer's task is o'er." Mr. T. L. Fry was at the organ and as the procession left the church he played Mendelssohn"s " 0 Rest in the Lord" with much expression. The last rites at the graveside having been performed the hymn " On the Resurrection morning " was sung. The churchyard.was bathed in glorious sunshine and as the large congregation stood around the grave and joined in the hopeful strains of the closing hymn • the scene .was one to live long in the memory. The officiating clergy were the Revs. T. T. Lee-Jones (vicar of Daresbury), W. G. Melville (vicar of Sfratford-on-Avon) and J. W. E. Walkei (St. Paul's Middlesbrough) the two last-named being (iod-son§, of the late Mr. White. Other clergy present Were :—the Revs. Canon Morley Stevenson, Hon. C. F. Cross, H. V. Pigot, Wilfrail Stanton, A. F. White. F. Clifton Smith, J. Holden, J. Collins, 'I% A. J. Rid- path, H. R. Wild, J. W. Glendenning, J. Armistead (vicar of Winmarleigh) and B. Clements (vicar of hale.). _ _</p>
        <p>The principal mourners were :—Private brougham, Mrs. Mite and Mr. Seeker; first carriage, Mr. and Mrs. Seeker Walker, Mr. and Airs. J. E. Seeker ; second, Mr. and Mrs. Meson and Mr. H. B. Melville ; third, Mr. and Miss Hilton Ganiner and Miss Ashberry ; fourth, Mr. and Mrs. Opeusbaw and Miss Dorothy Allen ; fifth, the Rev. William Yates and Mr. and Mrs. Herbert Woods ; sixth, four house servants; and seventh, two old servants. The representatives of public bodies and institutions were : Corporation of Warrineton.—The Mayor (Arderman Forshaa), the Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), Alderman James Evans, and Councillors Ashcroft, J. Fulton-Smith, H. Roberts, Rogers, Sankey, Skelland, Steel. Wilson and Vernon; Officials, the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), the Borough Surveyor (Mr. T. Longdin), the Magistrates' Clerk (Mr. George F. F. White), the Markets Inspector (Mr. Elijah Place), the Cemetery Registrar (Mr. Robert Young), the Assistant Clerk to the Magistrates (Mr. W. Parsons). Magistrates.—Messrs. H. Roberts, Charles White, Ziba Armitage, j. W. Hayward, W. H. Meekly, W. Roberts, C. S. Burgess, F. A. Frost, W. Richardson, T. Baxter, 0. Dakin, G. Pilling, James Hepberd and Colonel Edelsten. Warrington Conservative Association.—Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart. (president), Mr. W. Peter Rylandr, Councillor Fulton-Smith, Mr. Segar Owen, Mr. E. J. Hall, Mr. W. Holbrook .(registration agent), and Mr. Herbert Hayes (organizing secretary). Conservative Club.—Councillor Fulton-Smith (chair- man), Councillor Wilson, Councillor Rogers, Mr. H. Collin (secretary), Mr. R. H. Daintith and Mr. W. W. Westwell. Junior Conservative Club.—Councillor G. N. Vernon and Messrs. W. L. Williams, W. C. Brooks, Joseph Taylor, J. .1. Hands. R. Done, R. Bolton and H. Hayes. Infirmary and Dispensary.—Sir Gilbert Greenalli Bart. (president), Mr. W. Peter Rylands (chairman), Mr. George Dakin (hon. treasurer), the Rev. H. V. Pigot and Messrs. Charles White, W. J. Forshaw. Ziba Annitagp, John Fairclough, H. Roberts and J. H. J. Haropson (secretary). The Royal Lancashire Agricultural Society (the Council of which the late Air. White was chairman for ten years).—Mr. Windham, Captain Withington, Messrs. Hale (vice-chairman of the Council), J. J. Hornby, A. S. Robson, Jomes Ray, Stuart Heaton, George Mather, Richard Thornton, Alfred Smetham, C. E.- Linaker, Thomas Smith, William Stuart, Edward Bohan° (secre- tary). English Church Union (Warrington branch).—Rev. Wilfred Stanton (president), Mr. C. S. Burgess (bon. secretary), and Mr. R. Done. Lancashire and Cheshire Provincial Committee of the Surveyors' Institution.—Mr. John D. Wallis (vice- chairman) and Mr. J. Herbert Hall (hon. secretary). Moore Parish Council.—Messrs. Bubext Congreve (vice-chairman), Isaac Lawton and A. do Prez. Office Staff of Messrs. John White and Co.—Messrs. R. M. Swinton, H. Leece, W. T. Nickles, C. R. Bell and T. O'Neill.</p>
        <p>_ Messrs. Henry Greensll and Co.—Mr. E. J. Hall and Mr. F. G. Taylor. Messrs. H. B. White and Sons.—Mr. George F. F. White and Mr. W. Parsons._ _ . .</p>
        <p>The tenantry pr esent included :—The Walton estates of Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Lower, Walton, Messrs. William Beswick, William Turner, William Weir, Henry Dodd, J. Whitfield, Williams and Green; Higher Walton, Messrs. Alfred B:oady, John Cooper, Joseph Muskett, A. Moulsdale ; Appleton, Messrs. Joseph Hall and T. Blackshaw ; Dareshury, Messrs. J. Acton, Joseph Billington, Alfred Hall, C. W. Phillips, James Ryder and (Thomas Warburton; Hatton, Messrs, C. Blackshaw' William Blackshaw, James Dooley, T. H. Fairhurst, William Fowles, A. E. Weir' Henry Mawdsley; Jams Robinson, R. L. Warburton, and Thomas Whitlow, Newton-by-Daresbury, Messrs. Eaton Brethert on, Richard Cawley, Thomas Cawley, J. C. Edgerley, Daniel Fowles, William Hodgkinson, Harry Horton' Peter Moore, John Moore, and William Thomason; Preston Brook, Messrs. S. Basnett. Alfred Brcome, W. R. Harp, George Mounfleld, J. C. Priestley, Joseph Spruce,Peter Tickle, Harry Taylor. Joseph Whitlow, W. Whitlow, John Whitlow; Acton Grange,Messrs. James M. Basnett, T. D. Basnett, William Beswick, jun., Frank Birch, J. K. Priestner .; also Messrs. J. F. Jackson, William Yarwood, Edward Beckett, Charles Goves, and a large number of workmen on the Walton Estate. Rixton Estate of the Marchioness of Eleadfort.— Messrs. James Robinson, James Blundell, P. Hankinson, J. Hindley, George Higham. Other tenantry present •included Messrs. Ralph Tickle, Joseph Pennington and James Parkinson, Appleton; luah Darbyshire and Aaron Sankey, Stretton ; Charles Hodgkinson, Cronton ; John Cald- well, Warrington. Amongst others present were :—Mrs. Lyon Mr. Robert Pierplint, Mr. E. W. Griffith, Mr. W. Bainbridge, Lieutenant lreland-Blackburne, Mr. John Ireland- Blackburne, Drs. Langdale, Peacocke, and Bower, and Messrs. Colin Mac her, J. J. Whitley, Veto B. Davies, R. 1. Wynne-Jones, A. Lister-Kaye; Herbert Hatton, John Coxon, E. Draper, D. Garnett, W. H. J. Jenkins, W. Stone, H. Broadbent, Wilfred Bleckly, Frank Tinsley, J. Tinsley, E. G. Davies, L. ft. Rowsen, C. D. Parkins3n, Arthur Dun (son of the late Mr. John Dun), T. Ridgeway, E. Ridgeway, T. E. Greenall (reniesenting his father, Mr. Henry Greenall), G. R. Williamson (representing his father, the Rev. T. Pym Williamson), William Johnson (formerly chief clerk and cashier for Messrs. John White and Co.), James Bit-di, C.C. (Sefton), tell*</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>LORD WOLMER AND THE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>Lord Wolmer, the Conservative candidate for Newton-le-Willows, addressed a meeting there on Saturday afternoon. He said that Mr. Lloyd-George's Budget signalised the fusion of the Liberal and Labour parties. The whole force behind the Budget was Socialistic; the Liberal party had at length taken the plunge into the Socialist abyss. What had become of the old Liberal traditions? The whole trend of present-day Liberal statesmanship was to pull down and destroy the buildings of the old school of Liberalism— the schcol of Gladstone. Against all old traditions, against all expert economic opinion, Mr. Lloyd-George had formulated his Budget proposals; he had taken up a position unjustifiable from any standpoint but that of Socialism. The Conservative party, Lord Wolmer went on,did not object to having to pay taxes for the upkeep and defence of the country. They said that the greatest weight of taxation should be borne by the broadest shoulders. But the Government in trying to tax the rich man taxed the poor man as well. Their land tax fell on the poor as well as the wealthy, both directly and indirectly. Statistics showed how far-reaching was the interest that various democratic institutions, building societies, and the like had in the land of the country. These would be affected directly by the tax. Besides th s the industry of the country, wh'ch was the poor man's concern as well as the rich man's, would be affected injuriously. Summing up the Conservative objection to the Budget, the speaker said, "it. taxes the rich man in a manner financially unsound and the poor man in a manner morally unjust." He went on to point out ways in which the foreigner might be made to bear the weight of taxation. NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Thompson presided at the monthly meeting of this authority held at Earlestown Town H.ll on Monday afternoon. There sere also present, Messrs. C. B. F. Bcrron, E. T. Evans, T. Ritchie, W. Collingwood Messrs. C. Cole (clerk), and R. T. Surtees (water engineer).—At a meeting of the Water Committee, a letter ‘‘ as read from the Local Government Board stating, with reference to the Council's proposal to appropriate as an open-air bathing place, the cooling pond at the water pumping station, that they would instruut one of their .inspectors to visit the locality for the purpose of investigating the proposal. - The sub-committee appointed to meet the Health Com- mittee of the Warrington Corporation, at the latter's request, to reconsider the terms of the agreement for the reception and treatment of persons from this dis- trict suffering from infectious disease, reported that they were inquiring into the cost of erecting and main- taining a hospital _sufficient for this district.—A letter was read from the Corporation of Warrington giving formal notice that after the 3lst October next the agreement between that Corporation and this Council for the reception of patients in the Warrington Hospital would be terminated, and it was resolved that the War- rington Corporation be asked to enter into a new agree-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>STRANGE ST. HELENS CASE. PRISONER CONMIT rED FOR TRIAL. Patrick Dolan, of Russell-street, was again brought before the St. Helens magistrates on Tuesday on the charge of breaking into the house of William Wharton, 6, Lawrenson-street, and assaulting Mrs. "Wharto'h. The man had been remanded twice.—The evidence was that on Saturday tight, the 31st ult., Dolan took out the scullery window at Wharton's house, placed it in the yard, took Off his boots, end went upstairs. Although the woman was in bed with her husband and two children, the prisoner extinguished the candle burning in the bedroom and attempted to assault the woman. When she was awakened by the prisoner she screamed, and when her husband jumped up Dolan slipped under the bed. He was pulled out by Wharton, who banged him about, pulled him downstairs head first, andhanded him over to the police. Dolan made a series of charges against Mrs. Wharton, and said he bad a witness to prove that she had arranged to meet him. During the remand the evidence of this man was obtained, and this negatived all Dolan had said. When read to him, Dolan said : "I plead guilty, and withdraw what I have said against the woman."—He was committed to the assizes for trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>LATE MR. JOHN WHITE Impressive Funeral at Daresbury.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. JOHN WHITE Impressive Funeral at Daresbury.</p>
        <p>THE WREATHS.</p>
        <p>Floral tributes were sent by the following :—The Council of the Royal Lancashire Agricultural Society, Mr. Philip Park, Mr. and Mrs. H. Greenall, Mrs. Ridgway, the Choir of Daresbury Church, Rev. E. M. Walker and family, Mr. and Mrs. Z, Armitage, Mr. and Mrs. J. L. Jones, Mr. and Mrs. A. de Prez' Mrs. M. Armstrong, the Moore Mothers Mr. and Mrs. W. Weekly, Mr. and Mrs. Bullock, Mi.. and Mrs. H. Woods, Miss Bayley, the Misses Gardner and Mrs. Siddall, Mr. and Mrs. War! urton, Lady Headfort, Rev. W. J. and Mrs. Melville, Mrs. Rylands' Mr. and Mrs. W. Peter Rylands, Hon. Mrs. Harbord, Miss Woods and Miss E. B. Woods (Worthing), Miss Ryder, Mrs. %V. Mann, Hon. Colonel and Mrs. Lumley, Mr. and Mrs. Cyril Greenall. Mr. and Mrs. Hilton Gardner Miss Dorothy Allen, Mr, and Mrs. Finlay Dun Colonel and 111 rs. Edelsten, Mrs. G. Bruce Findlay, Hale; Rey. aud Mrs. Pym Williamson, Mr. and Mrs. A. Reynolds,Mr.'and Mrs. A. Bleckly, Mr. Harold and Miss Litton,lllr. and Mrs. John Coxon, Mr. and Mrs. R. I. Wynne-Jones, Miss G. and Mr. J. I. Blackbuine, Mr. Thomas Heald, Mr. Heywood Bright, the servants and employes at Hale Hall, Colonel and Mrs. Ireland Black rune, Rev. David M. and Mrs. Melville, Mr. and Mrs. Edward Griffith, Mrs. Millington, Hale Cottage Tenantry, Hale Tenantry, Mr. and Mrs. Vere Davies, Landican and Woodchurch Tenantry, Miss Milner, Rey. F. Clifton Smith, Mr. and Mrs. Ashberry, Members of the Office Staff, the Workmen on the Walton Estate, Rev. T. T., Mrs. and Miss Lee-Jones, Mr. John Wright and family, Miss Massy, Miss Andrew, Mr. and Mrs. T. 'Walker (Chapelthorpe), Mr. and I Mrs. NV. H. Seeker, Mr. and Mrs. Seeker Walker, Mr. and Mrs. J. E. Si cker, Miss Andrew, Members and Committee of the Warrington Conservative Club, Dr. and Mrs. Reginald Smith, Mr. H. B. Melville and family, Rev. W. G. Melville, Mr. and Mrs. J. J. Whitley, Rev. R. M. and Mrs. Stapylton, the Con- servative Association, Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall Mr. Gilbert and Mr. Edward Greenall, Mr. and Mrs., Bleckly, Rev. L. J. T. and Mrs. Darwall, Mr. and Mrs. Andrew, Hon. Miss Wilson-Patten, Hon. Miss Elizabeth Wilson-Patten, Mr. and Mrs. C. Higson,Messrs. Charles, lames and Frank Tinsley, Mr. and Mrs. E. Gaskell Davies, The IN ininarleigh and Wateby tenantry, Mr. T. Beesley, Mrs. Joseph Harrison and Miss Atkinson, Rev. H. and Mrs. Lester, Miss B. Dutton, Rev. E. C. E. and Mrs. Carleton, Mrs. Green and family (Lower Walton), Miss Agnes Woods, Mrs. and the Misses Broadbent, I Mr. and Mrs. Burder, Mr. and Miss Hilton Gardner,l Scholars of St. Augustine's Guild, Mrs. Hicks (Warrington), Maids of the Hall and Ann, Mrs. Bsbbing- ten, Mr. and Mrs. T. H. Lyon. Mr. and Mrs. Ilayes, Mr. Melville, Junior Conservative Club, Rixton Tenants, Old Sobolars of St. Augustine's Guild. REFERENCE AT THE COUNOIL MEETING. Before the commencement of the ordinary business at the meeting of the Warringtcn Town Council on Tuesday. The Mayor said he bad to announce the death of one, of their most esteemed townsmen, Mr. John White, who, they were aware, died suddenly in his office on Friday last. The name of Mr. White had been associated with the public work of the town as long as many of the members could remember. He was one of the senior magistrates having been appointed in the year 1879, and since that date be bad given much of his time in the administration of justice. Mr. White won the respect and esteem of all, and even those stile differed from him in politics could be counted as his personal friends. He was one of Warrington's most worthy sons and his regard for the town and all its associations was apparent to all who knew him. They could ill spare such men, .acd as Mayor he wished to express on behalf of thern town the great respect in which Mr. White was held, and its great regret at the loss of one of its oldest and best friends, lie begged to move :—" That this Council receives with deep regret the announcement of the death of John White, Esq., J.P., and desires to record the less which the town has sustained by his death, and that the Town Clerk be requested to convey to the widow of the late Mr, John White an expression of the sincere sympathy of the Council with her in her sad bereavement." The DEPUTY-MAYOR, in seconding, said he had keen associated with Mr. White ever since be (Alderman Smethurst) entered the borough, and had always found in him a good, kind, and esteemed friend. TLere were thousands of people who could speak of kindnesses received at the hands of Mr. White. He had left a name behind him which would be long remembered, and by his death Warrington had lost a noble and worthy son and good man. (Hear,thear.) Alderman JA-siEs EVANS, in supporting, said it was only on the previous Wednesday that he shook hands with Mr. White and bad a few minutes' conversation with him at Bank Quay Station. There was then no indication that War ringten would so soon sustain such a loss. He bad known Mr. White over 33 years, and had seen him in his daily walk of life and his public life. He was a man to be admired ; lie could always be relied upon' and the town was the poorer by hi i deatb. (Hear, hear). Councillor PEMBERTON said Mr. White was one of the first gentlemen with whom he became acquainted when he came to Warrington. His experience of that gentleman was thst he was a very lust man who was always willing to help anyone. He personally had received many kindnesses from him. He had never appealed to him in vain, and it was with very great regret that, he had beard of his sudden death. Councillor FULTON-SMITH said Mr. 11bite held a unique position in the town, and his characteeas an upright man caused him to be looked up to. His work was such that it would be esteemed as long as they lived. (Hear, hear). The resolution was passed unanimously, the Council standing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>DARESBURY CHURCH.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Preaching at Daresbury Church on Sunday morning, the Vicar (the ,Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones) alluded to the great loss the community, the parish, and the Church had sustained by the death of Mr. John Whiie. Prominent in every good work connected with this dis- trict, he was above all, a faithful, loyal and devoted son of the Church, and a great example as a Christian man. Fur many years he was a churchwarden of that church, and to the last full of zeal for its welfare and the rendering of its services in the beauty of holiness. It was onlyThalf an hour or so before be passed away that he was practising the hymns for that service. He had now gone from the corruptible to the incorruptible. Their loss was his gain ; yet while they mourned his loss.they thanked God for the beauty of his exemplary life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>REFERENCE BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD,</head>
        <p>REFERENCE BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD,</p>
        <p>Speaking at the prize distribution in connection with the final of the Warrington Works Bowling Association on Saturday, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., said he would like to take that opportunity of expressing the grief and sorrow that he felt on hearing the news that they had lost a very good old friend of all of them in Warrington—a gentleman opposed all his life to his (the speakers') father and in more modern times to himself, in politics, but who was always one of the best. personal friends of both of them. He referred to Mr. julm White whose death they deeply regretted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>MAGISTERIAL TRIBUTES.</head>
        <p>MAGISTERIAL TRIBUTES.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on S.Aturday the Chair- man (Mr. J. W. Hayward), said he wished to express the deep loss the town had sustained by the death of a person of the standing of Mr. White, who, be believed, was the father of the Borough Bench. The Clerk: Mr. John Fairclough is an older magis- trate by two or three months.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, continuing, said that Mr. White bad nOt of late attended the Court as regularly as he used to do, but they knew the great work he had done for the town, and big loss seemed to them irreparable. The sympathy of the whole of the Bench went out to Mrs. White iciher sad and sudden bereavement. At the DAresbury Petty Sessions, held at Stockton Heath on Tuesday, Mr. H. Roberts (chairman) said that in his capacity as a justice of the peace for the County of Chester, and County of Lancaster, as well as for the Borough of Warrington, Mr. White had for many years rendered important service. In all walks of life he vats a man whose observance of public duties had been remarkable in many ways. The town of Warrington and the various institutions would I e much the poorer for his death, He expressed the opinion of the whole of the magistrates present when he said how deeply tbey deplored his loss and sympathised with the widow and relatives. Mr. T S Steel associated himself with the remarks made by the Chairman on behalf of the solicitors practising in that Court. He said that Mr. White always listened most patiently to Loth sides of the question before coming to a decision. Mr. F. G. Taylor, (acting magistrates' clerk) and Superintendent Ennron endorsed the remarks of the previous speakers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Duchess of Marlborougb.—During the past season, the Duchess of Marlborough has come forward more than ever before, as a London hostess, several magnificent entertainments having taken place at Sun- derland House. The new ball room, uhich is also the music-room, is a wonderful apartment, so also is the dining room, with its panels of unpolished oak, and walls inlet .with portraits of former Duchesses of Marlborough. The present Duchess is a most delightful and perfect hostess and despite her immense wealth, one of the simplest dressers. She possesses jewels which crowned heads might envy, yet she wears few gems, while the famous Vanderbilt pearls—her father's wedding present—are rarely taken out, save for the necessary airing. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Wedding Modes.—A number of bridesmaids bas varied at fashionable wedding daring the past London seascn, but compared with last year it may be said to</p>
        <p>have increased, eight, ten, and even twelve "maids" having frequently been chosen. Wreaths and veils have led for these charming damsels, but it may be questioned whether they have proved as becoming to the fresh young faces, as big shady hats crowned with flowers. With regard to brides, althoueh the heavy lace veil has invariably been donned, it has usually been draped about the dress, instead of worn over the face, the tulle veil, so much more comfortable to wear, and so very much more becoming, having been substituted. Court trains have been quite out of favour among brides. Those who did not anticipate being presented at court dispensed with the arrangement, and contented themselves with the ordinary train cut long. And those who con- templated the honour of "passing the presence" had their train built up for the occasion, but did not wear it when appearing at the altar. Useful and Popular Wiaps.—For autumn holiday wear, nothing in the way of wrap is likely to prove more desirable than the knitted coat. All colours are to be had, but white and cream are undoubtedly the wiser choice, as they can be worn over any skirs and blouse, and again a journey through the tub turns them into new again. Care of course is necessary dining the process of washing, and also that of drying. The coat should be plunged into warm soapy water, in which it should be well doused and rubbed. When quite clean, it should he squeezed as tightly as possitde—not wrung , —and rinsed two or three times in clear warm water. Then squeezed, rolled in a cluth, well-pressed, and finally ' hung in the wind to dry. The best plan is to harg it by the skirts, and in such a manner as not to pull it out of shape, and if a dress-stand is available, it should be placed upon it to finish drying, always provided that the dummy is first dressed in a white slip to prevent any colour coming from it. The sery latest an ong these twitted coats are built with loose open stitch, that provides for ventilotion while yet supplying warmth, boast a useful roll collar, and some delightful pockets, Quite long specimens too have appeared, but save for motoring or yachting, the short or three- quarter varieties are more convenient and useful. Sea-Bathing Lessons.—Sea-bathing has become so popular with the up-to-date girl, that there must surely be aia opening for the professional woman swimming instructress at the popular holiday resorts, Since the old Victorian bathing woman has disappeared, no one has taken up her occupation on the beach on modern lines. Yet there must be plenty of beginners among the summer visitots at all the popular resorts, who would pay a small fee for a courseof swimming lessons. -There are many useful hints which a swimming mistress could give her pupils, such as the management of the hair, the length of time to stay in the water, -besides instruction in the quickest way to attain the confidence and movements of the swimmer. As a rule the amateur atthough a thorough master of petition, is not a very apt teacher of the art of swimming, and many would find professional instruction very much more helpful. The Too-Superior Nurse.—The professional nurse is a very different individual from what sbe was in Dickens' time, but borne nursing, if it is adequate and efßcient, is stiil to be preferred to professional services, which have been so much praised at the recent medical and nnrses' congresses. Nnrsing homes are just as much a necessity as hospitals, but as private concerns they are run to make money—and lack many of the comforts, the consolations, and the advantages that one parts with if forced to leave home. Then again, when the trained nurse is called in it is-often found that she is a little at ove her calling, and wants a maid to wait on her. There is, of course, no trouble about this in a large house with a staff of servants, but to the middle class housewife w;th one or two domestics, the pro- fessional nurse who is ,above making up the fire, or boiling a kettle of water, is an expensive and not very helpful experiment. Utilising Wini Power for the Home.—The applica- tion of wind power for doing the work of the house is a posibility opened out by a firm of electrical efigineers who are providing self-contained plant for this purpose. It is obviously more suitable for the country than for the towns as some epen space is necessary ter the fixing . of the wind turbine, which generatef electricity and charges a storage battery. This provides supply during intervals of calm. and it is provided with a device for checking over-charging. From the storage accumu- lators, which of course vary in size according to the capacity of the plant, sufficient current is drawn to do ell the lighting, heating, and cooking for the house. There is TIO doubt that sooner or later domestic beating and cookirg will all be done by electricity, and the id a is the more fascinating if we are able to produce this on the premises, saving not only coal bills, but all the dirt and work which bringing coal into the house involves. .</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>AGENTS in all Towns. London: KING, Clapham X, S.W. HnU : CROFT, Carr Lane. Swans are 4-fole, all shapes.</p>
        <p>SIZES 13 18-• -7:: DEPTHS 2:T.3" 111111111.10 rSPO law tyORl - AVENUE "P'AN Coalwle -PEMBROKE</p>
        <p>HERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad Truss never gets hard and is effectual in most difEcult cases. For slight cases the new Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable, is strongly recommended.— WHITE &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw st., opposite top of Bold st.,Liverpoul.</p>
      </div>
