      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Warrington Lodging Houses.</head>
        <p>Warrington Lodging Houses.</p>
        <p>Sir,- "Fiat Lu-x's " diatribe against the prevailing system or lack of it, of supplying and managing the 3r.c sof sheltering persons detached from the ordinary , ads of liVing seems to meto claim the thoughtful :deration oPus all. The fact that we do not all : hem does not exempt us from doing so, and I it is to the interest of the town in general that V. 'iould have an efficient lodging house accommoda- tion. From limited personal observation and to judge by Fiat :Lux's " letter we do not seem to have . a rational system. They do not seem to provide the maximum of what should be in the way of hygienics or tend to elevate the morals of their residents or help to make the town a healthier place to live in. Such places are a menace to the community as they tend towards the manufacture of hooligans, drunkards and much persons who are a constant source of trouble to the town in general. Unhygienic dwellings provide their quota to the generation and dissemina- tion of disease and death, and on these grounds we ought to safeguard ourselves. The next point to consider is ways and means, "Fiat Lux" advocates a more strenuous and vigilant action upon the part of the inspectors, which seems to me altogether inade- quate. Like most of the commercial undertakings, evasion of the arms of justice is carried on to the point of being a fine art, and I believe if we doubled the number of our eagle,eyed searchers for offenders of public propriety we should be no nearer the solution of abolishing the evil. The function of the Town Council in this matter ought to be one of providing an up-to-date central lodging-house under their owner- ship and supervision, and in doing this they would only be following the example of other towns. The innovation, I feel confident,. would be a success from all points of view, even including the vulgar accepted test of financial gain. A Well-equipped estab- lishment with efficent management would certainly add to our sum total of health and happiness. Clean beds, -commodious hcilities for bathing and the washing of clothing would add to the health of the occupants and would minimise the possibilities of disease permeatinr, 4le town. The provisions of reading rooms and matter would certainly elevate the morals and educate the minds of this class of people and prevent the multiplication of the progeny of condi- tions described in " Fiat Lux's " letter. if I may indulge a little in idealism I forsee the possibility of such a building adding beauty in otir midst, which. would put into the shade the miserable looking places which boast the name of " model" lodging houses. Alongside the erection of a new place the demolition of the present :houses -could be carried on which in itself would be advantageous. A municipal lodging house should receive the support of all interested in social progress. It is but an extension of a principle already put into extensive practice. It is the alter- native to chaos and anarchy by the fact that it sets up co-operation and organisaticn--the only basis upon which civilisation is possible. Thanking you in anticipation for further indulgence of your space.— am yours truly,</p>
        <p>LABOR OMNIA VINCIT.</p>
        <p>' .., .:41r:-..:-. • - - . . " ' - ''‘'-. de AA 'la . • • ,••• • L r to the •,;...'i if.... 71,1 -f .- - '------'''.;:": -,,.,•_______,.. - .r......t. ~ , , - . _ . _ -</p>
