      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" Man to Man."</p>
        <p>"Man to man" is a melodrama which has had a long run, but its popularity shows no sign of waning and large audiences have acclaimed it with enthusiasm this week. It is brimful of exciting situations and some of the scenes, notably the railway collision, are triumphs of stage craft. There is also a glimpse of convict life and a thrilling escape from prison. Miss Fay Garnet Vayne as Ethel Maythorne and Mr. Edward Lloyd as George Gordon do justice to exacting parts and prove great favourites with the audience. Mr. Clifford Bown is irresistibly amusing as Bill Burkley, the convict, a part which he has played no fewer than 2,000 times. In his case practice has meant perfect and he adds not a little to the life and interest of the play. Mr. W. Fraser Brunner gives a clever portrayal of the unscrupulous Richard Ellerton, and Mr. Charles Coleman is well placed as the Rev. Philip Ormand, a particularly out-spoken parson. The orchestral selec- tions are, as usual, highly pleasing.</p>
      </div>
