      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. JOHN PARKINSON.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. JOHN PARKINSON.</p>
        <p>A Well-known Vocalist</p>
        <p>Many of our readers, and particularly those in lozal musical circles, will deeply regret to hear of the death of Mr. John Parkinson, 44, Lovely-lane, who passed away at his residence on Tuesday afternoon. Mr. Parkinson, who was fifty-one years of age, had been in failing health for about Six months. He was attended by Dr. Naden, and Dr. Williamson, a Manchester specialist, was also consulted. Mr. Parkinson was a Warringtonian, having been born at Wilderspool. Hi s father was the late Mr. James Parkinson, who held a position at the Warrington Gas Works. From boyhoo Mr. Parkinson had been greatly devoted to music as a hobby and he was an acceptable tenor singer. He: had been connected with local church choirs since he was six years of age and he had done service in the choirs of Holy Trinity, St. 'Paul's, and Warrington Parish Churches. For the past fifteen years he had been a member of the Parish Church choir- and he also fulfilled the duties of deputy-organist. His loss will be greatly felt at the church, where he was held in high esteem. Mr. Parkinson had been a member of the Musical Society seventeen years and he took part in several of the recent operas pro- moted by the Opera Committee of that organization. In. addition to music he took Much interest in art and photography and Ile was also an enthusiastic cyclist. For twenty years he had carried on the business of a painter and glazier in Lovely-lane and he was one of the most familiar figures in that neigh- bourhood his genial and kindly disposition gaining him a large circle of friends. Deep sympathy is felt with the widow in her bereavement.</p>
        <p>• , ' • 4,,,0ti,z,‘ • • - - . „</p>
        <p>Photo Parkinson. THE LATE MR. JOHN PARKINSON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral, which took place at St. Thomas's Church, Stockton Heath, yesterday afternoon, was deeply impressive. The first part of the service was held in the Warrington Parish Church, the Rev. T. A. J. Ridpath officiating. The choir was in attendance and the hymns "Now the labourer's task is o'er" and "0 God our help in ages past" were feelingly sung. At the close Mr. Smith, who presided. at the organ, played the Dead March" in "Saul." The last rites at the graveside were performed by the Rev. J. W. Glendenning. The chief mourners were:— Mrs. Parkinson (widow), Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Parkinson, Winsford (brother and sister-in-law). Mrs. Forster (sister), Mr. J. Wilson, Helsel!, (father-in- law), Mr. and Mrs. W. Burton, Lowton, (brother-in-law and sister), Mr. T. Mossop, Bradford, Mr. and Mrs-. W. Nunnerley (cousins), Mr. and Mrs. Bosworth, "%lies Siddall, Ashton-in-Makerfield, Mrs. Lawless, Mr. G. 11. Walker and Mr. J. B. Poole. Amongst others present were: Alderman Button, Messrs. James Harding. W. W. Westwell, A. G. Allen and E. EVaCS. Many floral tributes were placed over the grave, including a beautiful cross from the choir of the Parish Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS. Phillips —Arnold.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS. Phillips —Arnold.</p>
        <p>A wedding of local interest took place at Richmond Baptist Church, Liverpool, on Thursday morning, when Miss Eveline Arnold, youngest daughter of the late Mr. William Arnold, Stockton Heath, Warrington, was married to Mr. Kenneth William Phillips, of Sharrow, Sheffield. The officiating minister was the Rev. J. S. Hughes, Stockton Heath, brother-in-law of the bride; who was given away by her brother (the Rev.. 'William Arnold, Golborne). The bride, who wore a smart travelling robe of amethyst cloth with a picturesque hat to match, was accompanied by her friend, Miss G. A. Hudson, of Waterloo°, as bride+. maid, who wore a pretty white silk alpaca dress with black hat. Mr. Watson, of Eckington, acted as best man. A number of relatives and friends attended to witness the ceremony,, immediately after which the happy couple took their departure for London en roure for Switzerland and Paris, where the honeymoon will be spent. On their return they will take up their residence in Sheffield. They have been the recipients of many beautiful presents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Shepherd—Dobson.</head>
        <p>Shepherd—Dobson.</p>
        <p>There was a pretty wedding at Christ Church, Latchford, on Thursday, when Mr. Alfred Douglas Shepherd, son of the late Mr. A. Shepherd, of Liver- pool, was married to Miss I.izzie (Doris) Dotson, fifth daughter of the late Mr. Joseph Dobson, of Brook House, Lumb Brook, Grappenhall. The Rev. G. C. Briggs officiated. The bride, who was given away by her mother, was charmingly gowned in cream eolienne silk, and carried a lovely shower bouqet of white flowers. She was attended by her sisters, Misses Jessie and Vera Dobson, as bridesmaids. Their dresses were of pale heliotrope silk, and they carried shower bouquets of pink carnations. Mr. Edward Roberts, of Liverpool., discharged the duties of best man. After the ceremony a reception was held at the house of the bride's mother, Black Bear House, Latch- ford. The honeymoon is being spent in S. Wales. Many handsome presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>"PAMPERED PETS."</head>
        <p>"PAMPERED PETS."</p>
        <p>Rev. A. M Mitchell on the Dining-out</p>
        <p>Parson.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell, Vicar of Burtonwood,.is nothing if not outspoken and in this month's issue of his magazine he has some strew, things to say under the beading, " Is a Parson's Life Worth Living ? " "it is true," he says," beyond all question or doubt that not a few of the great army of parsons do live very comfortably, in some cases even luxuriously. Upon some of the hocked the world has nothing to bestow but loving kindness ; they are its darlings, its pampered pets. These are they among the clergy, and they are no inconsiderable body, who enjoy fat and comfortable livings, consort with the gentry and the gentry only., take things quite easy, do nothing more than is absolutely required of them,keeping a curate to do the rough work,' thus leaving themselves free to practise knife and fork Christianity, with entire satisfaction to the stomach and to the great delight of admiring, religious worldings. 'Good diners-out, sherry and port men, after-dinner smokers, bridge-players, whist-drivers. and, in the pulpit all treacle and not a grain of brimstone—these be tbe parsons who, ss doing well to themselves and their children, are well spoken of and are warmly welcomed,' as being most desirable units at feeding functions, summer garden parties, winter house dances, and who, further, are considered indispensable butter- des at bazaars and fancy fairs! "Let the parson refuse to ignore social unrighteous ness, to wink at the cruel enormitie s of the drink traffic, to hobnob and be • hail fellow well met 'with brewers, publicans, gamblers, and tricksters, to bold his tongue concerning the corruption and dodgery of officialism, the ignorant blundering and increasing tyranny of the medical profession! let him refuse to condone sweating, to support and bolster up tradi- tions and customs which rob • the common people' of health and wealth, which undermine the national strength, which abnormally develop the power and capital of vested interests. In one word lot him speak out regardless of consequence, let him be true to God, true to man, true to the dumb animal, and as in a moment, a mighty tempest will be stirred up round about him. . • • "Prating, hypocritical officials, self-complacent libertines,self-righteous roguestwell-veneered scoundrels whose wives, if they are wives indeed, further the work of slander and reputation-murder, will all have a shy, aye, many shies, at the parson who is conscientious enough to scorn platitudes. and bold enough to open his mouth for the downtrodden and the oppressed. . . And yet, thongh his life may be so rough and hard, his state so seemingly deplorable, the parson who is a parson indeed.will assure you his lot is a blessed one, and his life well worth living."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>LADIES' BOWLING MAIN.</head>
        <p>LADIES' BOWLING MAIN.</p>
