      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>MRS. ALLISON DELIVERIN(; x WOOD.</head>
        <p>MRS. ALLISON DELIVERIN(; x WOOD.</p>
        <p>The Ladies' Main is one of the most popular functions of the year in connection with the Cemetery Hotel Bowling Club, but this year's competition, which took place on Thursday afternoon, was of exceptional interest, as the Club was favoured with a visit from the popular President (Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P.) and his wife. Everything conspired to make the affair a huge success, for there was brilliant sunshine through- out the afternoon, and the games were entered into with great enthusiasm, the entries numbering eighty. Mr. Allison's beautiful green was in tip-top condition and when .the handicap was in progress the scene was both animated and picturesque. The greeting " Welcome to our 'President and his wife." was dis- played in prominent positions, and appropriately expressed the sentiments of the gathering, for this was Mrs, Crosfield.'s first visit to the club and there was a natural desire to accord her a`hearty reception. The games began at two o'clock. and some of the ladies proved themselves adepts with the woods. There was an interval for tea which was provided 1)y Mr. and Mrs. Allison in the billiard room, and after- wards play was resumed. Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield arrived about 6.30 and were greeted with warm cheers. Mrs. Crosfield was much interested in the competition and she herself tried her skill with the woods and was applauded for several well-judged efforts. The final of the main rested between Miss Wells and Mrs. Sudlow and while both ladies played well, I,llss ' Wells was superior cn the long distances and won by 7-4. The first ten prizes were awarded as follows : Miss Wells, bracelet; Mrs. Sudlow, table; Mrs. Gatley, e.et of brushcs ; Mrs. Ferguson, get of pans; Mrs. Burke, silver backed hairbrush; Mrs. A. Forster, cushion; Mrs. Wood, ham ; Mrs. Bibby, Indian table cloth ; Mrs. Wilcox, cushion; Miss Drury, blouse. Other prize winners were :—Mrs. Sunderland, Mrs. Wilson, Miss Lockett, Mrs. Allison Mrs. J. T. Whittle, Mrs. Roberts, Mrs. J. E. Harrison, Allison, A. Ball, Miss C. Willett, Mrs. Tilling, Mrs. J. Melbourne, Miss A. Smith, Mrs. Lawton, Mrs. Taylor, Mrs. Rothwell, Mr.?„ J. Forster, Mrs. J. Willett, Mrs. Hill, Mrs. Pennington Miss Wareham, Mr. Cotton), Mrs. Barton, Mrs. Ford, Mrs. Kenwright, Miss Ashton, Miss Littlewood, Mrs. J. Gibson, Mrs. Antrobus Mrs. T. • Bolton;. Consolation prizes :—Miss Ball, Mrs. Scott, Mrs Newton, Mrs. Littlewood, Mrs. Bennion, Mrs. Henshall, Mrs. Mather, Mrs. Ellison, Mrs. Warburton, Mrs. Her- William°, Mrs. Bateman, Mrs. Povey, Mrs. Roughley, Miss Baldwin, Miss Bridge, Mrs. Pt. Woods, Mrs. Garnett, Mrs. Holden, Mrs. Palmer Miss A. Willett. At the close of the main Mrs. 'Crokfield gracefully</p>
        <p>presented the prizes to the winners, and was the recipient of a choice bouquet banded to her by Mrs. Allison.</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSFIELD,in the course of a short speech,thanked Mrs. Allison and the ladies' for their ;Try kind thought in giving his wife a very lovely bouquet. When he was on the old Cemetery bowling green he always felt he was on familiar ground,because he had been there many a time to celebrate many a well-fought match and many a capital victory. But he must say that in all the fifteen or sixteen years during which be had had the pleasure of coming down to the Cemetery Hotel green he had neverlseen such a beautiful gala gathering as they had that day. Besides tl.at, he thought they aught to thank the friends who had come there to give them very beautiful music which had been, he was sure, an addition to the pleasure of all present. He dare say they knew that some of the heroic ancestors of his wife—a few thousand years back—(laughter)—attached a great deal of importance to two things—music and athletics, and therefore it bad been an additional pleasure and satis- faction to her that afternoon to see the charming com- bination they bad had of the two. He hoped they would always continue to combine them and that every year they might have the very great pleasure of seeing their lady friends on that beautiful green. He had only to say in conclusion that be was sure they all wished the Cemetery team all possible success when they went to fight their great battle on Saturday. (Applause). Mr. ARTHUR JONES, in moving a vote of thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Crostleld for presenting the prizes, said that wts Mrs. Crosfleld's first visit to the green, but they hoped it would not be her last. (Applause). Mr. Crosfield's remarks as to her ancestors bad been exemplified that afternoon by the way in which Mrs. Crosfield had attempted to play bowls. He thought she would make a very good player in a short time. (Applause). _</p>
        <p>• Sir. SCOTT (captain of the first team) seconded and the vote was carried with enthusiasm, the company joining in singing "For they're a jolly good couple.' Mrs. CROSFIRLD, in reply said: "Thank you very much for the kind reception you have given me, but I am very sorry to tell you that next year you will have to get somebody else to present the prizes, because I intend to play in the competition, and to win one myself." (Laughter and applause.) During the evening a high-class nuisical programme was submitted by Crusfield's Band and Warrington Male Voice Choir, and it was most appreciatively received. The arrangements were well carried out by a com- mittee consisting of the foltpwing :—Messrs. J. Willett chairman), A. J. Jones, J. Baldwin, A. Scott, R. Roth- well, J. Allison, H. Willeocks, T. Harrison, H. Mather J. W. Kay, J. Lockett, J. E. Harrison, T. Humphries, W. Cottom and J. Wood (hon. secretary.).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>MR. J. A. SEDDON, M.P. The Budget the First Clarion Note of Hope for the Democracy</head>
        <p>MR. J. A. SEDDON, M.P. The Budget the First Clarion Note of Hope for the Democracy</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P., is now on a tour of his constituency, and he addressed an enthusiastic meeting on Thursday evening in the Padgate Church schools. Mr. 3. Ball presided and he was also supported by Arr. J. Burt of St. Helens. _ - - - -</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that whatever we might think about the differences in the outlook and ultimate aim of the Liberal party and the Labour party, at present certain policies, methods, and proposals were being pressed forward by the Liberal Government with which the Labour party found itself in entire agreement, because those proposals were intended to carry into effect many of the reforms which the Labour party had been striving so long to bring into prominence. He referred more particularly to the methods by which the present Government proposed to raise the necessary funds, not only for matters of Imperial defence but also for the alleviation of poverty by giving to old men and women a greater hope for their old rge, and by giving to the children greater opportunities for making head- way in life. Concluding, he said that the Labour party had advocated that the true property is human life and that all other forms of property should be subservient to that end. (A ppl au re) . Mr. Higham moved end Mr. T. Plumb seconded a resolution in support of the Budget, and also thanking. Mr. Seddon for his advocacy of their aims in Parlia- ment. Mr. J. Burt addressed the meeting, and he said that he was oat to preach a hsly divine discontent against unholy conditions. He greatly deplored the condition of the life of the poor, and maintained that the way to alleviate them was collectivism. Referring to Lord Wolmer's candidature, he asked: "Can any sensible man imagine that a man who has never worked is a fit and proper person to represent workers? What kind of man is this to send into the legislative assembly to deal with problems of poverty ?" Mi. Seddon who was received enthusiastically said that we were coming nearer and nearer to the struggle between the monopolists of the means of life and the people of the country. Mr. Seddon immediately referred to Lord Wolmer's candidature. He said Lord Weimer bad just left the University • whereas he (the speaker) left school at 12 years of age. For eight long years he bad had the opportunity of furnishing his mind with knowledge, and yet be had not learned how to describe himself properly. He had lately sent out a handbill, which said he was "The Right Hon. Lord Weimer." He had no more right to that than he (Mr. Seddon) hid. On that handbill there were some of the most profound fallacies that he had ever struck in his life, even includ- ing the "Daily Mail." Lord Weimer said that under Protection in America there were no workhouses, but of course there were many in England. To that Mr. Seddon replied that in America they had 4,600,000 paupers. In this country last year including England and Wales they were less than a million. If that were the flower of Oxford the sooner they abolished them and sent them to the elismentary schools the better. (Laughter.) Lord Wolmer talked about tariff reform being the salvation of the country. He said that we imported HO millions of manufactured articles, but 78 millions were only partly manufactured. if tariff reform were the harrier to keep out manufactures, how was it that 133 millions found their way into Germany? Tariff reform was not the solution of the unemployed problem. The speaker also praised the proposed Labour Exchanges, and said that the Government would have the power to advance to an unemployed workman means of taking him to the district where tthe re was work for him. The speaker referring to he price of food in Germany said that in July the price per pound of steak was 2s. 3d. Did they wonder that the German workman was physically unfit end the seeds of consumption found a fructifying ground in his constitution ? Lord Weimer said he was a strong navy man. He wanted a strong navy. The speaker proceeded: "I want it in a different way. I want it as strong in the stokehole and in the fo'castle as on the bridge. We shall never get i the best value for our money until the man who s a private can rite to the highest ranks if he has got the ability." Continuing, he said that he welcomed Lord Weimer as an antagonist. He had great sympathy for him. He had got a lot to learn. In justice to Mr. Lloyd-George he might say he had been more generous to the workers of this country than Prince Bulow was to the workers of Germany. Out of sixteen millions to he raised only three-and-a-half millions would fall upon the back of the worker in contrast to the eighteen millions that was being placed upon the workers in Germany. The speaker also referred to the price of beer in London and said it had risen 125. a barrel,and the tax was only 3d. a barrel, therefore that worked out at 45,000 per cent. And then they talked about confisca- tion and robbery! (Applause.) Mr. Lloyd-George had a courage that had never characterized any former Chancellor of the Exchequer. (Hear, hear.) The land taxes might only be pinpoints, but they were pin points that would widen and widen as the days went by. Those men were howling because unearned increment was going to be taxed 20 per ent. He thought if they were wise in their own generation they would pay up and look pleasant. (Laughter and applause.) They had got 80 per cent. left, but if the Labour party-had their way they would reverse it and take the 80 per cent. He would not rest content until every penny of mining royalties either went in wages to the miner, or as revenue to the country. The landlords were going against what was behind the Budget, and not ust what was proposed now. Because they itheught they were going to lose ,their power</p>
        <p>they were fighting the Budget with bitterness. He said" Come on !" He knew who would win in the end. Concluding he said that the Budget was not perfect. It was in the main a demderatic Budget. It was the first clarion note of hope for the industrial army; it opened up a brighter prospect. Labour Exchanges, insursnce against invaliiity, insurance against old age would be made possible by the tapping of those untapped resources of taxation. They would still go on fighting until they put down privilege, monopoly and greed. (Loud applause.) Only one question aas asked. Mr. Wardman asked if it was true that agricultural land was to be taxed or not, Lord Wolmer had said it was.</p>
        <p>_ . Mr. Seddon replied in the negative. The resolution was carried unanimously. A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>MEETING AT PENKETH.</head>
        <p>MEETING AT PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Mr.Seddon addressed another meeting of his constitu- ents in Penketh schoolroont last night. Mr. 0. Faulkner presided over a large attendance. Supporting Mr.Seddon on the platform were Messrs. T. Hamblett, J.P., and J. Haley.</p>
        <p>Mr. Seddon said he welcomed Lord Wolmer. So far as he was concerned he might 1.43 a very amiable young man, but he had demonstrated a profound ignorance on political questions. (Hear, hear.) Even in the printed matter that was circulated on his behalf he had never read anything so crude, anything so inaccurate as the statements that bad been put on the back of the hand- bill that convened Lord Wolmer's meetings. On that handbill there was a statement that in Germany under Protection there were no taxes upon beer and tobacco. As a matter of fact, the Germans Budget for this year had increased its taxes upon beer 115,000,000, the taxes upon spirits ,E 4,000,000 and on tobacco £2,150,000. Continuing the speaker simid that so far as the remission of indirect taxes was concerned, the record of the Liberal Government was far more creditable than that of the Conservative Government. He maintained that the effect of taxes upon the food of the people was to destroy the efficiency of the workers*</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Lymni.</head>
        <p>Lymni.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHOIR PICNIC.—The above choir heli their annual picnic on Wednesday, Castleton being the resort chosen this year. The party left Lymm at 11.11 a.m., breaking their journey at Manchester, where they bad dinner at Lockhart's Restaurant. Journeying to Castleton, the delightful scenery was much admired, the party visiting the mines, which were a special treat to the young folks. After inspecting more Leautiful spots, the choir returned to Manchester, where they again had refreshments, arriving at Lymm al 11.40 p.m. The arrangements were well taken in hand by Mr. T, M. Eastham.</p>
        <p>PLUCKY RESCUE.—On Sunday afternoon last Charlie Littler, a boy about eight years of age, was playing on the canal bank near his home when he fell in. His little companion tried at first to get him 03t, but find- ing that the boy was going further and further from the bank, they ran to the house of Mr. Sam Ogden, of Lymm Bridge, who on ascertaining thg facts immedi- ately ran to the place and unhesitatingly, without divest- ine himself of coat or boots, jumped into the canll, and fortunately was able to save the boy in the nick of time. The boy is now none the worse for his immersion, but a few seconds later he undoubtedly would have been droxned.</p>
        <p>UNaED TRIP To—BLAcKPooL.—The trip of the season WAS run on Rushbearing Monday, in connection with the Lymm Congregational Church and Primitive Methodist Sunday schools, when about 400 scholars and friends left Lymm Station at 7.15 (an hour after the advertised time), Blackpool being reached at 9.40. The scholars at once dispersed to enjoy themselves as inclination dictated. The day was beautiful and warm and the trip was a great success. A good number of friends took advantage of the short date tickets. Thanks are due to the Secretary (Mr. W. T. Moss), for the excellent arrangements. Lymm wait reached at 11.20 p.m. when everyone seemed to have had a pleasant day. THE LATE MRS. THOMAS RIDGWAY.—The funeral of the late Mrs. Thomas Ridgway, of Victoria Park, Manchester, took place on Thursday at the Baptist Chapel, Higher-lane, Lymm. The cortege arrived at the Chapel about 3.30 p.m., the service being conducted by Dr. Marshall, Principal of Manchester Baptisl College. The hymns, "0 God our help in ages Past" and "Rock of Ages" were sung, the service being beautiful and touching. The coffin was then borne to the grave which had been beautifully lined with flowers by Mr. W. Boom!, of Lymm. The chief mourners included :—Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Ridgway, Wildersmoore Lymm, and Mr. T. Ridgway, jun. Floral tributes were sent by Mr. and Mrs. T. Ridgway, Lymm ; "Winifred and Tom," Clithorpe ; Mr. and Mrs. Burrows, Atherton; Dr. and Mrs. W. 0. Steinthal, Wallington ; IL A. and H. W. Autram, Ramsgate ; Mr. and Mrs. F. Seacombe ' Burrows, Preston • Mr. Joseph R. Burrows, Mrs. W. Fitzjohn, Bath : Mr. J. T. Daiutrees and daughter, Croydon. Mrs. H. Gaskell, Woolton ; Dr. and Mrs. F. S. Arnold, Wallingford ; Mr. and Mrs. G. R. Chipps, Dr. and Mrs, Marshall, Manchester; Mrs. Golding, Mr. and Mrs. R. Maxwell, Dawbarn, Fakenham. The funeral arrange- ments were carried out by Messrs. Satterfield and Bye, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>TERRITORIALS IN CAMP.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIALS IN CAMP.</p>
        <p>The Battle of Caerwys.</p>
        <p>The brilliant sunshine of the past week has enabled the manoeuvres of the local Territorials at Caerwys, North Wales, to be carried out with complete success, and notwithstanding the great heat the men have benefited considerably by their sojourn under canvas and will return looking bronzed and healthy. Last Friday was one of the most important days at the camp, for it witnessed realistic manceuvres on a grand scale. Overnight the enemy, in strong force, had pushed its 'way forward until the hills overlooking Caerwys Station had been occupied. General Bethune marshalled the men in posit,on, and it was shortly after eleven o'clock when the battle commenced in the heights towards the south of Caerwys, the South Lancashires and the Liverpool Scottish being attacked in consider- able force. During the progress of the fight not only were the flee' signals and the heliographers continually at work, but the general was kept in touch with the various brigades by means of the field telephone. Hundreds and hundreds swarmed up the bill; and a perfect battle picture was presented. In addition, the looming of the big guns which covered their advance, the appearance now and again of the stretcher bearers conveying men to the field hospital owing to their having been overcome by the sun, gave a touch of grim reality to the scene. 'fho operations were followed with interest by the French General Langlois and two of his military colleagues, es well as representatives of leading French newspapers. During the week-eni the camp was reinforced to a Considerable extent by the arrival of those citizen soldiers whose conditions of employment allow them to spend but a week under canvas. A few minutes previous t 3 the arrival of the Liver- pools, however, the North Lancashiros, under Colonel Murray, made their appearance, and took the main road towards Denbigh with the object of carrying out a great turning movement and getting round to the rear of the enemy. This proved too much for the attackers to withstand, and the onslaught of the Liverpools in the centre having proved quite successful in the way of dislodging tho enemy, orders were again sent to Colonel Verschoyle to press home his attack with a view to a complete rout. Within about half an hour from the time of the arrival of the Liverpools and the North Lancashires on the scene the battle was over, and " Ceasefire " was sounded at General Bethune's orders, the Commander-in-Chief, being apparently highly pleased at the good work and the intelligence with which his men had carried out their arduous and trying duties. Saturday was a general holiday, and the majority of the Territorials went to Mold to the military carnival. General Bethune and most of the staff acted as judges and stewards, and the general's wife distributed the prizes. Captain Crosfield won the: Officers' jumping competition, the prize being a silver bowl given by Mr. Horace B. Davey, of Caerwys. Some of the men arranged for their relatives to meet them at Mold and afterwards took them to the sports. The proceeds were in aid of the local cbarit The remainder of the men either paid visits to the neighbouring towns such as Rhyl, Denbigh, Corwen, or took a flying trip home. On Sunday the Division was honoured by a visit from Bishop Taylor-Smith, chaplain to the Territorlal Forces, who made a special journey to Caerwys in order to be able to take part in the usual service. The men marched in their respective companies to the base of Mount Aberwheeler, and arranged themselves on the sides of the hill. The situation was a good one for the purpose, the grassy slopes forming a splendid auditorium. The Bishop took for his text St. James iv., 14: "What is your life? It is even vapour. It appears for a little time and then vanishes away." He said no one could answer the question "What is life ?" and it behoved everyone to make the most of it, for no one knew when the angel of death would call them.—A gloom has been cast over the camp by a death in the hospital, although the dead man was not a Territorial. A labourer named Joseph Cave, of Plas Cerrig Farm, was crushed between two waggons while walking through the camp, and received terrible injuries. He was conveyed to the ' camp hospital, and on Saturday an Operation was per- formed, but he died during the night. On Monday the camp was visited by Lieutenant General Sir Charles James Burnett, commander-in-chief of the Western command, who in company with the (Akers, adjutants, and quartermaster, inspected the camp. The day was very warm, ard the men were allowed to leave their coats in camp. Even some of the officers took advantage of this privilege, ,and it was a curious sight to see whole companies marching in their shirt sleeves. The object of the manoeuvres was the teaching of reconnaisance. It was again'the turn of the Sonth Lancashire Brigade to cover itself with glory by completely dislodging and routing the enemy, which was advancing upon the camp in renewed force. it was about five o'clock in the evening before the final attack was delivered, and the South Lancashires retraced their steps to the camp tired but happy with the consciousness of victory. Thanks to Colonel Fraser's ability, the water supply problem has been solved by the tapping of a new source, yielding 12,000 exti a gallons daily. The dust nuisance is described as appalling, but the health of the men is excellent, only three being in hospital. On Tuesday fighting went on without ceasing, but owing to General Burnett being in camp each brigade was working on its own, and operations were conducted exceedingly far afield. On Wednesday afternoon, the South Lancashires including the Liverpool ScOttish, were out in the direc- tion of Newmarket, engaged in skirmishing practice. The rattle and roar of the musketry, as the men, in skirmishing order, worked their way back to camp, was at times deafening, but the success of the afternoon's operations was marred as the result of one of the Scottish being shot in the face at rather close range by la blank c,artride. The injury is not considered to be serious, but, strange to say, this is the second of the Scottish to encounter the Same kind of mishap, The other brigades in camp were at the disposal of their several commanders. _ .</p>
        <p>The heat is tropical, but each day the men parade in shirt sleeves. They are enjoying the work immensely, but complain that at night, when the day's labour is over, they have only the isolated condition of Caerwys ; nothing to amuse themselves with. On that account the camp at night is a great centre of attraction, and ing-songs are general. The supreme effort of the enemy to break through the defence of the West Lancashire Division and seize Liverpool began on Thursday morning, and as the battle was to be fought to a finish it was believed that it would continue for 48 hours, so that the infantry were likely to pass the night in their place in the firing line- prepared for all emergencies. The Liverpool Brigade had to leave camp early in the morning and march direct to Newmarket, supported by a heavy artillery section. They were followed not long afterwards by the South Lancashires, which include the Liverpool Scottish. The North Lance- shires also moved out in full force, and the roads soon presented an extraordinarily busy appearance, guns and transport waggons giving the entire place an aspect of war in real earnest. .</p>
        <p>The operations were most realistic, and the guns shook the little village to its foundations. The honours of the day appeared to rest with the Liverpools who succeeded in forcing the Laucashires to retire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>MARKET PRICES.</head>
        <p>MARKET PRICES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON, WEDNESDAY, FRESH EGGS, 9 and 10. SALMON, Is 6d to FRESH BUTTER, is 2d and WHITING, 6d. 3s 3d. * Con, 6d. • CHES. RABBITS, 104 to Is. TURBUT, Is. 4d. CHEESE, 7d and 84. PLAICE, Gd to 84. POTATOES, 3s to 4s Gd a load. EELS, 10d. FOWLS (couple), 9s to 4s 6d. SOLES, 2s. DUCKS (couple), Gs to 6s 6d. LEMON SOLES, 104. REEF, 6d to 10d. per lb. LOBSTERS, is 44. MUTTON, 5d to 9tid lb. PORK, 74 to 90. LAMB, 6d to 10d. . VEAL,7d to 10d. WHEAT.-68 to 6s 3d per 701bs. OATS.-3s 14 to 3s 3Ad per 451bs. WHEAT STRAW.-35; per ton. OAT STRAW.-34S 6d per ton. CLOVER HAY.—£3 17s to £4 per ton. MEADOW HAY.—£3 15s Gd per ton. No quantities of grain offered. BUrTER.</p>
        <p>CORK, THURSDAY. - Average market; steady demand; prices unchanged. Firsts 88s, seconds 84;_ thirds 82s. Mild cured : Superfine 935, fine 88; choicest boxes 925, choice boxes 82s. Fresh butter 923:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>HAY AND STRAW.</head>
        <p>HAY AND STRAW.</p>
        <p>LONDON, THURSDAY.—Best clover was sold at 80s to 90s atbd inferior 68s to 755, specially-picked hay 85s, good 75s to 80s, inferior 603 to 68, mixture and sanfoin 75s to 82, and straw 28s to 31s pdr load.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>CHEESE.</head>
        <p>CHEESE.</p>
        <p>MARKET DRAYTON, THURSDAY.—There was a pitch of fifty-one tons, and buyers attended in large numbers. Trade ruled brisk and a capital clearance was effected. Finest lots realised 55s to 60s /per cwt., and medium 50s to 555.</p>
